hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3601","3608 - Muammal ibn Hisham, and al-Sari ibn Asim narrated to us, they said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Nadrah, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed in Makkah thirteen, praying two rakahs, and saying: We are travelers, so complete. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this act except from Imran ibn Husayn, and we do not know for it any chain from Imran other than this chain."
"3602","3609 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Harami ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Mihran narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is not one of you able to do every day like Uhud? He said: And who is able to it? He said: All of you are able to it. They said: And what is that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Glorified is Allah, the Magnificent, is greater than Uhud, and there is no god but Allah is greater than Uhud, and praise be to Allah is greater than Uhud. And this speech, we do not know it narrated except from Imran ibn Husayn from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we do not know anyone narrating it from Imran except al-Hasan, and we do not know anyone narrating it from al-Hasan except two men, one of them Ubayd ibn Mihran, and the other Muhammad ibn Juhadah."
"3603","3610 - As for the hadith of Muhammad ibn Juhadah, Abu Ghassan al-Judhuyi narrated to us, from Rawh ibn Hatim, he said: Amr ibn Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah."
"3604","3611 - Amr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears upon a binding false oath, let him take his seat of the Fire. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Imran ibn Husayn. And similar to its meaning has been narrated from more than one of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And it was narrated from Imran from other channels, so we sufficed with this channel of them."
"3605","3612 - Matar ibn Muhammad al-Sukkari narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Mumin ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hisham, meaning Ibn Hassan, narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Imran, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat of the Fire. And this hadith, we only heard it from Matar, from Abd al-Mumin. And we did not hear anyone narrating from this Abd al-Mumin other than him. And it is not narrated from Imran from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this channel."
"3606","3613 - Muhammad ibn Mirdas narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Isa Abu Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The servant does not cease in prayer as long as he awaits the prayer. And this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this speech from various channels with different isnads and different speech. And we do not know that it is narrated from Imran ibn Husayn except from this channel, and we do not know anyone narrating it from Yunus except Abu Khalaf."
"3607","3614 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Mutamir narrated to us, he said: Salm ibn Abi al-Dhayyal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Imran ibn Husayn, and al-Hakam al-Ghifari, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no obedience in disobedience to Allah. And this speech, we already brought it out from Imran from another good channel, and we only repeated it here due to the place of Salm ibn Abi al-Dhayyal, because Salm only narrated five or six hadiths with isnad, so we wanted to bring it out from Salm due to the rarity of the hadith of Salm."
"3608","Musnad of Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him. Hadith of Abu Bakrah. 3615 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, and Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Time has turned as its state the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. The year is twelve months, of them four are sacred, three consecutive: Dhu al-Qadah, and Dhu al-Hijjah, and al-Muharram, and Rajab of Mudar between Jumada and Shaban. And this speech has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from various channels, and we do not know for this speech any channel narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better than this channel from Abu Bakrah. And more than one narrated it, saying: from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Abu Bakrah, and not mentioning his son."
"3609","3616 - Salamah ibn Shabib, and al-Husayn ibn Mahdi, and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, and the wording is Salamah's, they said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while delivering a sermon in the Farewell Hajj: Do not return after me as disbelievers, striking the necks of one another. And whoever prays the morning, then he is in the protection of Allah; whoever violates Allah, Allah will cast him into the Fire upon his face. Let the witness convey to the absent, for perhaps it will be conveyed to peoples who are more retentive than whoever heard it. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except from this channel, and we do not know that anyone narrated it other than Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar. And similar to this speech has been narrated from nobler chains than Abu Bakrah narrated."
"3610","3617 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, and from another man who is superior in himself to Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed the people at Mina, so he said: Do you know which day? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best, until we thought he would name it by other than its name. He said: Is it not the day of sacrifice? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then which land is this? We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: So he fell silent until we thought he would name it by other than its name. He said: Is it not the town? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then your bloods and your wealths and your honors are forbidden upon you, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Shall I not convey? We said: Yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness. He said: Let the witness of you convey to the absent, for perhaps one conveyed to conveys it to one more comprehending than him. Do not return after me as disbelievers, striking the necks of one another. And when it was the day Ibn al-Hadrami was burned, when Jariyah ibn Qudamah burned him, he said: They overlooked Abu Bakrah while he was in a garden of his, so they said: This is Abu Bakrah. Abd al-Rahman said: So my mother narrated to me from Abu Bakrah that he said: Had they entered upon me, I would not have brandished to them a reed. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrating it with this wording except Qurrah, from Muhammad."
"3611","3618 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr. 3619 - And Ziyad ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The judge does not judge between two while he is angry."
"3612","3620 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you think, if Juhaynah, and Muzaynah, and Aslam, and Ghifar are better than Banu Tamim, and than Banu Asad, and than Banu Abd Allah ibn Ghatafan, and than Banu Amir ibn Sasah. So a man said: They have failed and lost. He said: Then they are better than Banu Tamim, and than Banu Asad, and than Banu Abd Allah ibn Ghatafan, and than Banu Amir ibn Sasah. And this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other channels, and this is a good isnad of what is narrated in the like of this."
"3613","3621 - Muhammad ibn Bashshar, and Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: al-Muhajir Abu Makhlad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he gave concession to the traveler three days and their nights, and to the resident a day and a night, when he purifies and wears his two socks. And Abu Bakrah, when he broke wudu, would perform wudu and remove his two socks. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except from this channel, and its isnad is good."
"3614","3622 - Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that Jibril, upon him peace, said: Recite the Quran upon one letter, so he asked him for more. So he said: Upon two letters, so he asked him for more, until it reached seven letters, each healing, sufficient, like your saying: come and approach."
"3615","3623 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which people are best? He said: Whoever’s life is long and his action good. It was said: Then which people are worst? He said: Whoever’s life is long and his action evil. And this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other channels, and this is among the best isnads narrated in that, Allah willing."
"3616","3624 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us. Amr said: And al-Mutamir narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father."
"3617","3625 - And Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, all of them raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Two months of festival do not decrease: Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijjah. And this speech, we do not know anyone narrating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with better isnad than Abu Bakrah, although I do not know anyone narrating it with this wording except Abu Bakrah. And similar to its speech has been narrated from other than Abu Bakrah without its wording. And a group narrated it from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, and the highest narrating it are Khalid and Ishaq, so we sufficed with them apart from others."
"3618","3626 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Ubayd ibn Jannad narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go in the morning learned, or learning, or listening, or loving, and do not be the fifth, lest you perish. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from any of the channels except from this channel from Abu Bakrah. And Ata ibn Muslim, there is no harm in him, and he was not corroborated upon it."
"3619","3627 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man praised a man before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: You have cut his back. And this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other channels, and this channel is the best channel narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that. And we do not know for Abu Bakrah any chain other than this chain."
"3620","3628 - Abd Allah ibn Muawiyah al-Jumahi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two parents of the Dajjal will remain thirty years, no child born to them. Then a boy will be born to them, one-eyed, most harmful thing and least beneficial. His two eyes sleep and his heart does not sleep. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described his two parents: His father is a tall man, lean of flesh, as if his nose is a beak, and his mother is broad-hipped, long of breasts. Abu Bakrah said: So we heard of a newborn among the Jews, so I and al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam went until we entered upon his two parents, and there was the description of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we said: Do you have a child? So they said: We remained thirty years, no child born to us. Then a boy was born to us, one-eyed, most harmful thing and least beneficial; his two eyes sleep and his heart does not sleep. He said: So we exited from them, and there he was, lying in a blanket in the sun, and he had a murmur. So I uncovered his head. He said: What did you two say? We said: Did you hear what we said? He said: Yes; my two eyes sleep and my heart does not sleep. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrating it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than Abu Bakrah, and we do not know for it any isnad other than this isnad. And we do not know anyone narrating with it except Hammad ibn Salamah alone."
"3621","3629 - Muammal ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3622","3630 - And Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: al-Jurayri informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) they said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the major sins? He said: Associating with Allah, and disobedience to parents. And he was reclining, so he sat. Indeed, and false speech. He said in the hadith of Abd al-Wahhab: Or false testimony, Abu Masud doubted. He said: So he kept saying it until we said: Would that he be silent. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except from the hadith of al-Jurayri. And more than one narrated it from al-Jurayri, so we sufficed with the hadith of Ismail ibn Ibrahim apart from others."
"3623","3631 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Anbari, and Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, they said: al-Hasan ibn Buwayh narrated to us, and the wording is Amr's, he said: Bahr ibn Marrar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, he said: And another than him said: from a client of Abu Bakrah, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray in his two sandals. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except from this channel with this isnad, though it is narrated from other than Abu Bakrah from various chains."
"3624","3632 - Amr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Bahr ibn Marrar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah narrated to us, from his grandfather Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in some of his Umrahs, and I went out with him; he did not cut the talbiyah until he touched the Stone. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrating it from Abu Bakrah except from this channel, and we do not know anyone corroborating Amr ibn Malik upon this hadith from Abu Bakrah, nor from Bahr ibn Marrar. And Bahr ibn Marrar is well-known Basran."
"3625","3633 - Muammal ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold for gold except like for like, nor silver for silver except like for like, nor wheat for wheat, nor barley for barley, nor dates for dates except like for like."
"3626","Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Shabbuwayh narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it except Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, and we do not know for Abu Bakrah any route other than this route which we have mentioned."
"3627","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Faddalah narrated to me, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that he saw people praying Duha and said: This is a prayer which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never prayed, nor did most of his Companions. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except Abu Bakrah, and we do not know of anyone who narrates this hadith from Shubah except Muadh ibn Muadh alone."
"3628","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, he said: Bahr ibn Mirar narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was walking, he came upon two graves being punished, and he said: These two graves are being punished. Then he called for a palm stalk, and a stalk was brought, and he split it into two halves and placed one in this grave and one in this grave, then said: Perhaps it will be lightened for them as long as they remain moist. Then he said: They are being punished for what is not major: backbiting and urine. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from Abu Bakrah except through this route, and similar words and this act have been narrated from other than Abu Bakrah, so we mentioned each hadith with its wording in its place."
"3629","Salamah ibn Shabib and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: Abdur Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When two Muslims face one another with their swords, and one of them kills his companion, then the killer and the killed are both in the Fire. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we know the killer, but what of the killed? He said: He intended to kill his companion."
"3630","Talut ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it with this wording except Abu Bakrah, and it has routes from Abu Bakrah."
"3631","Yusuf ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdul Ala narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah. And Salamah and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: Abdur Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fragrance of Paradise is found from a distance of a hundred years journey, and no servant who kills a covenanted soul shall have Paradise made unlawful for him to even find its fragrance. Abu Bakrah said: May Allah deafen my ears if I did not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say this. And these words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through other routes, and also narrated from Abu Bakrah through other routes, and others narrated it from al-Hasan, and the hadith of Qatadah is the strangest of them, for we do not know of anyone who narrated this hadith from Abdul Ala except Yusuf ibn Hammad, and he was trustworthy."
"3632","Talut ibn Abbad narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a Muslim brandishes a weapon against his brother, the angels of Allah continue to curse him until he withdraws it from him."
"3633","And with his chain that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When two Muslims face one another with their swords, then the killer and the killed are in the Fire. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Qatadah except through the route of Suwayd and Mamar. And the first one, we do not know of anyone who narrated it except Suwayd, and it is: When a Muslim brandishes a weapon against his brother."
"3634","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Let none of you say: I fasted Ramadan and stood it all in prayer. He said: I do not know whether he disliked self-praise or said: There may be drowsiness and sleep. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrated it, Ibn Abi Adi being followed in it; rather it is said that Said heard it from al-Muhallab ibn Abi Habibah. I heard Amr ibn Ali say: I said to Yahya: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, this hadith, and Yahya said: This is not from the hadith of Qatadah; rather al-Muhallab ibn Abi Habibah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah. Abu Bakr said: And I saw one of our companions narrating it from Abu Bahr al-Bakrawi, from Said, from a man, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah. And Hammam narrated it from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah."
"3635","I heard Amr ibn Ali saying: Affan narrated it to us, Hafs ibn Amr al-Rabbali narrated to us, he said: Abu Bahr al-Bakrawi Abdur Rahman ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from a man, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording. End of the thirtieth juz, and much praise be to Allah."
"3636","The remainder of the hadith of Abu Bakrah. Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali and Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Muhallab ibn Abi Habibah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Let none of you say: I fasted all of Ramadan nor stood all of it in prayer; Allah knows best, he disliked self-praise, or there must be heedlessness or sleep."
"3637","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Imran narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast upon seeing it and break fast upon seeing it, and if it is obscured from you then complete the number. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The month is thus and thus and thus. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except through this route, and none narrated it from Qatadah except Imran al-Qattan."
"3638","Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man from the people of Persia came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: My Lord has killed your lord, meaning Khosrau. He said: And it was said to him, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after that: He has appointed his daughter as successor. He said: No people shall prosper who are ruled by a woman."
"3639","al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Kathir Abu Sahl, trustworthy and reliable, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording. Abu Bakr said: And these words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through other routes, and we do not know of anyone who narrated it except Abu Bakrah through this route."
"3640","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Allah protected me through something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No people shall prosper who entrust their affair to a woman. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith, Abu Bakrah narrated it, and a group narrated it from Abu Bakrah, and this chain is the best chain narrated in this matter from the hadith of Humayd al-Tawil."
"3641","Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa informed us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording."
"3642","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Muadh narrated to us, from al-Ashath, from Ziyad al-Alam, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, that he bowed before reaching the row, and when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed, he said: May Allah increase you in eagerness, but do not repeat. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah, and Ziyad, we do not know of anyone who narrated from him except Ashath and Hammad ibn Salamah and Ibn Abi Arubah."
"3643","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father."
"3644","And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: al-Ashath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw as if a scale was let down from the sky, and I was weighed against Abu Bakr, then Abu Bakr was weighed against Umar, and Abu Bakr outweighed Umar, then he was weighed against Uthman, and Umar outweighed Uthman, then the scale was lifted. So the Messenger of Allah took it as the caliphate of prophethood, then Allah gives dominion to whom He wills. And the wording of this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Abu Bakrah and Safinah and others narrated it. And Abu Bakrah has another route; this chain is better than the other chain narrated from Abu Bakrah, which is what Ashath narrated, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah."
"3645","Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3646","And Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Israil Abu Musa narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah."
"3647","And Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu Faddalah, and he is Mubarak ibn Faddalah, from al-Hasan, he said: Abu Bakrah narrated to me, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning al-Hasan ibn Ali: This son of mine is a leader, and Allah shall reconcile through him between two parties of Muslims. And when the affair of Muawiyah occurred, not a cupping-glass of blood was shed. And this hadith is narrated from Jabir and from Abu Bakrah, and the hadith of Abu Bakrah is more famous and better in chain, and the hadith of Jabir is stranger, so we mentioned it from Abu Bakrah. And the hadith of Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, we do not know of anyone who narrated it from Ali except Hammad ibn Zayd. And the hadith of Israil Abu Musa, we do not know of anyone who narrated it except Ibn Uyaynah from him."
"3648","And Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Faddalah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: Abu Bakrah narrated to me, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to lead us in prayer, and al-Hasan ibn Ali would come while small; whenever the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated, he would leap onto his neck and back, and the Prophet would raise his head gently until he set him down. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you do with this boy something we have not seen you do. He said: He is my sweet basil in this world; this son of mine is a leader; perhaps Allah shall reconcile through him between two parties of Muslims. And this hadith has been narrated from Abu Said and from Abu Bakrah, and there is no harm in the hadith of Mubarak ibn Faddalah; many people of knowledge narrated from him."
"3649","Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Ashath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the fear prayer: he prayed two rakahs with these and two rakahs with those, so it was four for the Prophet and two plus two for them. And these words are narrated from Jabir and from Abu Bakrah, and the hadith of Abu Bakrah is better in chain, so we mentioned it from Abu Bakrah due to the goodness of its chain, except that Jabir adds words in it."
"3650","Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Abu Hurrah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led his companions in the fear prayer; he arranged them in two rows, one row facing the enemy, and led them in two rakahs, then gave salam, then these went to the station of those, and those came, and the Messenger of Allah led them in two rakahs, then gave salam. So it was four rakahs for the Messenger of Allah and two plus two for them. And this hadith, we mentioned it because it adds to Ashath and explains what Ashath narrated concisely; and because it is a rare hadith from al-Hasan; none narrated what Ashath and Abu Hurrah narrated, I do not know of others besides them, so I gathered them in one place for that. And the name of Abu Hurrah is Wasil ibn Abdur Rahman, and he is sound in hadith, Basran."
"3651","Zayd ibn Akhzam narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rakahs during the solar eclipse."
"3652","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Subayh narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he bowed before reaching the row, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: May Allah increase you in eagerness, but do not repeat."
"3653","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the sun eclipsed, so he rose to the mosque dragging his cloak in haste, and the people stood with him, and he prayed two rakahs as you pray. Then when he sat, he addressed us and said: The sun and the moon are two signs of Allah by which He frightens His servants; they are not eclipsed for the death of anyone nor for his life. So when you see the eclipse of either of them, pray and supplicate until what afflicts you is removed."
"3654","Abu Zayd al-Ubulli narrated to us, he said: al-Hurr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Faddalah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no retaliation except with the sword. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who transmitted it with a better chain than this chain from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we do not know of anyone who said it from Abu Bakrah except al-Hurr ibn Malik, and there was no harm in him, but I think he erred in this hadith, for people narrate it from al-Hasan as mursal."
"3655","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Malik al-Saigh narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever removes something from its boundary and thereby hits a person is liable. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrated this from the Messenger of Allah except through this route with this chain, and people narrate it from al-Hasan as mursal, and Hammad ibn Malik al-Saigh is not strong among the companions of al-Hasan."
"3656","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, he said: A shaykh from Quraysh narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon his mule, when a woman came to him and said: She has committed adultery, so stone her, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Cover yourself with the cover of Allah. Then she came to him another day while he was upon his mule and said: O Messenger of Allah, stone her. He said: Cover yourself with the cover of Allah. Then she came on the third day while he was upon his mule, and she took the bridle and said: O Messenger of Allah, I implore you by Allah but that you stone her. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go until you give birth. So she gave birth to a boy, and she brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah said to her: Go and purify yourself from blood. So she went and came and said: She has become pure from blood. So he sent to some women and commanded them to examine her, then he dug a pit for her, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a pebble like a chickpea, then threw it, and the people threw. He said: And this hadith with this wording, we do not preserve it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this chain, and we do not know for it any route other than this route, and Zakariyya ibn Sulaym is Basran, and we do not know of anyone who named this shaykh."
"3657","Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father. Abu Bakr said: I only said: from Ibn Abi Bakrah, because Abu Kurayb said: from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakrah, and I do not know of Abu Bakrah having a son called Abdullah, so I rendered it as from Ibn Abi Bakrah."
"3658","And Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned a land called al-Basrah, near it or beside it a place called Dijlah, which they shall settle and their number shall increase there. Then the sons of Qantura shall come to them, and the people shall split into three parties: a party that follows them, a party that turns their backs to them and they perish, and a third party that fights them, and Allah shall grant victory to their remainder. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah alone, and Said ibn Jumhan is a famous Basran."
"3659","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: Abdul Malik ibn Abi Naamah al-Hanafi narrated to us, from Yusuf ibn Abi Maryam al-Hanafi, he said: While I was sitting with Abu Bakrah, a man came and greeted him and said: Do you not know me? Abu Bakrah said to him: Who are you? He said: Do you know a man who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him that he had seen the barrier? Abu Bakrah said to him: And are you he? He said: Yes. He said: Sit and narrate to us. He said: I set out until I reached a land whose people had nothing but iron which they worked. I entered a house and lay on my back, placing my feet against its wall. When the sun was about to set, I heard a sound the like of which I never heard, and I was frightened and sat up. The master of the house said to me: Do not be alarmed, for this shall not harm you; this is the sound of people departing at this hour from this barrier. He said: Would it please you to see it? I said: Yes. He said: I went to it in the morning, and behold, bricks of iron, each like a rock, and as if striped garments, and its nails like tree trunks. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Describe it to me. I said: As if striped garments. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to look at a man who has come to the barrier, let him look at this one. Abu Bakrah said: He spoke the truth. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah, and we do not know for it any route from Abu Bakrah other than this route."
"3660","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Awn ibn Kahmas narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Nawwar, freedman of Quraysh, he said: Abdullah ibn Abdur Rahman ibn Bakrah narrated to us, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Abu Salamah while he was dying, and when his sight became fixed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hand to him and closed his eyes. When he closed them, the people of the house cried out, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) silenced them, then said: When the soul departs, the sight follows it, and the angels attend the deceased and say amen to what the people of the house say. Then he said, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Allah, raise the rank of Abu Salamah among the guided, and leave him a successor among his offspring among those who remain, and forgive us and him on the Day of Judgment. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it with this wording except Abu Bakrah, and we do not know for it any route from Abu Bakrah except this route."
"3661","Amr ibn Ali and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, the wording being from one of them, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Mihran al-Kindi, he said: Sad ibn Aws narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Kusayb al-Adawi, he said: Ibn Amir went out on a Friday and ascended the pulpit wearing thin garments. Abu Bilal said: Look at your commander wearing the garments of the wicked. Abu Bakrah, while beneath the pulpit, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever humiliates the authority of Allah on earth, Allah shall humiliate him. And this hadith, similar words have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes, and we do not know it narrated with this wording from the Messenger of Allah except from Abu Bakrah, and Humayd ibn Mihran and Said ibn Aws and Ziyad ibn Kusayb are all Basrans."
"3662","Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Sulayman al-Asari, he said: Uqbah ibn Suhban narrated to me, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: People shall be carried over the Sirat on the Day of Resurrection, and its two sides shall buffet them as moths buffet in the Fire. Then Allah by His mercy shall save whom He wills. Then permission shall be given to the angels and the prophets and the martyrs to intercede, and they shall intercede and bring out, and intercede and bring out, and intercede and bring out whoever had in his heart the weight of an atom of faith. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except Abu Bakrah, and the chain of this hadith are all Basrans."
"3663","Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Jahdari narrated to us, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: Yes, My verses had come to you, but you denied them and were arrogant. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah with this chain, and none narrated it except Abdullah ibn Hafs al-Artabaani."
"3664","al-Abbas ibn Abi Talib and Ahmad ibn Mansur and Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahim narrated to us, they said: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Hafs al-Artabaani narrated to us, from Asim al-Jahdari, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite: Reclining on green cushions and rich carpets. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah, and we do not know for it any route from Abu Bakrah except this route, and Abdullah ibn Hafs is Basran, there is no harm in him."
"3665","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, he said: Abu Bakrah narrated a hadith. I said: Did you hear it? He said: My ears heard it and my heart retained it from Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Whoever claims for other than his father in Islam while knowing he is other than his father, Paradise is forbidden to him. Sad said: And my ears heard it and my heart retained it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except Abu Bakrah and Sad, and Abu Uthman has routes from Sad and Abu Bakrah, and Khalid added in his hadith a letter not in the hadith of Asim nor in the hadith of Dawud, so we mentioned it for that."
"3666","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Uthman al-Shahham narrated to us, from Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah, he said: My father used to say after prayer: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from disbelief and poverty and the punishment of the grave. He said: So I used to say them, and I said to my father: You say them. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say them after every prayer."
"3667","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Uthman, meaning al-Shahham, from Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There shall emerge from my nation peoples young in age who recite the Quran which does not pass their throats. If you meet them, kill them; then if you meet them, put them to sleep, meaning kill them. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from Abu Bakrah except through this route, and these words and the like have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes with different wordings, so we mentioned each hadith with its wording in its place, and in the hadith of Abu Bakrah there is something not in the hadith of others."
"3668","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi informed us, from Uthman, he said: We asked Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah about tribulations, and he said: My father Abu Bakrah narrated to me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that there shall be tribulations, then there shall be a tribulation in which the one walking is better than the one hastening to it, and the one standing is better than the one walking to it, and the one lying down is better than the one sitting in it. So when it descends, whoever has sheep let him join his sheep, and whoever has land let him join his land, and whoever has camels let him join his camels. He said: A man from the people said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make me ransom for what you see, what of one who has neither sheep nor land nor camels? He said: Let him take his sword, then strike it against a rock until it is blunted. O Allah, have I conveyed? He said it thrice. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except through this route, and none narrated it from Muslim ibn Abi Bakrah except Uthman ibn al-Shahham, though more than one narrated from him but did not transmit from him with chains."
"3669","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Uyaynah, meaning Ibn Abdur Rahman, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no sin more deserving that Allah hasten its punishment for its doer in this world along with what He stores for him in the Hereafter than transgression and severing kinship."
"3670","And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a covenanted person without right shall not smell the fragrance of Paradise. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah, and it has routes from Abu Bakrah, and Uyaynah was narrated from by Shubah and others; he is a known Basran."
"3671","Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Uyaynah, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, he said: We went out with him in the funeral of Abdur Rahman ibn Samurah, while Ziyad was walking before the bier, and men from his freedmen and family were walking behind him before the bier, saying: Gently, gently, may Allah bless you. So Abu Bakrah said, while upon his mule, urging them with the whip: Leave it; by the One who honored the face of Abul Qasim, I saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) almost trotting with it. He said: And this hadith, we do not preserve it from Abu Bakrah except through this route with this chain, and Shubah also narrated it from Uyaynah."
"3672","Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Uyaynah narrated to us, from his father, he said: I mentioned Laylat al-Qadr in the presence of Abu Bakrah, and he said: As for me, I seek it only in the last ten after a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Seek it in the ninth remaining, or the seventh remaining, or the fifth remaining, or the third remaining, and the last night. He said: So Abu Bakrah used to pray the twenty of Ramadan as he did not pray in the rest of the year, and when the last ten entered, he exerted himself. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except through the hadith of Uyaynah, from his father, from Abu Bakrah."
"3673","And Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Bakrah narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was gladdened by news he was given and fell prostrate. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except through this route, and we do not know of anyone who narrates it except Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from his father, from Abu Bakrah."
"3674","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Bahr ibn Kunayz Abu al-Fadl narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade money exchange two months before his death. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except through this route with this wording, and Bahr ibn Kunayz is the grandfather of Amr ibn Ali, and he is weak in hadith."
"3675","al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad and Zayd ibn Akhzam narrated to us, they said: Abu Qutaybah al-Rifai narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Minhal al-Bakrawi narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: On the day of Taif I let myself down to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a pulley, and he said: You are Abu Bakrah. And this hadith, we do not preserve it from Abu Bakrah except through this route, and Abu al-Minhal, we do not know of anyone who transmitted from him except Abu Qutaybah, who transmitted two hadiths from him."
"3676","Zayd ibn Akhzam narrated to us, he said: Abu Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Minhal al-Bakrawi narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When Khosrau died, he said: Whom did they appoint after him? He said: His daughter Buran. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No people shall prosper who entrust their affair to a woman. And this hadith has been narrated from Abu Bakrah through routes, and we do not know of anyone who narrated it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Bakrah, and we only mentioned it from Abu al-Minhal because Abu al-Minhal did not narrate other than these two hadiths, so we mentioned them to gather them in one place."
"3677","Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Bakrah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to glorify in his bowing, Glory be to my Lord the Great, thrice, and in his prostration, Glory be to my Lord the Most High, thrice. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from Abu Bakrah except through this route with this chain, and Abdur Rahman ibn Bakkar is known in lineage, sound in hadith."
"3678","Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: My father Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, he said: I heard my father Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Bakrah narrating from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution; he washed his hands thrice, rinsed thrice, sniffed thrice, washed his face thrice, washed his forearms to the elbows, wiped his head bringing his hands from front to back and from back to front, then washed his feet thrice, ran fingers between his toes, and ran fingers through his beard. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except through this route with this chain, and Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz is unobjectionable, and Abdur Rahman is sound in hadith, whose mention we have already made."
"3679","Muhammad ibn Mamar and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Umar ibn al-Hajanna, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: It was said: What prevents you from not having fought on the day of the Camel? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There shall emerge destroyed people who shall not prosper, their leader a woman, their leader in Paradise. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Abu Bakrah through this route, and Umar ibn al-Hajanna, we do not know of anyone who narrated from him except Ata ibn al-Saib, and other than Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Abbas narrated from Ata saying: From Bilal ibn Buqtur, from Abu Bakrah. And we do not know of anyone who followed Abd al-Jabbar in his narration, and he is a known man from the people of Kufah, from whom a group narrated."
"3680","Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Uyaynah, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, that he used to make nabidh for himself in a green jar. He said: Abu Barzah came from an absence he was absent, and began with the house of Abu Bakrah, but did not find him at home. He stood by his wife and asked her about Abu Bakrah, and she informed him. Then he saw the jar in which the nabidh was and said: What is in this jar? She said: Nabidh for Abu Bakrah. He said: I wish you had put it in a waterskin. Then he left, so she ordered that nabidh to be placed in a waterskin. Then Abu Bakrah came, and she informed him about Abu Barzah al-Aslami. He said: What is in this waterskin? She said: Abu Barzah ordered us to put your nabidh in it. He said: I shall not drink what is in it; if you put wine in a waterskin it shall not become lawful for me, and if you put honey in a jar it shall not become unlawful for me. We know what we were forbidden; we were forbidden the dubba, the hantam, the naqir and the muzaffat. As for the dubba, we, the people of Thaqif, were at Taif; we would take the gourd and hollow clusters of grapes into it, then bury it until it fermented, then died. As for the naqir, the people of Yamamah would hollow the base of the palm tree, then crush fresh and unripe dates into it, then leave it until it fermented, then died. As for the hantam, they are red jars in which wine was carried to us. And as for the muzaffat, these are the vessels containing pitch. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it explained as Abu Bakrah narrated it except through this route."
"3681","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Said, from Abu Abdullah, freedman of Quraysh, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan, he said: Abu Bakrah entered upon us, and a man stood for him from his seat. So Abu Bakrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Let no man make another stand from his seat then sit in it, nor wipe his hand on the garment of one who does not own it. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it except Abu Bakrah, and we do not know for it any route except this route, and we do not know of anyone who named this man, meaning Abu Abdullah, freedman of Quraysh. We only mentioned it as it is because it is not narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except through this route."
"3682","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Umar al-Bakrawi narrated to us, he said: Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shows off, Allah shall expose him, and whoever seeks repute, Allah shall make him heard of. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from Abu Bakrah except with this chain, and we have already mentioned Bakkar elsewhere, so we dispensed with repeating his mention afterwards."
"3683","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Umar al-Bakrawi narrated to us, he said: Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a conquest came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he prostrated, then began asking the messenger, who had their news: Whom did they appoint? Or whom did they entrust their affair to? He said: A woman. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Men perish when women rule them."
"3684","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Umar al-Bakrawi narrated to us, he said: Bakkar ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah delays all sins as He wills, except transgression and severing kinship, which Allah hastens for its doer before death."
"3685","Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Uyaynah, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever forgets a prayer or sleeps through it, let him pray it when he remembers it. And this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes, and we do not know it narrated from Abu Bakrah except through this route, and we did not hear of anyone who narrates it from Ibn Ulayyah except Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-Ijli."
"3686","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from Uyaynah, from his father, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) almost trotting with it."
"3687","Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn al-Araj, from al-Ashath ibn Thurmalah, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a covenanted soul without its right, Allah shall forbid him Paradise and even smelling its fragrance."
"3688","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Sulayman Kab ibn Shabib al-Asari narrated to us, he said: Uqbah narrated to us, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: People shall be carried on the Day of Resurrection over the Sirat, and its two sides shall buffet them as moths buffet in the Fire. Then permission shall be given to the angels and the prophets and the martyrs and the righteous, and they shall intercede and bring out, and intercede and bring out."
"3689","The musnad of al-Falatan ibn Asim, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of al-Falatan ibn Asim, Kufan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle al-Falatan, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was shown Laylat al-Qadr then made to forget it, and I was shown the false messiah. I saw two men quarreling and I intervened between them, so I was made to forget it. So seek it in the last ten, on the odd nights. As for the false messiah, he is a man with a receding forehead, his left eye effaced, broad of chest, as if Abd al-Uzza ibn Qatan. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route, and we do not know for al-Falatan any route other than this route, though similar words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes with different wordings."
"3690","Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, from his father, from al-Falatan ibn Asim, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when revelation descended upon him. When it descended upon him, he opened his eyes and devoted his hearing and sight to what came to him from Allah. When he finished, he said to the scribe: Write: Those of the believers who sit back and those who strive in the path of Allah with their wealth and selves are not equal; Allah has favored the strivers over those who sit back by a degree. Then Ibn Umm Maktum the blind stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, then excuse us. So Allah revealed while he was standing, and the scribe said: Write: Except those with disability. And this hadith has been narrated with similar words through routes, and we mentioned this from al-Falatan due to the rarity of the hadith of al-Falatan, though it may be narrated with other than this chain with what is better than this chain in another wording."
"3691","Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahim narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his maternal uncle, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting in the gathering when he stared at a man walking in the mosque and said: Abu so-and-so. He said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, and none disputed speech with him but said: O Messenger of Allah. He said to him: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: No. He said: Do you read the Torah? He said: Yes. He said: And the Gospel? He said: Yes. He said: And the Quran? He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if we wished we could read it. Then he adjured him: Do you find me in the Torah and the Gospel? He said: We find one like you and like your appearance and like your emergence. We hoped he would be among us, but when you emerged we feared you are he, then we looked and you are not he. He said: Why is that? He said: With him are seventy thousand of his nation upon whom there is no reckoning nor punishment, while with you there are only a few. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I am indeed he, and they are indeed my nation, and they are indeed more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this chain."
"3692","The musnad of Salamah ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of Salamah ibn Nufayl, Syrian, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Salamah ibn Shabib and Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Artah ibn al-Mundhir informed us, he said: Damrah ibn Habib narrated to me, from Salamah ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), were you brought food from the sky? He said: Yes, I was brought a heated dish. He said: Was there any leftover from you in it? He said: Yes. He said: What was done with it? He said: It was raised. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except through this route, and Artah ibn al-Mundhir and Damrah ibn Habib are two known men from the people of Syria."
"3693","Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Aftas narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to me, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Salamah ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man said: O Messenger of Allah, the horses have been emaciated and weapons laid down, and they claim there is no fighting. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They lie; now the time for fighting has come. There shall remain of my nation a nation standing upon the truth, manifest. And he said, turning his back to Yemen: I find the breath of the Most Merciful from here, and it has been revealed to me that I am withheld, not long to remain, and groups shall follow me, and horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and their people are aided upon them. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it with these wordings except Salamah ibn Nufayl, and this is the best route narrated in that from Salamah, and its men are known men from the people of Syria, famous, except Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Aftas."
"3694","The musnad of Qutbah ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of Qutbah ibn Malik, Kufan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Bishr ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Ilaqah narrated to us, from Qutbah ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that he prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him reciting in the dawn prayer Qaf: And tall palms with arranged clusters."
"3695","al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah, may Allah be pleased with him: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him reciting Qaf, and I heard him reciting: And tall palms with arranged clusters. And he said: Its towering is its height."
"3696","Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Qutbah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting in dawn: And tall palms with arranged clusters. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except Qutbah ibn Malik, and we do not know of anyone who narrates from Qutbah except Ziyad ibn Ilaqah. And Abu al-Mundhir added, from al-Masudi: And its towering is its height, but it is from the speech of Qutbah, which he inserted into the marfu, erring therein."
"3697","Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Misar narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from his uncle Qutbah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking refuge from wayward desires, evil states and diseases. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Qutbah ibn Malik with this chain, and we do not know of anyone who narrated it except Misar, from Ziyad, and we do not know of anyone who narrated it from Misar except Abu Usamah, and it is strange."
"3698","The musnad of Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of Abu Humayd al-Saidi, Madinan, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man from al-Asad for charities called Ibn al-Lutbiyyah. He came and said: This is for you and this was gifted to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: What is with the worker whom we send upon our work, then he comes and says: This was gifted to me and this is for you? Why does he not sit in the house of his father or the house of his mother and see whether a gift comes to him or not? By the One in Whose Hand is Muhammad, none of you brings anything from it secretly but shall bring it on the Day of Resurrection upon his neck, whether a camel bellowing, or a cow lowing, or a sheep bleating. Then he raised his hands until we saw the whiteness of his armpits, then said: O Allah, have I conveyed? Thrice. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it with this wording except Abu Humayd, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Many narrated it from al-Zuhri, and we sufficed with the narration of Ibn Uyaynah from him, unless one adds therein, when it is written due to the addition."
"3699","Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a man called Ibn al-Lutbiyyah for charity. When he came, he accounted with him and said: This is for you and this was gifted to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood upon the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: As for what follows, what is with the worker whom we send? Then he mentioned the hadith. And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it except Abu Usamah with this wording."
"3700","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Qurrah ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) borrowed dates from a Bedouin. The Bedouin came demanding repayment, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have nothing with which to repay you. Then he mentioned the hadith."
"3701","al-Hasan ibn Arafah narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to me, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi."
"3702","And Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata narrated to me, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard him saying, while among ten of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them Abu Qatadah ibn Ribi, saying: I am the most knowledgeable of you of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). They said: You were neither the earliest of us in his companionship nor the most following him. He said: Yes indeed. He said: Then present. He said: When he, upon him be peace, stood to prayer, he stood straight, then said takbir and raised his hands until parallel to his shoulders. When he intended to bow, he raised his hands until parallel to his shoulders, then said: Allah is Most Great, then bowed, then straightened, neither raising his head nor lowering it, and placed his hands upon his knees. Then he said: Allah hears him who praises Him, and raised and stood straight until every bone returned to its place straight. Then he fell prostrate to the ground and said: Allah is Most Great. Then he kept his upper arms away from his sides and opened his toe fingers. Then he folded his left leg and sat upon it, straight, until every bone returned to its place straight. Then he fell to the ground and said: Allah is Most Great. Then he rose, then did in the second rakah likewise, until when he stood from the two prostrations he said takbir and raised his hands until parallel to his shoulders as he did when opening the prayer. Then he did likewise, until when it was the rakah in which the prayer ends, he moved his left leg back and sat upon his side, sitting mutawarrikan, then gave salam."
"3703","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Abbas ibn Sahl, from Abu Humayd, among ten of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Humayd with better than these two chains."
"3704","Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Qutaybah narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qutai narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Musa ibn Abdullah, from Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no harm for a man to look at a woman when he intends to marry her without her knowledge. Abu Bakr said: And this hadith, similar words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes, and we do not know for Abu Humayd any route other than this route. The wording of the hadith of Abu Humayd differs from the rest of the hadiths narrated in that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Musa ibn Abdullah is Musa ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid, famous."
"3705","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Mashmul narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is named with my name, let him not take my kunya. He said: This hadith, its words are narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes, and we do not know for Abu Humayd any route other than this route. And Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah is weak in hadith, though a group narrated from him and transmitted from him."
"3706","Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Every prophet has an inner circle, and my inner circle is this tribe of the Ansar. Were it not for the Hijrah I would be a man of the Ansar. If people take a valley and the Ansar take a valley, I would take the path of the Ansar. The Ansar are the inner garment and people are the outer garment. Whoever takes charge of any matter, let him do good to their good-doers and overlook their wrongdoers. He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Humayd except through this route. These words have been narrated from many groups with different wordings, and we mentioned this hadith from Abu Humayd due to the rarity of the hadith of Abu Humayd."
"3707","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Abdur Rahman ibn Said, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a Muslim to take the staff of his brother except with his goodwill, and that is part of what he, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), stressed of what Allah has forbidden of the wealth of a Muslim to a Muslim. He said: And this hadith, similar words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes with other than this wording, and we do not know for Abu Humayd any route other than this route, and its chain is good."
"3708","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabiah, meaning Ibn Abi Abdur Rahman, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said ibn Suwayd, he said: I heard Abu Humayd and Abu Usayd, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you hear a hadith which your hearts recognize, your hair and skin soften to it, and you see it near to you, then I am the most entitled to it. And when you hear a hadith from which your skins shiver, your hearts or hair change, and you see it far from you, then I am the furthest of you from it. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through a better route than this route."
"3709","Some of our companions narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abdur Rahman, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said ibn Suwayd, from Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be moderate in seeking the world, for each is eased to his decreed share. He said: This hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a better chain than this chain, and we do not know of anyone who narrates, more eminent than Abu Humayd al-Saidi with this chain."
"3710","Nasr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn al-Fayyad narrated to us, the wording being Muhammad ibn Yahya's, they said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to us, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abdur Rahman, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said, he said: I heard Abu Humayd, or Abu Usayd, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you enters the mosque, let him say: O Allah, open for me the doors of Your mercy. And when he exits, let him say: O Allah, I ask You of Your bounty. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with better than this chain. It has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes, so we mentioned this hadith due to the defect of Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, and we mentioned it from Abu Humayd and Abu Usayd, though narrated from others, due to how little they narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3711","And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Abd al-Malik Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Rabiah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Said, from Abu Humayd and Abu Usayd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording."
"3712","Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Tamimi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Amr ibn Yahya, from Abbas ibn Sahl, from Abu Humayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when we were at a place he named, and it was the expedition of Tabuk, we came upon a garden of a woman. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Estimate it. So we estimated it, or the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) estimated it, as ten awsuq. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned, he asked the woman about her garden, how much its fruit reached. She said: It reached ten awsuq, neither more nor less. He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Abu Humayd al-Saidi alone."
"3713","Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahim narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gifts to workers are embezzlement. He said: And this hadith, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated it, shortening it and erring therein; rather it is from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abu Humayd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man for charity."
"3714","The musnad of Rifaa ibn Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of Rifaa ibn Rafi. Ziyad ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman narrated to me, he said: Ubayd ibn Rifaa al-Zuraqi narrated to me, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: On the day of Uhud the polytheists turned back, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand straight and firm until I praise my Lord. So they stood behind him in rows and he said: O Allah, Yours is praise; none withholds what You spread, none spreads what You withhold, none guides whom You misguide, none misguides whom You guide, none gives what You prevent, none prevents what You give, none brings near what You distance, none distances what You bring near. O Allah, spread upon us of Your blessings, mercy, bounty and provision. O Allah, I ask You the lasting bliss on the Day of Resurrection. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the evil of what You gave us and the evil of what You withheld from us. O Allah, make faith beloved to us and adorn it in our hearts, make disbelief, wickedness and disobedience hateful to us, and make us of the rightly guided. O Allah, take us as Muslims and join us with the righteous, neither disgraced nor tempted. O Allah, fight the disbelievers who deny Your messengers. O Allah, place upon them Your punishment and torment. O Allah, fight the disbelieving People of the Book. He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route; Rifaa ibn Rafi alone narrated it from him. We do not know of anyone who narrated it from Ubayd except Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman, and he is a famous man, unobjectionable in hadith; people of knowledge narrated from him."
"3715","Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Rifaa, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar: Gather your people for me. So Umar gathered them at the house of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he entered upon him and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I admit them to you or shall you come out to them? He said: Rather I shall come out to them. He said: So he came to them and said: Is there among you anyone from other than you? They said: Yes, among us are our allies, the sons of our sisters, and our freedmen. He said: Our allies are from us, the sons of our sisters are from us, our freedmen are from us. And you: Do you not hear that my allies among you are the Godfearing? If you are those, then that is it; otherwise, see that people do not come with deeds on the Day of Resurrection while you come with burdens, and I turn away from you. Then he raised his hands and said: O people, Quraysh are trustworthy; whoever seeks them with faults, Allah shall throw him upon his nostrils. He said it thrice. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it with this wording except Rifaa ibn Rafi. This route from him is among the good chains narrated in that. Waki narrated, from Sufyan, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Ismail ibn Ubayd, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet, some of its words. And the hadith of Bishr is fuller than the hadith of Sufyan."
"3716","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from his father, from his paternal uncle Rifaa ibn Rafi, and he was a Badri, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque when a man entered."
"3717","And Hudbah narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from his father, from his paternal uncle, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the mosque with people around him, a man came and faced the qiblah. When he finished his prayer, he came and greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: And upon you; return and pray, for you have not prayed. So he returned and prayed, then came, and we watched his prayer. When he finished his prayer, he came and greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: And upon you; return and pray, for you have not prayed. So he repeated it twice or thrice. The man said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not know what you fault in my prayer, and I did my best. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of one of you is not complete until he perfects ablution as Allah commanded him: washing his hands and face and his arms to the elbows, wiping his head and his feet to the ankles. Then he magnifies Allah and praises Him and recites of the Quran what Allah permits him. Then he says takbir and bows, placing his palms upon his knees until his joints settle and relax. Then he says: Allah hears him who praises Him, until he straightens his back and stands even, every bone taking its place. Then he settles his face, and I heard him say: his forehead, until his joints settle and relax. Then he says takbir and raises his head until he sits even upon his seat and straightens his back. Then he says takbir and prostrates until he settles his face, his joints relax and settle. Then he says takbir and raises. He described thus; if one does not do thus, his prayer is not complete. And the hadith of Rifaa is fuller than the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, and its chain is good."
"3718","Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: Rifaa ibn Yahya al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muadh ibn Rifaa, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: My brother Khallad and I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Badr upon a lean camel of ours. When we were at the Barid place behind al-Rawha, our camel knelt with us. We said: O Allah, Yours is upon us that if You deliver us to Madinah we shall slaughter it. While we were thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed us and said: What is with you two? We informed him it had knelt with us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dismounted and performed ablution, then spat in his ablution water, then ordered us and we opened for him the mouth of the camel, and he poured into the belly of the young camel from his ablution water, then poured upon the head of the camel, then upon its neck, then upon its withers, then upon its hump, then upon its rump, then upon its tail. Then he said: O Allah, carry Rafi and Khallad. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went, and we stood to mount and mounted, and we caught the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Munsaf, our young camel first of the riders. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw us, he laughed. We went until we came to Badr, and when we were near Badr it knelt with us. We said: Praise be to Allah, and we slaughtered it and gave its meat in charity? He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated except from Rifaa ibn Rafi, and we do not preserve for it any route from Rifaa except this route."
"3719","Ahmad narrated to us, Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: Rifaa ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Muadh ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: On the day of Badr people gathered upon Umayyah ibn Khalaf. I approached him and saw a piece of his armor cut from beneath his armpit, so I stabbed him with the sword. And on the day of Badr I was shot with an arrow and my eye was gouged. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat and supplicated for me therein, and nothing of it harmed me. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Rifaa ibn Rafi, and we do not know for it any route from Rifaa except this route."
"3720","Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Mamar ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Habibah, from Ubayd ibn Rifaa, from his father, he said: We used to do it at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); if we did not ejaculate, we did not wash. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it with better than this chain, and we do not know of Mamar ibn Abi Habibah transmitting from Ubayd other than this hadith."
"3721","Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahim narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mundhir Ismail ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Wail ibn Dawud, from Ubayd ibn Rifaa, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which earning is best? He said: A man working with his hand, and every blessed sale. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who transmitted it from al-Masudi except Ismail ibn Umar. Other than Ismail narrated it saying: From Ubayd ibn Rifaa, without saying: from his father."
"3722","Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Rifaa ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man entered the mosque while we were praying with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he raised his head from bowing, the man said: Our Lord, Yours is praise, much, good, blessed praise. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said: Who spoke just now? The man said: I. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw more than thirty angels racing to it, which of them writes it first? Abu Bakr said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from Rifaa ibn Rafi except through this route. And the hadith of Malik, from Nuaym, from Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad, from his father, from his uncle Rifaa ibn Rafi, has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through routes with different chains; we mention each hadith with its wording in its place, Allah willing."
"3723","Abdullah ibn Jafar al-Barmaki narrated to us, he said: Man narrated to us, from Malik, from Nuaym ibn Abdullah, from Ali ibn Yahya al-Zuraqi, from his father, from Rifaa ibn Rafi al-Zuraqi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording."
"3724","Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari and Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, they said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Harun al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi narrated to me, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the Ansar, the descendants of the Ansar, the descendants of their descendants, and their neighbors. He said: And this hadith, we do not know of anyone who narrates it from Rifaa ibn Rafi except through this route with this chain, and its chain is good."
"3725","The musnad of Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him. The hadith of Sad ibn Ubadah. I found in my book, from Ziyad ibn Ayyub, he said: Husayn ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Mukhariq narrated to us, from Tariq, from Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: O Sad, upon you is hearing and obedience in your hardship and ease, your activity and dislike, and preference over you, and not disputing the rulers unless they call you to contrary to what is in the Book of Allah. If they call you to contrary to what is in the Book of Allah, then follow the Book of Allah. He said: And this hadith, its words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through other routes, and we do not know it narrated from Sad ibn Ubadah except through this route. We only mentioned it from Sad ibn Ubadah due to the rarity of his narration from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Husayn ibn Umar is weak in hadith, though people of knowledge narrated from him and bore him despite that."
"3726","Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Said al-Sarraf, from Ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Ansar are a trial; none loves them but a believer, and none hates them but a hypocrite. He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except through this route. Some of its words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through other routes. We only mentioned it from Sad because the narration of Sad from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is little, and therein is a letter none narrated as we preserve except Sad, and it is: The Ansar are a trial."
"3727","Hafs ibn Amr al-Rabbali narrated to us, he said: Abu Bahr al-Bakrawi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Stand over the charity of so-and-so, and see that you do not come on the Day of Resurrection with a young camel carrying it upon your neck, bellowing. He said: Accept your charity from me, so he accepted it from him. He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Sad ibn Ubadah except through this route with this wording, and its chain is good."
"3728","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Amr ibn Shurahbil ibn Said ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, from his grandfather, from Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The master of days is Friday. In it are five traits: In it Adam was created, in it he was sent down, in it Allah took Adam, in it is an hour in which a servant does not ask his Lord for anything therein but He gives him, unless he asks for sin or severing kinship, and in it the Hour shall be established. No close angel, nor heaven, nor earth, nor mountains, nor winds, nor sea, but fears Friday lest the Hour be established therein. He said: And these words, we do not know them narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route with this chain, and its chain is sound."
"3729","Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us. And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ghundar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Isa ibn Faid or Luqayt, from a man, from Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No man learns the Quran then forgets it but meets Allah on the Day of Resurrection maimed, and no worker over ten but shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection shackled; none frees him but deed. He said: And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this route. End of the thirty-first juz and beginning of the thirty-second juz, and much praise be to Allah as He deserves."
"3730","The musnad of Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him. The musnad of Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Muhammad ibn Ayyub ibn Habib informed us, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If faith were suspended at the Pleiades, men from the sons of Persia would reach it. And perhaps he said: From the red sons among the freedmen."
"3731","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: I heard my father saying: I heard Mansur ibn Zadham narrating, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me one day after I had prayed dawn and lain down, striking me with his foot: Shall I guide you to a treasure of the treasures of Paradise? There is no power nor strength save by Allah."
"3732","Tahir ibn Khalid ibn Nizar al-Misri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most usurious of usury is a man overreaching in the honor of his brother."
"3733","Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahman ibn Sad ibn Zurarah, from Muhammad ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah came to the house of Sad and greeted, and Sad returned the greeting quietly. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left, and Sad followed him and said: O Messenger of Allah, what made me do that is I wanted you to increase us of much greeting. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came until he entered upon Sad. He brought him a vessel with water and he washed, then brought him a dyed wrap and he wrapped himself with it. Qays said: So I saw the trace of wars upon his folds. Then he said: O Allah, bless the Ansar, the descendants of the Ansar, and the descendants of the descendants of the Ansar. Then Sad saddled a donkey of his with a blanket and said to his son: Go and return the donkey. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ride upon the front of your donkey. He said: O Messenger of Allah, ride. He said: You are its owner. He said: It is yours, O Messenger of Allah."
"3734","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We used to fast Ashura before the month of Ramadan was revealed. When it was revealed, we were neither commanded nor forbidden, and we do it. We were commanded Zakat al-Fitr before Zakah was revealed. When Zakah was revealed, we were neither commanded nor forbidden, and we do it."
"3735","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu Ammar, from Qays ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us. Then he mentioned similar."
"3736","Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Husayn, from al-Shabi, from Qays ibn Sad ibn Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If I ordered anyone to prostrate to anyone, I would order the woman to prostrate to her husband."
"3737","The musnad of Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him. What Fadalah ibn Ubayd transmitted from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abu Abdur Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani informed me that Abu Ali Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi informed him that he heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man supplicating in his prayer without praising Allah nor blessing the Prophet, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah, upon him be peace, said: He hastened. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said to him and to others: When one of you prays, let him begin with praising Allah and extolling Him, then let him supplicate with what he wills."
"3738","Salamah narrated to us, he said: al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Haywah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani informed me that Amr ibn Malik informed him, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Three, do not ask about them: A man who leaves the community, disobeys his leader and dies disobedient; a slave girl or slave who flees his master and dies; a woman whose husband is absent while he sufficed her worldly matter, then she displays herself after him. And three, do not ask about them: A man who disputes with Allah His cloak, for His cloak is pride and His garment might; a man in doubt of the matter of Allah; and despairing of the mercy of Allah."
"3739","Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani informed me that Abu Ali narrated to him that he heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led people in prayer, men would fall from their standing in prayer due to hunger, and they are the people of the Suffah, until the Bedouins say: These are mad. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished prayer, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned and said: If you knew what is for you with Allah, you would love that you increase in poverty and need. So Fadalah said: And I was with the Messenger of Allah that day."
"3740","Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani narrated to me, from Abu Ali Amr ibn Malik, from Fadalah, he heard him saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was. Then he mentioned similar."
"3741","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Abu Hani al-Khawlani, from Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi, that Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said in the Farewell Hajj: This is a sacred day and a sacred land, so your blood, wealth and honors are sacred to you like this day and this land until the day you meet Him. Even a push by which a Muslim pushes a Muslim intending evil is unlawful. I shall inform you who is the Muslim: one from whose tongue and hand Muslims are safe; the believer is one whom people trust over their wealth and selves; the emigrant is one who abandons sins and wrongs; the struggler is one who strives against himself in obedience to Allah."
"3742","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani informed me, from Amr ibn Malik, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: Every dead is sealed over his deed except one guarding in the path of Allah, for his deed grows for him until the Day of Resurrection, and he is secured from the tribulation of the grave."
"3743","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Hani informed me, from Amr ibn Malik, that he heard Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I am a guarantor, and the guarantor is the surety, for whoever believes in me, embraces Islam and emigrates, a house in the outskirts of Paradise and a house in its highest part. Whoever does that leaves no pursuit of good nor escape from evil; he dies wherever he wills to die."
"3744","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Misri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi al-Sabah, from Hanash, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: Whoever grays a hair in Islam, it shall be light for him on the Day of Resurrection. A man with him then said: Men pluck gray hair. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wills, let him pluck his light."
"3745","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Khalid ibn Abi Imran, from Hanash ibn Abdullah, he said: We were with Fadalah ibn Ubayd at sea, and a necklace with gold dust and pearls or jewels fell to me and my companions. They said: Buy it from us, we shall be lenient with you. I said: Until I ask Fadalah ibn Ubayd. So I came to Fadalah and asked him. So Fadalah said: I bought a necklace on the day of Khaybar with gold dust and jewels for seven or nine dinars. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to me: Separate it; remove the jewels and buy gold with gold, like for like."
"3746","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ubaydullah, he said: al-Jullah Abu Kathir said: Hanash narrated to me, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Khaybar, trading with the Jews an ounce of gold for two or three dinars. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not sell gold except weight for weight."
"3747","Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdur Rahman ibn Jubayr, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) campaigned the expedition of Tabuk. He said: The mounts were exhausted severely. He said: That was complained to him. He said: And he saw them as men. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked from the narrow pass of the people therein and stood upon it while people passed. He said: So he breathed into it, then said: O Allah, make them for Your path, for You carry the strong and the weak, the moist and the dry, on land and sea. He said: So they continued from their outset. He said: We did not enter Madinah but they were tugging at their reins."
"3748","The musnad of Abu Inabah al-Khawlani, may Allah be pleased with him. What Abu Inabah al-Khawlani transmitted from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahman ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mahdi Said ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Abu Inabah al-Khawlani, and he was of the Companions, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to recite in the Friday prayer Surah al-Jumuah and the surah in which the hypocrites are mentioned."
"3749","Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahman ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mahdi Said ibn Sinan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Abu Inabah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he walked, walked vigorously."
"3750","Muhammad ibn Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Inabah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Do not burden my nation. O Allah, whoever burdens my nation, avenge from him, or similar to that."
"3751","The musnad of Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him. What Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani transmitted from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Yahya ibn Khalaf Abu Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever touches his private part, let him perform ablution."
"3752","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Labid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Khallad ibn al-Saib, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gabriel descended to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Allah commands you to order your companions to raise their voices with the talbiyah, for it is of the rites of Hajj."
"3753","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Abu Amrah al-Ansari, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man died, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from Khaybar. They informed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of him, and he said: Pray for your companion, for he embezzled in the path of Allah. His color changed for that. They searched his belongings and found therein beads of the Jews not worth two dirhams."
"3754","Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from a man, from Zayd ibn Khalid, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording."
"3755","And Yahya ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us, from al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Yahya, from Abu Amrah, from Zayd ibn Khalid, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3756","And Hisham ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abdur Rahman, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I burden my nation, I would command them with the siwak at every prayer. Abu Salamah said: I saw Zayd ibn Khalid with his siwak at his ear, like the pen at the ear of the scribe."
"3757","Ahmad ibn Abdah, Hawtharah ibn Muhammad and Khalid ibn Yusuf narrated to us, they said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Abu Hurayrah, Zayd ibn Khalid and Shibl ibn Mabad, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about a slave girl who fornicates before being married. He said: If she fornicates, flog her, then if she fornicates, flog her, then if she fornicates, flog her, then if she fornicates, sell her even for a braid of hair."
"3758","Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Kaysan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A rooster crowed one day while we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a man cursed it. So he said: Do not curse it."
"3759","Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah, from Abu Hurayrah, Zayd ibn Khalid and Shibl, may Allah be pleased with them, they said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man stood to him and said: I implore you by Allah but that you judge between us by the Book of Allah. His opponent stood, more understanding than him, and said: Yes, judge between us by the Book of Allah and permit me to speak. He said: Speak. He said: My son was a laborer for this one and fornicated with his wife. I was told my son deserves stoning, so I ransomed from him with a hundred sheep and a servant. Then I asked men of knowledge, and they informed me that upon my son is a hundred lashes and a year of exile, and upon the wife of this one is stoning. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I shall judge between you by the Book of Allah. The hundred sheep and the servant are returned to you. Upon your son is a hundred lashes and a year of exile. Go, Unays, to the wife of this one; if she confesses, stone her. So he went to her and she confessed, and he stoned her."
"3760","Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, and Ibn Uyaynah, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: People were rained upon one night at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In the morning he said: Did you not hear what your Lord said tonight? He said: No blessing I bestow upon My servants but a group becomes disbelieving thereby, saying: We were rained by such-and-such star. As for one who believes in Me and praises Me for My watering, that is one who believes in Me and disbelieves in the star. As for one who says: We were rained by such-and-such star, that is one who disbelieves in Me and believes in the star."
"3761","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah, from Busr ibn Said, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not prevent the bondwomen of Allah from the mosques of Allah, and let them go out unscented."
"3762","And Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Abdur Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Rabiah ibn Abi Abdur Rahman, from Yazid, freedman of al-Munbaith, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about lost property. He said: Memorize its container and its strap, then announce it for a year. Then if its owner comes, return it to him."
"3763","And Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Rabiah, from Yazid, from Zayd, with similar wording. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about stray camels? He said: What have you to do with it? With it are its shoes and its water; it comes to water and eats trees. And he was asked about stray sheep? He said: For you, your brother, or the wolf."
"3764","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Ata, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever provides breakfast for a fasting person shall have like his reward, without diminishing the fasting person's reward at all. And whoever equips a warrior in the path of Allah or stays behind for him among his family shall have like the warrior's reward, without diminishing the warrior's reward at all."
"3765","Yahya ibn Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Abdullah ibn Tumah, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed sacrifices among his companions. He said: So he sent to me a young kid goat. I brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, it is a kid. He said: Sacrifice with it."
"3766","Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Ata, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray in your houses and do not make them graves."
"3767","Bishr ibn Adam ibn bint Azhar and Salamah ibn Shabib narrated to us, they said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Ubayy ibn Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman narrated to me, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm narrated to me, he said: Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit narrated to me, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Amrah al-Ansari narrated to me, he said: Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The best witnesses are those who give their testimony before being asked for it."
"3768","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Had, from Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, from Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his guest. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or be silent. Hospitality is three days; what is beyond is charity."
"3769","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz informed us, he said: Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Amr informed us, from Busr ibn Said, from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Angels do not enter a house in which there is an image nor a dog. He said: And I heard him saying: Except a figure on a garment."
"3770","Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Amr, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah, from Zayd ibn Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sleep one night, then wake startled, stand and pray two light rakahs, then stand and pray two long rakahs, then pray two light rakahs less than the two before them, then pray two less than the two before them, then pray two less than the two before them, then pray two less than the two before them, then pray witr. That is thirteen rakahs."
"3771","3782 - Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id, he said: Abu Juhaym sent me to Zayd ibn Khalid asking him about one who passes in front of one who is praying, so he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: If the one who passes in front of one who is praying knew what is upon him, his standing for forty autumns would be better for him than passing in front of him."
"3772","The Musnad of Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, may Allah be pleased with him. What Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3783 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Ibn al-Mughirah meaning Ubaydullah, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that anyone cleanse himself with a bone, or dung, or charred remains."
"3773","3784 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih al-Harrani Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu Zurah Amr ibn Jabir al-Hadrami, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People will come out from the east and prepare the authority for al-Mahdi."
"3774","3785 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Ziyad al-Hadrami, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz, that he and a companion of his passed by some people, and youths of Quraysh had loosened their waist-wrappers and were naked striking one another with them. Al-Zubaydi said: When we passed by them, they said: These are such-and-such, so leave them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them, and when they saw him they rushed away, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned angry, and I was behind the chamber hearing him saying: Glory be to Allah; neither did they show modesty before Allah nor did they cover themselves before His Messenger."
"3775","3786 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I would wish that there were a barrier between me and the people of Najran, because of how intensely they used to dispute with him."
"3776","3787 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: al-Miqdam ibn Salamah al-Hajri narrated to me, from Abbas ibn Julayd al-Hajri, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi saying: We never heard a loud sound in Madinah except that we thought it was the Dajjal, because of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to narrate to us about him and bring him near to us."
"3777","3788 - Umar ibn al-Khattab and Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq narrated to us, they said: Ibn Abi Maryam informed us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Mulayl, that his father informed him that he heard Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi mentioning that the Jews brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a Jewish man and a Jewish woman who had committed adultery though married, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded regarding them and they were stoned. Abdullah ibn al-Harith said: And I was among those who stoned them."
"3778","3789 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih informed us, he said: al-Layth informed us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man died from among those who had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and accepted Islam as a stranger, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while at the grave: What is your name? I said: al-Asi. And he said to Ibn Umar: What is your name? He said: al-Asi. And he said to al-Asi: What is your name? He said: al-Asi. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are the slaves of Allah; go down. He said: So we buried our companion, then we came out of the grave while our names had been changed."
"3779","3790 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Muslim ibn Yazid al-Sadafi, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque and ascended the pulpit and said: Amin, Amin, Amin. When he finished it was said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you do something you did not used to do. He said: Jibril appeared to me on the first step and said: O Muhammad, whoever reaches one of his parents and they do not admit him into Paradise, may Allah cast him far away, then cast him far away. He said: So I said: Amin. Then he said to me on the second step: And whoever reaches the month of Ramadan and is not forgiven, may Allah cast him far away, then cast him far away, so I said: Amin. Then he appeared to me on the third and said: Whoever you are mentioned in his presence and he does not send blessings upon you, may Allah cast him far away, then cast him far away, so I said: Amin."
"3780","The Musnad of Jariyah ibn Zufar al-Hanafi, may Allah be pleased with him. What Jariyah ibn Zufar al-Hanafi narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3791 - Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Dahtham ibn Qurran narrated to us, he said: Nimran ibn Jariyah narrated to us, from his father, that some people brought a dispute to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning a reed hut, so he sent Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman to judge between them, and he judged it for the one to whom the binding strips were nearer. When he returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, he said: You judged rightly and well."
"3781","3792 - Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, he said: Dahtham ibn Qurran narrated to us, he said: Nimran ibn Jariyah narrated to me, from his father, that a man struck a man with a sword on his forearm and cut it off, so he sought justice against him before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered blood-money for him. He said: I want retaliation. He said: Take the blood-money, may Allah bless it for you. And he did not judge retaliation for him."
"3782","3793 - One of our companions narrated to us, from Asad ibn Amr, from Dahtham ibn Qurran, from Nimran ibn Jariyah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take fresh water for the head."
"3783","The Musnad of Abu Burdah ibn Niyar, may Allah be pleased with him. What Abu Burdah ibn Niyar narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3794 - Abdullah ibn Said Abu Said al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Ashath narrated to us, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara, that he saw his maternal uncle Abu Burdah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to a man who had married his father's wife and commanded me to strike his neck."
"3784","3795 - Shuayb ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, from Sufyan, and al-Hasan ibn Salih, from al-Suddi, from Adi ibn Thabit, from al-Bara, he said: I met my maternal uncle with the banner with him, and he said: Where are you going? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to a man who married his father's wife and commanded me to strike his neck, and he said: Kill him."
"3785","3796 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Abu Yala Muhammad ibn al-Salt narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father, from Abu Burdah ibn Niyar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not strike more than ten lashes except in a prescribed punishment of Allah's punishments."
"3786","3797 - al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim narrated to us, he said: Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Isa, from Jumay ibn Umayr, from his uncle meaning Abu Burdah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever cheats us is not of us."
"3787","3798 - Abdah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Wail ibn Dawud, from Jumay ibn Umayr, from his uncle, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which earning is purest? He said: A man's work with his hand, and every blessed sale."
"3788","3799 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Said ibn Said Abu al-Sabbah, from Said ibn Umayr, from his uncle Abu Burdah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sends blessings upon me of his own accord, Allah sends ten blessings upon him for it, and removes ten sins from him, and raises him ten degrees."
"3789","The Musnad of Amir ibn Rabiah, may Allah be pleased with him. What Amir ibn Rabiah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3800 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Qushayri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a servant dies and Allah knows evil of him, while people say good, Allah the Mighty and Majestic says to His angels: I have accepted the testimony of My servants concerning My servant and forgiven him what I know of him."
"3790","3801 - Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abdullah, the freedman of the family of Manzur, narrated to me, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the mosque and his sandal-strap broke, so I took his sandal to mend it, but he took it from my hand and said: It is favoritism, and I do not love favoritism."
"3791","3802 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from Asim ibn Umar, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj alone."
"3792","3803 - Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to me, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not touch of the corners except the Yamani and the Black."
"3793","3804 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us. 3805 - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, he said: Nafi informed me, from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabiah."
"3794","3806 - And Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bahr al-Bakrawi and Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn al-Akhnas, he said: Nafi narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabiah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3795","3807 - And al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Nafi and Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabiah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3796","3808 - And Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabiah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3797","3809 - And Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bahr and Azhar ibn Sad narrated to us, they said: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Amir ibn Rabiah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you sees the funeral procession, if he is not walking along with it, let him stand for it until it passes him by or is placed down."
"3798","3810 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray the voluntary prayer upon his mount."
"3799","3811 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sends a blessing upon me of his own accord, Allah sends ten blessings upon him for it."
"3800","3812 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ashath ibn Said narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and the sky became cloudy and the qiblah became unclear to us. He said: So we prayed, and when the sun rose we found that we had prayed toward other than the qiblah, so we mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah revealed: So wherever you turn, there is the Face of Allah."
"3801","3813 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) using the siwak while fasting more times than I can count."
"3802","3814 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father."
"3803","3815 - And Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that a woman of Banu Fazarah married for two sandals, and that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Are you pleased? She said: Yes, so he permitted that. And Sharik said in his hadith: He said: Then it is your affair and his."
"3804","3816 - Muhammad ibn Muawiyah al-Ziyadi narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn Bayan narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man took another man's sandal and frightened him, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fright of a Muslim is grave before Allah."
"3805","3817 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever dies with no obedience upon him dies the death of ignorance, and whoever casts it off after contracting it will meet Allah with no proof for him. Behold, no man is ever alone with a woman, for Satan is the third of them, and he is farther from the two. Whoever is pleased by his good deed and grieved by his evil deed, he is a believer."
"3806","3818 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The womb is a branch; whoever joins it, Allah joins him, and whoever cuts it, Allah cuts him."
"3807","3819 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, he said: Mualla ibn Mansur, or Ali ibn Qadim, narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man entered the mosque and said: Praise be to Allah, abundant, good, blessed praise, until our Lord is pleased and after pleasure. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when he finished: Which of you said the words? The man said: I did. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw twelve angels racing to it, which of them would write it."
"3808","3820 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Abdullah al-Umari narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat with three fingers and lick them when he finished."
"3809","3821 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Mukharrimi narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: al-Umari narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss Uthman ibn Mazun after he died."
"3810","3822 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-Umari narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at the grave of Uthman ibn Mazun after he buried him and ordered the sprinkling of water."
"3811","3823 - And Muhammad ibn Isa al-Tamimi and al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Umari narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let those of understanding and reason stand nearest to me, then those next to them."
"3812","3824 - Sulayman ibn Sayf al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Amir ibn Rabiah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: On buried treasure is the fifth."
"3813","3825 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would indeed send us on expeditions with no food except salaf of dates, so we would take a handful at a time until we ended up with a single date at a time."
"3814","The Musnad of Safinah, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with him. What Safinah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3826 - Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Jumhan narrated to us, from Safinah, that Ali hosted guests and prepared food for them, so Fatimah said: If only we would invite the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to enter with us. So they invited him and he came and placed his hand on the two doorposts, then saw the patterned curtain hung on one side of the house, so he turned back. Fatimah said to Ali: Go and see what turned him back. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what turned you back? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been forbidden to enter a decorated house."
"3815","3827 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Talhah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Jumhan narrated to us, from Safinah."
"3816","3828 - Talut narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The caliphate after me is thirty years. Then Safinah said to me: Count two years for Abu Bakr, ten for Umar, twelve for Uthman, and six for Ali."
"3817","3829 - Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw as if a balance was let down from the sky, and you were weighed against Abu Bakr and you outweighed Abu Bakr, then Abu Bakr was weighed against Umar and Abu Bakr outweighed Umar, then Umar was weighed against Uthman and Umar outweighed Uthman, then the balance was raised. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) interpreted it as the caliphate of prophethood, then Allah will give sovereignty to whom He wills."
"3818","3830 - Rizqullah ibn Musa narrated to us, if Allah wills, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and when any of the people grew weary he would cast his shield upon Safinah, until he carried much baggage from that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are Safinah."
"3819","3831 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Amr ibn Harun, from Suhayb, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he shed the blood of a camel with a stump, then asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that. He said: Did the blood gush forth? He said: Yes, so he commanded him to eat it."
"3820","3832 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Abu Rayhanah narrated to us, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution with a mudd and bathe with a sa."
"3821","3833 - Muammal ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Abu Rayhanah, from Safinah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"3822","3834 - Ishaq ibn Hatim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Safinah informed me, from his father, from his grandfather Safinah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped and said to me: Hide the blood. So I went and drank it, then came. He said to me: What did you do? I said: I hid it. He said: Did you drink it? I said: Yes."
"3823","3835 - Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Safinah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade traveling with the Quran to the land of the enemy, fearing the enemy would get hold of it."
"3824","3836 - al-Nadr ibn Tahir narrated to us, he said: Burayh ibn Umar ibn Safinah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather."
"3825","3837 - And al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Burayh ibn Umar ibn Safinah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather Safinah, he said: I ate bustard meat with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3826","3838 - Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was at sea and our ship broke, and we did not know the way, when a lion confronted us. My companions held back, so I drew near to it and said: I am Safinah, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we have lost our way. So it walked before me until it brought us to the road, then turned aside and pushed me as if showing me the way, then began to hum, and I thought it was bidding us farewell."
"3827","3839 - al-Sakan ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us. And Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Said ibn Jumhan, from Safinah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting when a man passed on a camel with a leader before it and a driver behind it, so he said: May Allah curse the leader, the driver, and the rider."
"3828","3840 - Muhammad ibn Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Misari narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to me, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Safinah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) devoted himself to worship before he died and withdrew from women until he became like a worn water-skin."
"3829","3841 - And Abd al-Ala ibn Wasil narrated to us, he said: Awn ibn Sallam narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Buraydah ibn Sufyan narrated to us, from Safinah, and he was a servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Some birds were gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I prepared some of them for him. In the morning I brought it to him, and he said: Where did you get this from? I said: From what was brought to you yesterday. He said: Did I not tell you not to store up food for tomorrow; for every day is its provision? Then he said: O Allah, admit to me the most beloved of Your creation to You to eat this bird with me. So Ali entered, and he said: O Allah, and to me."
"3830","The Musnad of Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him. What Abu Barzah al-Aslami narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3842 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated to us, they said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were on a journey with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and an Ansari girl was on a camel of hers. She looked at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) behind her while the mountain pass had grown narrow for them, so she said: Hal, Hal, may the curse of Allah be upon you, meaning her camel. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is the owner of this camel or mount? No camel upon which is a curse from Allah shall accompany us."
"3831","3843 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Samah narrated to us, from Abu al-Wazi, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me something I may benefit from. He said: Remove harm from the path of the Muslims."
"3832","3844 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, from Abu al-Hakam, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What I fear for you is the lusts of error in your bellies and your private parts, and the misguiding desires."
"3833","3845 - Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Wazi narrated to us, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man to a quarter of the Arab quarters, and they beat him and reviled him. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: If you had gone to the people of Oman, they would neither have beaten you nor reviled you."
"3834","3846 - Amr ibn Ali and Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Sharik ibn Shihab, he said: I used to wish to meet a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask him about the Khawarij, so I met Abu Barzah on a day of Eid among a group of his companions, and I said to him: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention the Khawarij? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my ears and saw him with my eyes. He said: Wealth was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he gave those on his right and those on his left, and gave nothing to those behind him. So a man from behind him stood and said: O Muhammad, by Allah you have not been just in division since today. He was a black man with close-cropped hair, upon him two white garments. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became intensely angry and said: By Allah, you will not find after me a man more just to you than me. Then he said: There will emerge at the end of time a people as if this one is of them; they will recite the Quran and it will not pass beyond their throats; they will pass out of Islam as the arrow passes out of the game; their mark is shaving. They will keep emerging until the last of them emerges with the False Messiah, the Dajjal. When you meet them, kill them; they are the worst of creation and creatures."
"3835","3847 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Kinanah ibn Nuaym, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met the enemy and Allah granted him booty. Then he said on the second and the third: Do you miss anyone? They said: No. He said: But I miss Julaybib; go and seek him among the slain. So they sought him and found him beside seven whom he had killed and then they killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and was informed, so he came until he stood over him and said: This one is of me and I am of him; he killed seven then they killed him; this one is of me and I am of him, saying it twice. Then he carried him upon his forearms; he had no bed other than the forearms of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until a grave was dug for him and he was buried, and he did not mention washing."
"3836","3848 - Muhammad ibn Mamar and Raja ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abu Hashim, from Rufay Abu al-Aliyah, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and wanted to stand, he would say: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; I bear witness that there is no god but You; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you say words you did not used to say before. He said: This is an expiation for what occurs in the gathering."
"3837","3849 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Nusayr and Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, they said: Abu Talhah narrated to us, from Abu al-Wazi, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have a Hawd whose breadth is like its length, between Aylah and Sana; two spouts pour into it from Paradise, one of silver and the other of gold; it is whiter than milk, sweeter than honey, and cooler than snow; in it are vessels as many as the stars of the sky; whoever drinks a drink from it will never thirst until he enters Paradise. Rawh said in his hadith: And Shaddad ibn Said added, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Wazi, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: They leap in the hands of the believers."
"3838","3850 - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bahr narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Musawir al-Warraq, he said: I said to Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him: Whom did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stone? He said: A man of us called Maiz ibn Malik."
"3839","3851 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Masadah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: I saw Abu Barzah, and Ibn Ziyad sent to him asking him about the Hawd. He said: And inform me who entered with him. He said: When Ibn Ziyad saw him he said: This Muhammad of yours is short and stout. He said: When he heard it he said: I did not think I would live until I was taunted with the companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So it was said to him: You are not taunted for that. And he said: And Ibn Ziyad asked him about the Hawd, so he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I have a Hawd. So whoever denies it, may Allah not bring him to it."
"3840","3852 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Uthman narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Barzah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sleeping before it and talking after it."
"3841","3853 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, from Shubah, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu al-Minhal, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3842","3854 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Barzah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Aslam, may Allah grant them safety, and Ghifar, may Allah forgive them."
"3843","3855 - Ahmad ibn Sinan al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in a long hadith, that he performed ablution and wiped over the two leather socks."
"3844","3856 - Muhammad ibn Musa al-Qattan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Musayyib Sallam ibn Salim al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of people are my generation, then those next to them, then those next to them."
"3845","3857 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Numan narrated to us, he said: Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The rulers are from Quraysh; I have a right upon them and they have a right upon you, so long as they do three things: when mercy is sought from them they show mercy, when they judge they are just, and when they pledge they fulfill. Whoever of them does not do that, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people."
"3846","3858 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Amir narrated to us, from Muhammad of the family of Abu Barzah, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting while traveling is not righteousness."
"3847","3859 - Abbad ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from Abu Hilal al-Akki, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at two men on the day of Uhud reciting this poetry about Hamzah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, overturn them into tribulation with overturning, and summon them to punishment with summoning."
"3848","The hadith of Abu Barzah al-Aslami from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3842 (m) - Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Shahid narrated to us, they said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and an Ansari girl was on a camel of hers. She looked at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) behind her while the mountain pass had grown narrow for them, so she said: Hal, Hal, may the curse of Allah be upon you, meaning her camel. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is the owner of this camel or mount? No camel upon which is a curse from Allah shall accompany us. And these words have been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in different wordings, and we have mentioned each hadith with its wording in its place. And we do not know it narrated from Abu Barzah except from this channel. And the name of Abu Barzah is Nadlah ibn Ubayd, and the name of Abu Azzah is Yasar ibn Ubayd, and the name of Abu Uthman al-Nahdi is Abd al-Rahman ibn Mull."
"3849","3847 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Kinanah ibn Nuaym, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met the enemy and Allah granted him booty. So he said: Do you miss anyone? They said: Yes, so-and-so and so-and-so. Then he said: Do you miss anyone on the second and the third? They said: No. He said: But I miss Julaybib; go and seek him among the slain. So they sought him and found him beside seven whom he had killed and then they killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and was informed, so he came to him until he stood over him and said: This one is of me and I am of him; he killed seven; this one is of me and I am of him, saying it twice. Then he carried him upon his forearms; he had no bed other than the forearms of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until a grave was dug for him and he was buried, and he did not mention washing. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it except Abu Barzah, and we do not know for it a channel from Abu Barzah except this channel."
"3850","3846 (m) - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad meaning Ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Sharik ibn Shihab, he said: I used to wish to meet a man of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to ask him about the Khawarij, so I met Abu Barzah on a day of Eid among a group of his companions, and I said to him: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention the Khawarij? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my ears and saw him with my eyes. Wealth was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he gave those on his right and those on his left and gave nothing to those behind him. So a man from behind him stood and said: O Muhammad, by Allah you have not been just in division since today. And he was a black man with close-cropped hair, upon him two white garments. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became intensely angry and said: By Allah, you will not find after me a man more just to you than me. Then he said: There will emerge at the end of time a people as if this one is of them; they will recite the Quran and it will not pass beyond their throats; they will pass out of Islam as the arrow passes out of the game; their mark is shaving. They will keep emerging until the last of them emerges with the False Messiah, the Dajjal. When you meet them, kill them; they are the worst of creation and creatures. And this hadith has been narrated with similar wording from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from more than one channel, so we mentioned each hadith with its wording in its place. And we do not know anyone narrated from Sharik ibn Shihab except al-Azraq ibn Qays, and we do not know he narrated other than this hadith."
"3851","3860 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Wadi, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two traders have the choice so long as they have not parted."
"3852","3861 / 1 - And Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Khidash narrated it to us, he said: Abd al-Qahir ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Jamil ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Wadi, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it."
"3853","3843 (m) - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Samah narrated to us, from Abu al-Wazi, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me something I may benefit from. He said: Remove harm from the path of the Muslims. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Abu Barzah. And Abu al-Wazi is a man of Basra; Ayyub, Shaddad ibn Said, Mahdi ibn Maymun and others narrated from him."
"3854","3849 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Aslam and al-Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us, they said: Abu Talhah, and he is Shaddad ibn Said, narrated to us, from Abu al-Wazi, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have a Hawd whose breadth is like its length, between Aylah and Sana; two spouts pour into it from Paradise, one of silver and the other of gold; it is whiter than milk, sweeter than honey, and cooler than snow; in it are vessels as many as the stars of the sky; whoever drinks a drink from it will never thirst until he enters Paradise. Rawh said in his hadith: And Shaddad ibn Said added, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Wazi, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: They leap in the hands of the believers, meaning the vessels. And we do not know Ayyub narrated from Abu al-Wazi except this hadith, nor did anyone narrate it from Ayyub except Shaddad ibn Said."
"3855","3845 (m) - Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Wazi narrated to us, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a man to a quarter of the Arab quarters, and they reviled him and beat him. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: If you had gone to the people of Oman, they would neither have beaten you nor reviled you. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and something close to it has been narrated from Ibn Umar in other than its wording."
"3856","3848 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar and Raja ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abu Hashim, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and wanted to stand, he would say: Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; I bear witness that there is no god but You; I seek Your forgiveness and repent to You. They said: O Messenger of Allah, you say words you did not used to say before. He said: This is an expiation for what occurs of the gathering. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Barzah except from this channel with this isnad."
"3857","3852 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Uthman al-Ghatafani narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Barzah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sleeping before it and talking after it, meaning al-Isha."
"3858","3853 (m) - And Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Shubah, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu al-Minhal, from Abu Barzah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it. And the hadith of Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Barzah, from his father, I think Uthman ibn Uthman erred in it; the correct is Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu al-Minhal, from Abu Barzah. And Abu al-Minhal, his name is Sayyar ibn Salamah."
"3859","3857 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Numan Muhammad ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, he said: Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The rulers are from Quraysh; I have a right upon them and they have a right upon you, so long as they do three things: when mercy is sought from them they show mercy, when they judge they are just, and when they pledge they fulfill. Whoever of them does not do that, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated except from Abu Barzah with this isnad. And Sukayn is a well-known man of Basra."
"3860","3844 (m) - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab, and his name is Jafar ibn Hayyan, narrated to us, from Abu al-Hakam, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What I fear for you is the lusts of error in your bellies and your private parts, and the misguiding desires. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated with this wording except from Abu Barzah with this isnad."
"3861","3854 (m) - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, they said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Barzah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Aslam, may Allah grant them safety, and Ghifar, may Allah forgive them."
"3862","3861 / 2 - And Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi narrated it to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Barzah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) similar to it. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Barzah except from this channel, and we do not know anyone narrated it from Ali ibn Zayd except Shubah."
"3863","3851 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Masadah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: I saw Abu Barzah, and Ibn Ziyad sent to him asking him about the Hawd. He said: And he informed me who entered with him. He said: When Ibn Ziyad saw him he said: This Muhammad of yours is short and stout. He said: When Abu Barzah heard it he said: I did not think I would live until I was taunted with the companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So it was said to him: You are not taunted for that. He said: And Ibn Ziyad asked him about the Hawd, so he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, I think he said: I have a Hawd. So whoever denies it, may Allah not bring him to it, meaning it."
"3864","3850 (m) - Yahya ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Bahr Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman informed us, he said: Awf informed us, from al-Musawir, he said: I said to Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him: Whom did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stone? He said: A man of us called Maiz ibn Malik."
"3865","3856 (m) - Muhammad ibn Musa al-Qattan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Musayyib Sallam ibn Salm al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of people are my generation, then those next to them, then those next to them. And this hadith, we do not know anyone attributed it except Mubarak ibn Fadalah, from al-Azraq, from Abu Barzah, and we do not know anyone narrated it from Mubarak except Sallam ibn Salm."
"3866","3859 (m) - Abbad ibn Yaqub al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, from Abu Hilal al-Akki, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at two men on the day of Uhud reciting this poetry about Hamzah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, overturn them with overturning, and summon them to punishment with summoning. And Sulayman ibn Amr ibn al-Ahwas, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad and others narrated from him. And Abu Hilal al-Akki is an unknown man."
"3867","3855 (m) - Ahmad ibn Sinan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam informed us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, from Abu Barzah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in a long hadith he mentioned, that he performed ablution and wiped over the leather socks."
"3868","3858 (m) - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Uthmah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Amir narrated to us, from Muhammad, of the children of Abu Barzah, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness."
"3869","3862 - Muhammad ibn Ammar al-Razi narrated to us, he said: al-Sindi ibn Abduwayh narrated to us, he said: Jisr ibn Jafar narrated to us, from al-Azraq ibn Qays, he said: I heard Abu Barzah al-Aslami, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I fought with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) such-and-such campaign and such-and-such campaign, until he counted eight campaigns, and we saw from him nothing but ease and lightening. Abu Bakr said: Alqamah ibn Marthad only narrates from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father. And Muharib ibn Dithar only narrates from Sulayman ibn Buraydah. And Muhammad ibn Juhadah only narrates from Sulayman ibn Buraydah. And Sulayman al-Amash only narrates the hadith of Ibn Buraydah. So he mentioned more than one in it from Sulayman, and some of them said: From Ibn Buraydah without naming him, and in my view he is Sulayman. And the rest of the companions of Ibn Buraydah only narrate from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, except a man named Sulayman."
"3870","The Musnad of Abu Rafi, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with him. What Abu Rafi, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3863 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Qusayt informed me, from Abu Rafi, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a guest stayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent me seeking food for him. I came to a Jewish man and said: Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says to you: A guest has stayed with us and we do not find with us some of what would suffice him, so sell to me or lend to me until the crescent of Rajab. The Jew said: No, by Allah, I will neither lend him nor sell him except with a pledge. So I returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He said: By Allah, I am trustworthy among the people of heaven, trustworthy among the people of earth; had he lent me or sold me I would have paid him. Go with my armor. Then this verse was revealed consoling him over the world: Do not extend your eyes toward what We have given enjoyment of to spouses among them."
"3871","3864 - Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Amr informed us, from Ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution once once."
"3872","3865 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate meat from a shoulder, then prayed and did not perform ablution."
"3873","3866 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abdullah, from Ikrimah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: Abu Rafi said: I was in charge of the wealth of al-Abbas, and Islam had entered us, the household. So I accepted Islam, and al-Abbas accepted Islam, and Umm al-Fadl accepted Islam. Al-Abbas feared his people and disliked opposing them, and he concealed his Islam. He had much wealth scattered among his people. Abu Lahab had stayed back and sent in his place al-Asi ibn Hashim ibn al-Mughirah; that is what they used to do: no man stayed back except he sent another man in his place. When the news came of the calamity of Quraysh at Badr, we found strength and honor in ourselves. I was a weak man, and I used to make drinking-cups, carving them in the chamber of Zamzam. By Allah, I was sitting in it carving my cups, with Umm al-Fadl sitting with me, and what had come to us had pleased us, when Abu Lahab came until he sat at the tent-peg of the chamber and leaned his back against my back. Then the people said: Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib has come, with the people standing over him. He said: O son of my brother, inform me, for you have the news. He said: No, by Allah, it was nothing but that we met the people and turned our backs to them; they killed us as they wished and captured us as they wished. And by Allah, with that I saw men on piebald horses between heaven and earth, against which nothing could stand. Abu Rafi said: So I raised the tent-peg of the chamber and said: By Allah, those are the angels. So Abu Lahab raised his hand and struck my face with it a severe blow. I grappled with him, but he lifted me and struck me to the ground, then knelt upon me beating me, though I was a weak man. Umm al-Fadl said: You found him weak. So he rose turning away humiliated. By Allah, he did not live after that except seven nights, until Allah struck him with lentil-sores and killed him. His sons left him two or three nights without burying him until he stank in his house. Quraysh feared lentil-sores as people fear the plague, until a man of Quraysh said to his son, or to his two sons: Woe to you both, are you not ashamed that your father has stunk in your house and you do not bury him? They said: We fear it. He said: Then go, and I am with you. So they only washed him by throwing water upon him from afar, not touching him. Then they carried him and buried him in the upper part of Makkah."
"3874","3867 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Abu Rafi, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed, he would buy two fat horned white-black rams. When he prayed and delivered the sermon, one of them would be brought while he was in his prayer-place, and he would slaughter it, then say: O Allah, this is for all my ummah, whoever bore witness to You of oneness and bore witness to me of conveying. Then the other would be brought, and he would slaughter it and say: This is for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. Then he would feed both of them entirely to the poor, and he and his household would eat of them. He said: We remained years with no man of Banu Hashim sacrificing, for Allah had sufficed him by the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, the burden and the expense."
"3875","3868 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, would say as the muezzin says, and when he reached: Come to prayer, come to success, he would say: There is no power nor strength except by Allah."
"3876","3869 - Amr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abbas ibn Abi Khidash informed me, from al-Fadl ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Rafi, kill every dog in Madinah. So I found some Ansari women who had a dog, and they said: O Abu Rafi, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has sent our men on expeditions, and this dog protects us after Allah. By Allah, no one can approach us until a woman of us stands. So mention it to the Prophet, peace be upon him. He said: So I mentioned that to the Prophet, peace be upon him. He said: Kill it, for Allah is the One who protects them."
"3877","3870 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna Abu Musa and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, they said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdullah, from Abadil ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his grandmother, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Baqi al-Gharqad, walking behind him, he said: May you neither be guided nor guide; may you neither be guided nor guide; may you neither be guided nor guide. Abu Rafi said: What is with me, O Messenger of Allah? He said: I do not mean you, but I mean the owner of this grave. And he was asked about me, and he claimed he does not know me. And there was a grave sprinkled with water when its owner was buried."
"3878","3871 - Abbad ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim ibn al-Barid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet, peace be upon him, was given prophethood on Monday, and Ali accepted Islam on Tuesday."
"3879","3872 - Abbad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim ibn al-Barid narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi narrated to us, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The first to accept Islam of men was Ali, and the first to accept Islam of women was Khadijah."
"3880","3873 - Abbad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali before his death: You will clear my liability and be killed upon my Sunnah."
"3881","3874 - Abbad narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from his father and his uncle, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali as commander over Yemen, and a man of Aslam called Amr ibn Shas went out with him. He returned blaming Ali and complaining of him. So the Messenger of Allah sent to him and said: Tell us, Amr, did you see any injustice from Ali in his judgment or favoritism in his division? He said: O Allah, no. He said: Then why do you say what reaches me? He said: Hatred of him, which I cannot control. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry until that was seen in his face, and said: Whoever hates him has hated me, and whoever hates me has hated Allah; and whoever loves him has loved me, and whoever loves me has loved Allah."
"3882","3875 - Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Fayid, the freedman of Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Ali, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A sheep's belly was cooked for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ate, then prayed and did not perform ablution."
"3883","3876 - Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Fayid narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Ali, from his grandfather, he said: I slaughtered a sheep with a peg, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I slaughtered a sheep with a peg. He said: Eat it."
"3884","3877 - Muhammad ibn al-Haytham al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hunayni narrated to us, he said: Fayid narrated to us, the freedman of Ubaydullah ibn Ali, from Ubaydullah ibn Ali ibn Abi Rafi, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Prophet, peace be upon him, circumambulate the House upon his mount, touching the corner with his staff."
"3885","3878 - Abbad ibn Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim ibn al-Barid narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir. Muhammad said: And my father and Abdullah meaning his uncle, and Ubaydullah, narrated to me, from their father, from Abu Rafi, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali ibn Abi Talib: Allah commanded me to teach you and not to slight you, and to bring you near and not to distance you. So it is a duty upon me to teach you, and a duty upon you to retain."
"3886","3879 - Yusuf ibn Musa and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, they said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the adhan in the ear of al-Hasan ibn Ali when his mother Fatimah bore him, with the prayer."
"3887","3880 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Mindal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bathed for the two Eids, and came to the Eid walking, and returned by other than the road by which he went out."
"3888","3881 - al-Hasan ibn Qazaah narrated to us, he said: al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami narrated to us, from Abu Asma, the freedman of the family of Jafar, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: There will be something between you and Aishah. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I? He said: Yes. He said: I, of all your companions? He said: Yes. He said: Then am I the most wretched of them? He said: No. He said: Then when that happens, return her to her place of safety."
"3889","3882 - Ghassan ibn Ubaydullah al-Rasibi narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Nafi ibn Abdullah ibn Nafi ibn Thabit ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Mawali narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and reached Baqi al-Gharqad. He turned to me and said: Do you hear what I hear? I said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, no, O Messenger of Allah. He said: This is so-and-so son of so-and-so being punished in his grave for a woolen cloak he misappropriated on the day of Khaybar."
"3890","3883 - Ghassan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Mawali narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When you name Muhammad, do not beat him and do not deprive him."
"3891","3884 - Ziyad ibn Yahya Abu al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Mamar ibn Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the ear of one of you rings, let him say: O Allah, mention with good whoever mentions us with good."
"3892","3885 - Ghassan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Mawali narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We found a document in the scabbard of the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after his death, in it was written: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Separate the beds of boys and girls, and brothers and sisters, at seven years. And beat your children for prayer when they reach, I think he said, nine. Cursed, cursed is whoever claims belonging to other than his people or to other than his patrons. Cursed is whoever cuts off anything of the boundaries of the land, meaning thereby the roads of the Muslims."
"3893","3886 - Ghassan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Mawali narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died with his head in the lap of Ali ibn Abi Talib, while saying to Ali: Fear Allah, fear Allah, regarding what your right hands possess; fear Allah, fear Allah, regarding prayer. So the last of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke was that."
"3894","3887 - Ghassan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Mawali narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in prayer, he struck something in his prayer, and it was a scorpion; he struck it and killed it. And he commanded killing the scorpion, the snake, the mouse, and the kite for the one in ihram."
"3895","The Musnad of Abu Dharr al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him. The Musnad of Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Abbas from Abu Dharr. 3888 - Abu Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ayyub al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Akhzam Abu Talib al-Tai narrated to us, he said: Abu Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: al-Muthanna ibn Said al-Dubai narrated to us, he said: Abu Jamrah informed me, he said: Ibn Abbas said to us: Shall I not inform you of the Islam of Abu Dharr? We said: Yes. He said: Abu Dharr said: I was a man of Banu Ghifar, and it reached us that a man had emerged in Makkah claiming he is a Prophet. So I said to my brother: Go to this man to speak to him and bring me his news. So he went and met him, then returned. I said: What have you? He said: By Allah, I saw a man commanding good and forbidding evil. I said: You have not sufficed me of the news. So I took a provision-bag and a staff, then headed to Makkah. I did not know him and disliked asking about him, and I would drink from the water of Zamzam and stay in the mosque. A man passed by me and said: It is as if the man is a stranger. I said: Yes. He said: Then go to the dwelling. So I went with him, he asking me nothing and I asking him nothing. In the morning I went to the mosque to ask about him, with no one informing me anything about him. A man passed by me and said: Do you know his dwelling, though I did not yet know his dwelling? I said: No. So he went with me and admitted me to his dwelling. Then he said to me: What brought you to this town? He said: I said: It reached us that a man has emerged here claiming he is a Prophet, so I sent my brother and he did not suffice me of the news, and I wanted to meet him. He said to me: Know that you have been guided; this is my face toward him, so follow me and enter where I enter. If I see anyone I fear for you, I will stand behind the wall as if mending my sandal, and you go on. So he went and I went with him until he entered and I entered with him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, present Islam to me. So he presented it to me and I accepted Islam on the spot. He said to me: O Abu Dharr, return to your land, and when our emergence reaches you, come. I said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I will surely cry it out among them. So he came to the mosque with Quraysh in it and said: O company of Quraysh, I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. They said: Stand to this renegade. So they stood and I was beaten until near death, and al-Abbas caught up with me and bent over me and said: Woe to you, would you kill a man of Banu Ghifar while your trade passes over Ghifar? So they left me. In the morning I went to the mosque and said the like of what I had said yesterday, and the like of what was done yesterday was done to me. Al-Abbas caught up with me and bent over me, and said: Would you kill a man of Banu Ghifar? And he said the like of his saying yesterday. So this was the Islam of Abu Dharr. And we do not know it narrated from Ibn Abbas, from Abu Dharr, concerning the story of his Islam, except from this channel. And this al-Muthanna ibn Said is Basran, trustworthy. And Abu Jamrah, his name is Nasr ibn Imran."
"3896","3889 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Buhlul ibn Muwarriq narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Nuwayfi or Nufay informed me, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, that Abu Dharr said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The most beloved of you to me and nearest of you to me is the one who joins me upon what I covenanted with him. And these words, we do not know them narrated except from Abu Dharr. And we do not know anyone narrated from al-Walid ibn Nuwayfi except Musa ibn Ubaydah, and Musa was of the best of people and their worshippers."
"3897","Ibn Umar from Abu Dharr. 3890 - Amr ibn Ali, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, and Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Ata narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Umar, he said: I said to Abu Dharr: O my uncle, advise me. He said: You asked me as I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: If you pray Duha two rakahs, you will not be written among the heedless. If you pray four, you will be of the worshippers. If you pray six, no sin will catch up with you. If you pray eight, you will be written among the devout. If you pray twelve, a house will be built for you in Paradise. There is no day, nor night, nor hour except that Allah has in it a charity He bestows upon whom He wills of His servants. And He has not bestowed upon a servant the like of inspiring him His remembrance. And these words, we do not know them narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this channel. And we do not know Ibn Umar narrated from Abu Dharr a musnad hadith except this hadith."
"3898","Hudhayfah ibn Asid Abu Sarihah from Abu Dharr. 3891 - Yusuf ibn Musa and Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, they said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Jumay narrated to us, from Amir ibn Wathilah, and he is Abu al-Tufayl, that Abu Sarihah informed him that Abu Dharr stood at the gathering of Banu Ghifar and said: O Banu Ghifar, say and do not swear, three times, that the truthful one believed informed me that people will be gathered in three groups: a group eating and clothed, a group walking and running, and a group whom the Fire will gather and the angels drag upon their faces. A speaker said: These we have known, and these we have known. So what of those who walk and run? He said: Allah will cast a calamity upon mounts until even a man with a garden will give it for a worn-out camel, I think he said, but he will not give, or will not be able to. These words, we do not know them narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this channel with this isnad. And we do not know Hudhayfah ibn Asid narrated from Abu Dharr except this hadith."
"3899","Anas ibn Malik from Abu Dharr. 3892 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Yunus meaning Ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Dharr used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The roof of my house was split open while I was in Makkah, and Jibril, peace be upon him, descended and split open my chest, then washed it with the water of Zamzam. Then he brought a basin of gold filled with wisdom and faith and emptied it into my chest, then closed it. Then he took my hand, I think he said, and took me up to the heaven. When we came to the lowest heaven, Jibril said to the keeper of the heaven: Open. He said: Who is this? He said: Jibril. He said: Is anyone with you? He said: With me is Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And was he sent for? He said: Yes. He said: So he opened. When we rose above the lowest heaven, I was with a man sitting. He said: Welcome to the righteous Prophet and the righteous son. I said to Jibril: Who is this? He said: This is Adam. Then he took me up until he came to the second heaven, and Jibril said to its keeper the like of what he said to the keeper of the lowest heaven. Anas said: He mentioned that he found in the heavens Idris, Musa, Isa, and Ibrahim. And he mentioned that he found Ibrahim in the sixth heaven. He said: Welcome to the righteous Prophet. I said: Who is this? He said: Ibrahim. And these words have been narrated similar to them from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from more than one channel, and we do not know it narrated from Abu Dharr except from this channel."
"3900","Muawiyah ibn Hudayj from Abu Dharr. 3893 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Suwayd ibn Qays, from Muawiyah ibn Hudayj, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Arabian horse except it is permitted at every dawn two supplications, saying: O Allah, You entrusted me to whom You entrusted me of Your servants, so make me of the most beloved of his family and wealth to him, or the most beloved of his family and wealth to him. And these words, we do not know them narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this channel. And this Muawiyah ibn Hudayj has narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than the hadith of Salamah ibn al-Akwa from Abu Dharr."
"3901","3894 - Muhammad ibn Hashim, the nephew of Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath, narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Bakkar, the nephew of Musa ibn Ubaydah, narrated to us, from his uncle Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: A trial will befall you after me. I said: For Allah? He said: For Allah. I said: Welcome to the command of Allah. He said: O Abu Dharr, hear and obey, and I think he said, even to a black slave. And we do not know Salamah ibn al-Akwa narrated from Abu Dharr except this hadith, nor do we know for it a channel except this channel."
"3902","Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan from Abu Dharr. 3895 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, he said: I was in the mosque when Abu Dharr entered the mosque and prayed two rakahs at a pillar. Uthman said to him: How are you? He said: Well, how are you? Then he turned and began: Rivalry in increase distracted you. He was a man of loud voice, so he raised his voice and the mosque shook. Then he turned to the people, and I said: O Abu Dharr, or the people said to him: Narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: On camels is their charity, and on sheep is their charity. Abu Asim said: And I think he said: On cattle is their charity, and on wheat is its charity, and on gold, silver, and ore is its charity. Whoever gathers wealth and does not spend it in the path of Allah, and for debtors and the wayfarer, it will be a branding upon him on the Day of Resurrection. I said: O Abu Dharr, fear Allah and consider what you say, for wealth has become abundant in people's hands. He said: O son of my brother, ascribe yourself to me. So I ascribed myself to him. He said: I have known your greater lineage. Do you recite the Quran? I said: Yes. He said: Then recite: And those who hoard gold and silver, to the end of the verse. He said: Then understand."
"3903","3896 - And Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad informed us, he said: Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to us, from Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: On wheat is its charity, and he did not doubt concerning wheat. And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrates it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Dharr."
"3904","Abu al-Tufayl from Abu Dharr. 3897 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri wrote to me informing me in his letter that Ibn Uyaynah narrated to him, from Fitr ibn Khalifah, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left us with no bird in the sky flapping its wings except he had given us knowledge in it. And this hadith, Ibn Uyaynah narrated it, from Fitr, from Mundhir al-Thawri, he said: Abu Dharr said. And Mundhir al-Thawri did not meet Abu Dharr."
"3905","Abu Rafi from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3898 - Abbad ibn Yaqub al-Arzami narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather Abu Rafi, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said to Ali ibn Abi Talib: You are the first to believe in me, and you are the first to shake hands with me on the Day of Resurrection. You are the greatest truthful one, and you are the distinguisher separating between truth and falsehood. You are the leader of the believers, and wealth is the leader of the disbelievers. And this hadith, we do not know it narrated from Abu Dharr except from this channel, nor did Abu Rafi narrate from Abu Dharr except this hadith."
"3906","Ubaydullah ibn Abbas from Abu Dharr. 3899 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Said ibn Kathir al-Madani, he said: Kulthum ibn Jabr and Musa, and he did not attribute him, narrated to me that they heard Ubaydullah ibn Abbas saying: Abu Dharr said to me: O son of my brother, I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), holding his hand, and he said: O Abu Dharr, I would not love that I had Uhud in gold and silver to spend in the path of Allah, then die the day I die leaving of it a qirat. I said: O Messenger of Allah, a qintar? He said: O Abu Dharr, I go to the lesser and you go to the greater; I want the Hereafter and you want the world: a qirat. Then he repeated it to me three times. And these words have been narrated from Abu Dharr from more than one channel, and we do not know Ubaydullah ibn Abbas narrated from Abu Dharr except this hadith."
"3907","Said ibn al-Musayyib from Abu Dharr. 3900 - Amr ibn Ali, al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad, and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, with the wording of Amr, they said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of my household is like the ark of Nuh: whoever boards it is saved, whoever stays back from it drowns. And whoever fights us at the end of time is like one who fights alongside the Dajjal. And these words, we do not know them narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Abu Dharr from this channel, and we do not know anyone corroborated al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar on this hadith."
"3908","al-Ahnaf ibn Qays from Abu Dharr. 3901 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated to us, he said: Khulayd ibn Abdullah al-Asari narrated to us, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, he said: I was sitting among people of Quraysh when Abu Dharr came until he was near them, and said: Give glad tidings to hoarders of branding before their backs, emerging from before their bellies. He said: I said: Who is this? They said: Abu Dharr. He said: So I stood to him and said: What is this I heard you calling out before? He said: I said to them nothing but something I heard from their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I said: What do you say concerning this stipend? He said: Take it today, for in it is assistance. When it becomes a price for your religion, leave it. And this hadith is of good isnad. And we do not know Khulayd al-Asari attributed from al-Ahnaf except this hadith."
"3909","3902 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: A man came to Yazid ibn Abdullah and narrated, while I was listening, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, he said: I came to Madinah and mentioned similar to this hadith from Abu Dharr."
"3910","3903 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Harun ibn Riab, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: No servant prostrates to Allah a prostration except Allah raises him a degree by it and removes a sin from him by it. And these words, we do not know them narrated from Abu Dharr with a better isnad than this isnad."
"3911","Abu Uthman al-Nahdi from Abu Dharr. 3904 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Abu Uthman, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting three days of every month is the fasting of the whole year. Then he said: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth; Allah says: Whoever comes with a good deed will have ten like it. Thus Asim narrated it, from Abu Uthman, from Abu Dharr. And Thabit al-Bunani narrated it, from Abu Uthman, from Abu Hurayrah."
"3912","Abdullah ibn Shaqiq from him. 3905 - Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, from his father, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: I said to Abu Dharr: Had I seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would have asked him. He said: What would you have asked him? He said: I said: I would have asked him: Did you see your Lord? He said: I did ask him, and he said: Light; how could I see Him?"
"3913","3906 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: I said to Abu Dharr: Had I seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would have asked him. He said: What would you have asked him? He said: I would have asked him: Did you see your Lord? He said: I did ask him, and he said: How could I see Him? And this hadith, we do not know anyone narrated it from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq except Qatadah, nor do we know anyone narrated it from Qatadah except Hisham and Yazid ibn Ibrahim."
"3914","3907 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muadh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: I came to Madinah and there was a man standing upon black grain-sacks saying: Shall I not give glad tidings to hoarders of branding in foreheads and sides? They said: This is Abu Dharr, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3915","Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir from Abu Dharr. 3908 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ala Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir narrated to us, he said: Abu Abdullah Mutarrif said to me: A hadith of Abu Dharr used to reach me which I desired to meet him for, I think he said, so I met him. I said: I desired to meet you. He said: May Allah bless your father, you have indeed met, so bring it. I said: It used to reach me from you that you claim the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to narrate to you that Allah the Blessed and Exalted loves three and hates three. He said: Yes, and I do not think I would lie about my beloved; yes, and I do not think I would lie about my beloved; yes, and I do not think I would lie about my beloved. He said: I said: Who are these three whom Allah loves? He said: A man who fights in the path of Allah, expecting reward, striving, and meets the enemy and fights. He said: And you find him in the revealed Book of Allah. Then he interpreted this verse: Indeed Allah loves those who fight in His path in ranks as if they are a solid structure. He said: I said: And who? He said: And a man with an evil neighbor who harms him, and he endures his harm expecting reward until Allah suffices him or he dies. He said: And I said: And who? He said: And a man among a traveling people who journey at night until when in the last of the night fatigue and drowsiness overcome them, so they dismount and lay their heads down. Then he performs ablution, stands, purifies himself, and prays, out of awe of Allah and desire for what is with Him. He said: I said: Who are the three whom Allah hates? He said: The arrogant boaster, and you find him with you, meaning in the Book of Allah: Indeed Allah does not love whoever is arrogant, boasting. I said: And who? He said: The stingy taunter. I said: And who? He said: The oath-swearing merchant, or the oath-swearing seller, Yazid said: I do not know which of the two he said. I said: O Abu Dharr, what is wealth? He said: What is morning does not reach evening, and what is evening does not reach morning. He said: I said: O Abu Dharr, what is with you and your brothers of Quraysh? He said: By Allah, I will neither seek their help for religion, nor ask them for world until I join Allah and His Messenger; by Allah, I will neither seek their help for religion, nor ask them for world until I join Allah and His Messenger. And these words, some of them have been narrated from Abu Dharr from more than one channel, and we do not know it narrated from him with this wording except from this channel. Nor did Mutarrif narrate from Abu Dharr except this hadith."
"3916","3909 - Narrated Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi, from Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from Sasaah ibn Muawiyah, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There are no two Muslims between whom three children who have not reached puberty die except that Allah admits them both to Paradise by virtue of His mercy toward them. He said: I said: Tell me, may Allah have mercy on you. He said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever spends two pairs of his wealth in the path of Allah, the gatekeepers of Paradise hasten to meet him. He said: So I said: What are two pairs of his wealth? He said: Two slaves from his slaves, and two horses, and two camels. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith has been narrated with this wording and the like by a group from al-Hasan, among them Yunus, Habib, Humayd, Ashath, Abu Hurrah, al-Mufaddal ibn Lahiq and Jarir ibn Hazim."
"3917","3910 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Thabit, from Abu Hashim al-Mughirah ibn Salamah, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Yunus and Habib ibn al-Shahid and Humayd, from al-Hasan, from Sasaah, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning as the previous hadith."
"3918","3911 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Azraq, from Abu Qatan Amr ibn al-Haytham, from Abu Hurrah whose name is Wasil ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Hasan, from Sasaah, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3919","3912 - And narrated Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad, from his father, from al-Mufaddal ibn Lahiq, from al-Hasan, from Sasah, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3920","3913 - And narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Asim, from Jarir ibn Hazim, from al-Hasan, from Sasaah, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). All of these narrated something like the hadith of Yunus from al-Hasan from Sasaah ibn Muawiyah from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3921","3914 - And narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abd al-Wahhab, from Anbasah, and he is Anbasah ibn Abi Raitah, from al-Hasan, from Sasaah, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to the hadith of Bishr from Yunus. As for the hadith of Humayd and Habib, we know of no one who narrated them except Hammad ibn Salamah; as for the hadith of al-Mufaddal ibn Lahiq, we know of no one who narrated it from him except Abd al-Samad, and we do not recall that al-Mufaddal narrated with a chain from al-Hasan any hadith other than this one; as for the hadith of Ashath, Quraysh ibn Anas narrated it; as for the hadith of Anbasah, we know of no one who narrated it except Abd al-Wahhab."
"3922","3915 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Ala and Azhar ibn Jamil, from al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman, who said: I read to al-Fudayl ibn Maysarah, from Abu Hariz, from al-Hasan, from Sasaah, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever frees a believing slave, for every limb of his a limb of his will be ransomed or protected from the Fire. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated these words from Abu Dharr except through the hadith of Sasaah, and no one narrated it from al-Hasan except Abu Hariz."
"3923","Report of Abu al-Aswad al-Dayli from him. 3916 - Narrated Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Wasil the freed slave of Abu Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Uqayl, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The deeds of my nation were shown to me, the good of them and the evil of them, and I saw among the good of their deeds the harmful thing removed from the road, and I found among the evil of their deeds the spit in the mosque that is not buried. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except through this channel with this chain."
"3924","3917 - Narrated Yahya ibn Habib ibn Arabi, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Wasil, from Yahya ibn Uqayl, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: They said: O Messenger of Allah, the wealthy have taken all the rewards; they pray as we pray and fast as we fast and give charity from the excess of their wealth. He said: Has Allah not made for you that from which you may give charity? Every glorification is charity, every praise is charity, every magnification is charity, every declaration of oneness is charity, enjoining good is charity, forbidding evil is charity, and in the intercourse of one of you with his wife is charity. They said: O Prophet of Allah, one of us fulfills his desire and there is reward for him in it? He said: What do you think, if he placed it in what is unlawful, would there be sin upon him for it? Likewise, if he places it in what is lawful, there is reward for him in it. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this channel with this chain; and this hadith in my view is narrated in two places from Abu al-Aswad from Abu Dharr, and in one place from Yahya ibn Yamar from Abu Dharr with no Abu al-Aswad between them."
"3925","3918 - And narrated to us Fitr ibn Hammad, from Mahdi ibn Maymun, from Wasil, from Yahya ibn Uqayl, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"3926","3919 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abd al-Samad, from his father, from Husayn, meaning al-Muallim, from Ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, that Abu al-Aswad narrated to him from Abu Dharr, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: No man accuses another man of immorality or accuses him of disbelief except that it returns upon him if his companion is not so. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except through this channel with this chain."
"3927","3920 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abd al-Samad, from his father, from al-Husayn, meaning al-Muallim, from Ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, that Abu al-Aswad al-Duali narrated to him that Abu Dharr narrated to him, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he wore a white garment and he was sleeping; then I came to him while he was sleeping, then I came to him after he had woken, and I sat with him, and he said: There is no servant who says: There is no god but Allah, then dies upon that, except that he enters Paradise. I said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals? He said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals. I said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals? And he said on the fourth time: Despite the nose of Abu Dharr. So Abu Dharr went out saying: Even though the nose of Abu Dharr is rubbed in dust. And Abu Dharr used to narrate this hadith along with: even though the nose of Abu Dharr is rubbed in dust. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Abu Dharr through more than one channel, but we know of no narration of it from Abu Dharr through the hadith of Abu al-Aswad from him except through this channel; and Abu al-Aswad, his name is Zalim ibn Amr ibn Sufyan, a man of Basra, well known."
"3928","3921 - Narrated Amr ibn Ali, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Ajlah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dayli, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning as the previous hadith."
"3929","3922 - And narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Abdullah ibn Idris and Abu Usamah, from al-Ajlah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The best of what grey hair is changed with is henna and katam. And Yahya said: katam and henna."
"3930","Report of Muwarriq al-Ijli from Abu Dharr. 3923 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Muwarriq al-Ijli, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever among your slaves suits you, feed them from what you eat and clothe them from what you wear, and whoever does not suit you, sell them and do not torment the creation of Allah. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Abu Dharr through more than one channel with different wordings, and we have mentioned each hadith with its chain and wording in its place."
"3931","3924 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa and al-Husayn ibn Mahdi, from Ubaydullah ibn Musa, from Israil; 3925 - and narrated to us Amr ibn Ali, from Abu Ahmad, from Israil, both mentioning it from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Muwarriq, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I see what you do not see and hear what you do not hear; the heaven has groaned, for there is no space of four fingers in it except that an angel has placed his forehead prostrating to Allah; and if you knew what I know you would laugh little and weep much. He said: And I wish I were a tree to be cut down. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except through this channel, and we know of no other path for it; and we know of no narration by Mujahid from Muwarriq from Abu Dharr except these two hadiths. Ahmad said: I think these last words, I wish I were a tree to be cut down, are the words of Abu Dharr."
"3932","Hadith of Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Report of Said ibn Abi al-Hasan from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3926 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Affan ibn Muslim, from Hammam, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, that he was with Abu Dharr when his stipend came out, and with him was a slave girl of his attending to his needs, and some pieces remained from it, so he ordered her to buy small coins with it. I said: If only you had saved it for a need that befalls you or a guest who comes to you. He said: My close friend enjoined upon me that any gold or silver that is tied up, or either of them, is a burning coal upon its owner until he spends it in the path of Allah. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Qatadah from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan with this chain except Hammam, and more than one narrated it from Hammam."
"3933","3927 - Narrated al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, in a raised report, saying: The black dog, the donkey and the woman cut off the prayer. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan except Ubaydullah ibn Abi Humayd."
"3934","Report of al-Mushaath ibn Tarif from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3928 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from al-Mushaath ibn Tarif, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: How will you act when hardship reaches you such that a man cannot rise to his bed from hardship? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Remain chaste. He said: O Abu Dharr, what will you do when death becomes so frequent that a grave is sold for a slave? He said: I said to Abu Imran: What is meant by the house? He said: The grave. I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Then be patient. He said: O Abu Dharr, I think he said: How will you act when killing becomes so frequent that the stones of the oil-press are drowned in blood? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Sit in your house. He said: I said: If I am not left alone? He said: Join those to whom you belong. I said: Should I carry my weapon with me? He said: Then you would have shared with them. He said: If you fear that the gleam of the sword dazzles you, take the edge of your garment and cast it over your face; he will bear your sin and his sin. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words with this wording except from Abu Dharr; and none narrates this hadith from Abu Imran from Abdullah ibn al-Samit except Hammad ibn Zayd, for he mentioned al-Mushaath ibn Tarif between Abu Imran and Abdullah ibn al-Samit."
"3935","Report of Humayd ibn Hilal from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. Khalid al-Hadhdha from Humayd. 3929 - Narrated Abu Kamil, from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Zamzam is a satisfying food and a healing for illness. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Khalid al-Hadhdha except Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar."
"3936","3930 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Wasiti, from Ali ibn Asim, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The prayer of a man is cut off, if there is not before him something like the back of a saddle, by the donkey, the woman and the black dog. He said: I said: What is it with the black dog as distinct from the white or the red? He said: O son of my brother, I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as you asked me, and he said: The black dog is a devil. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Khalid al-Hadhdha with this chain except Ali ibn Asim."
"3937","3931 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mufaddal al-Harrani, from Umar ibn Habib, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Did you see your Lord? He said: Light; how could I see Him? Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Khalid al-Hadhdha except Umar ibn Habib, and he was a Basran judge from Banu Adi."
"3938","3932 - And narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abd al-Ala, from Said, from Qatadah or Matar al-Warraq, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, with the same meaning as the previous hadith about the prayer being cut off."
"3939","3933 - And narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Shubbuwayh al-Marwazi, from Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Missisi, from Abdullah ibn Shawdhab, from Matar, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3940","3934 - And narrated Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Aqil, from his grandfather Ubayd ibn Aqil, from Hisham, the author of al-Dastuwai, from Qatadah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3941","3935 - And narrated Amr ibn Ali, from Asim ibn Hilal, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3942","3936 - And narrated al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Uruzzi, from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, from Hammad, meaning Ibn Salamah, from Ayyub and Yunus ibn Ubayd and Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3943","3937 - And narrated Ali ibn Muslim al-Tusi, from Hushaym, from Yunus, meaning Ibn Ubayd, and Mansur, meaning Ibn Zadhdhan, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3944","3938 - And narrated Abdullah ibn Ishaq al-Attar, from Abu Asim, from Hisham, meaning Ibn Hassan, and Ashath ibn Abd al-Malik, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3945","3939 - And narrated Ziyad ibn Yahya al-Hassani, from Sahl ibn Aslam al-Adawi, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3946","3940 - And narrated Ahmad ibn Abdah, from Ziyad ibn Abdullah, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3947","3941 - And narrated Amr ibn Ali, Azhar ibn Jamil, Ziyad ibn Yahya and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, from al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman, from Salm ibn Abi al-Dhayyal, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3948","3942 - Narrated Wahb ibn Yahya ibn Zimam al-Qaysi, from Isa ibn Shuayb, from al-Hasan ibn Dhakwan, al-Hasan ibn Dinar and Ashath, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), their reports being close together, that he said: The prayer of a man is cut off, if there is not before him something like the back of a saddle, by the donkey, the woman and the black dog. He said: I said: O Abu Dharr, what is it with the black dog as distinct from the white or the red? He said: O son of my brother, I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as you asked me, and he said: The black dog is a devil. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Humayd ibn Hilal from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr by a group besides those we named, among them Shubah, Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, Abu Hilal and Ismail ibn Muslim."
"3949","3943 - As for the hadith of Shubah, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Dawud and Wahb ibn Jarir, from Shubah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3950","3944 - Narrated Ali ibn Qurrah ibn Habib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Umar al-Muzani, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning."
"3951","3945 - And narrated Abu Burayd Amr ibn Yazid al-Jarmi, from Salim ibn Nuh, from Umar ibn Amir, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The prayer is cut off by the black dog, the donkey and the woman. He said: So I said to Abu Dharr: What is it with the black dog as distinct from the yellow or the white? He said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as you asked me, and he said: The black dog is a devil."
"3952","Report of Ibn Awn from Humayd ibn Hilal from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3946 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Ibn Abi Adi, from Ibn Awn, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, who said: He said to me: O son of my brother, I prayed before I met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for three years. He said: I said: Where did you turn? He said: I turned wherever Allah turned me. I would stand at night as long as Allah willed, and when it was the end of the night I cast myself down as though I were a covering. And we were with our maternal uncle, when a man said to him: Unays visits your family in your absence. He said: So he said to him: My brother Unays, O uncle, as for the kindness you did, by Allah you have spoiled it, and we shall not live with you in a land where you are. He said: And we were with our mother among our camels, when my brother Unays contended with a man over our camels and they went for judgment to a soothsayer, and Unays kept praising him until he overcame him, so he took his camels and joined them to ours. And my brother Unays went to Mecca and said: I have seen there a man who resembles you most, called the Sabian. He said: I said: Until I go and look. He said: So I came to Mecca and approached a man and said: Where is this one called the Sabian? He said: So he raised his voice and said: Sabian, Sabian. He said: So I was pelted until I was left as though I were such-and-such - a word Ibn Abi Adi mentioned - then I went and stayed between Mecca and its outskirts. One night I went out and saw two women circumambulating, calling upon Yasaf and Nailah. He said: I said: Marry one of them to the other. So they said: Sabian, Sabian. He said: I said: I am a foul thing like a stick in a foul thing, except that I do not use euphemism. He said: So they went off and met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Abu Bakr approaching from the lower part of Mecca, and they said: This is a Sabian between the Kabah and its coverings. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, circumambulated the House and prayed two cycles behind the Station. He said: So I came to him, and I was the first of the people to greet him with the greeting of Islam. He said: I said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And upon you, who are you? I said: I am from Banu Ghifar. He said: So he placed his hand thus upon his face. He said: I said: He disliked the people to whom I had ascribed myself, so I went to speak by his hand. He said: So his companion gestured with his hand below my hand, and he knew better than I. He said: So he raised his hand and said: How long have you been here? He said: I said: For fifteen days. He said: And what was your food? I said: The drink of Zamzam, and I have not felt the slightness of hunger upon my liver, and the folds of my belly have broken. He said: It is indeed a satisfying food and a healing for illness. He said: So Abu Bakr said: Let me enjoy the hospitality of tonight. He said: So he took me to a house in the lower part of Mecca and scooped for me handfuls of raisins. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: A land with palm trees has been mentioned to me, so when it reaches you that we have come to it, come to us. He said: So I returned to my family, and Unays said: What did you do? I said: I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and accepted Islam. He said: I have no aversion to your religion, so my brother accepted Islam, and my mother said: I have no aversion to the religion of you two, so she accepted Islam, and people of our tribe accepted Islam, and the other half said: Until we meet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we shall stipulate for ourselves."
"3953","3947 - And narrated to us al-Walid from Amr ibn Sukayn, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, from Ibn Awn, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, similar to it."
"3954","3948 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Dawud, from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We went out with our people Ghifar, who used to declare the sacred month lawful, so I went out with my brother Unays and our mother until we came to a maternal uncle of ours possessing wealth and standing, and our uncle treated us well, but his people envied us and said: When you go out, Unays visits your family. So our uncle repeated to us what had been said to him. So we said to him: As for you, you have spoiled your past kindness, and there shall be no gathering between us hereafter. Our camels carried us forth, and our uncle covered himself with his cloak weeping. So we went until we were in a valley near Mecca, when Unays contended over our camels and went to a soothsayer, and brought us our camels and the like of them with them. And, O son of my brother, I had prayed before I met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for three years. He said: I said: To whom? He said: To Allah. I said: Where did you turn? He said: Wherever Allah turned me; I would pray the night prayer until, when it was the time before dawn, I cast myself down as though I were a covering until the sun rose over me. Then Unays said to me: I am going to Mecca, so take my place until I come to you. So he went and was long in coming to me, then he came. So I said: What kept you? He said: I met in Mecca a man upon your religion who claims that Allah sent him. He said: I said: And what do the people say of him? He said: They say: A poet, a soothsayer. And I have heard the speech of soothsayers, and it is not his speech; and I have placed his speech upon the measures of poetry and it does not fit upon anyones tongue. Abu Dharr said: O son of my brother, and Unays was one of the poets. He said: By Allah, he is truthful. He said: I said: Then take my place until I visit Mecca. He said: Yes, but beware of the people of Mecca, for they have become hostile toward him. He said: So I went and examined a man among them and said: Where are those who call him the Sabian? He said: So he pointed at me and said: The Sabian, the Sabian. He said: So the people of the valley turned upon me with every stone and bone, and I fell unconscious. When I rose, it was as though I were an idol. So I came to Zamzam, washed the blood from me and drank of its water, and I stayed, O son of my brother, thirty days and nights with no food or drink except Zamzam; and I grew fat until the folds of my belly broke, and I did not feel the slightness of hunger upon my liver. He said: While I was one night on a bright moonlit night, when Allah had put the people of Mecca to sleep so that none of them circumambulated the House except two women calling upon Yasaf and Nailah, I put out my head and said: Marry one of them to the other. By Allah, that did not restrain them. Then they passed by me calling upon Yasaf and Nailah, so I said: A foul thing like wood, except that I do not use euphemism. So they went off wailing, saying: If only some of our supporters were here. And the first to meet them was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Abu Bakr, descending from the mountain. So they said: The Sabian is between the Kabah and its coverings. He said: What did he say to you? They said: He said to us a word that fills the mouth. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, began with the Stone and touched it, then circumambulated the House seven times. So I came to him with his companion, and I was the first to greet him with the greeting of Islam. He said: And upon you and the mercy of Allah, who are you? I said: A man from Banu Ghifar. He said: So he placed his hand upon his forehead, and I said to myself: He disliked that I ascribed myself to Ghifar, so I went to lift his hand from his forehead, but his companion prevented me, and he knew him better than I. He said: How long have you been here? I said: For thirty days and nights. He said: And what was your food and drink? I said: Zamzam; it has made me fat until the folds of my belly broke, and I do not feel hunger. He said: It is blessed; it is a satisfying food and a healing for illness. Then Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me to host him tonight. So he permitted him, and Abu Bakr took me and hosted me, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A land with palm trees has been mentioned to me between two lava tracts; if it reaches you that we have come to it, then come to us. I said: I had no food except Zamzam water, and I grew fat until the folds of my belly broke, and I found no slightness of hunger upon my liver. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is blessed; it is a satisfying food. Then Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, gift me with his food tonight. He said: Yes. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went, Abu Bakr went, and I went with them; and he opened a door for us and scooped for us of Taif raisins - I think he said: a handful - and that was the first food I ate there. He said: I remained as long as I remained, then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been directed to a land of palms, I think it is none but Yathrib; will you convey from me to your people, that Allah may benefit them through you? He said: So I went until I came to my brother Unays, and he said to me: What did you do? He said: I have accepted Islam and believed. Then he said to me: I have no aversion to your religion; I have accepted Islam and believed. Then we came to our mother and presented Islam to her, and she said: I have no aversion to the religion of you two; I have accepted Islam and believed. Then we traveled until we came to our people Ghifar and presented Islam to them; half of them accepted Islam, and the remaining half said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) comes to Medina, we shall accept Islam. Ima - meaning Ibn Rahdah al-Ghifari - used to lead them, and he was their chief. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Medina, the rest of them accepted Islam. Then our brothers of Aslam came and said: O Messenger of Allah, we accept upon what Ghifar accepted. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ghifar, may Allah forgive her; and Aslam, may Allah grant her peace."
"3955","3949 - And narrated to us Yahya ibn Mualla ibn Mansur, who said: I found in my fathers book in his handwriting, and he brought out to me a book which he mentioned was his fathers, from Uththam ibn Ali, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Nadr, meaning Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, similar to the hadith of Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah concerning his acceptance of Islam, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the story at length. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith, as the hadith of al-Amash with this chain, we heard only from Yahya ibn Mualla, from his father, from Uththam."
"3956","3950 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ali ibn Yazid al-Sudai, from Yaqub ibn Ishaq, from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to the previous story of his acceptance of Islam."
"3957","3951 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Amir and Hibban, from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: O Messenger of Allah, a man loves people but cannot act as they act. He said: You, O Abu Dharr, are with those whom you love. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a better chain than this."
"3958","Report of Abu al-Aliyah al-Bara from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3952 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Qabisah ibn Uqbah, from Sufyan, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Aliyah, meaning al-Bara, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I asked my close friend, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he struck my thigh and said: Pray the prayer at its appointed time, and if you catch them, pray with them, and do not say: I have already prayed so I shall not pray with them."
"3959","3953 - And narrated to us al-Husayn ibn Mahdi, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How will you act when you remain among people who delay the prayer - I think he said: So what do you command me? - He said: Pray the prayer at its time, then if you are in the mosque when the prayer is established, pray with them."
"3960","3954 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Budayl, meaning Ibn Maysarah, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck his thigh, then said: How will you act when you remain among people who delay the prayer? He said: Pray the prayer at its time; if you are in the mosque when the prayer is established, pray with them. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration by Abu al-Aliyah al-Bara from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr other than this hadith."
"3961","Report of Abu Imran al-Jawni from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3955 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abd al-Samad, from Shubah, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, a man does a deed for himself and people love him for it. He said: That is the prompt glad tidings of the believer."
"3962","3956 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, a man does a deed for himself and people love him for it. He said: That is the prompt glad tidings of the believer. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated these words from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Dharr."
"3963","3957 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Wahb ibn Jarir, from Shubah, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: My close friend (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me three things: to hear and obey even a slave with mutilated limbs, when I make broth to increase its water then look to some of my neighbors and give them of it, or a word like it, and to pray the prayer at its time; if you catch the imam having prayed, your prayer has sufficed you, otherwise it is voluntary for you."
"3964","3958 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Asim al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad, from Salih ibn Rustum, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning as the previous counsels."
"3965","3959 - And narrated to us Abu Musa, from Marhum ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: How will you act when hardship reaches you such that a man cannot rise to his bed from hardship? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Remain chaste. He said: O Abu Dharr, how will you act when death becomes so frequent that a grave is sold for a slave? He said: I said to Abu Imran: What is meant by the house? He said: The grave. He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Then be patient. He said: O Abu Dharr, how will you act when killing becomes so frequent that the stones of the oil-press are drowned in blood? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Sit in your house. I said: If I am not left alone? He said: Join those to whom you belong. I said: Should I carry my weapon with me? He said: Then you would have shared with the people. He said: I said: Then what should I do? He said: If you fear that the gleam of the sword dazzles you, take the edge of your garment and cast it over your face; he will bear your sin and his sin. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated by a group from Abu Imran from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr, except Hammad ibn Zayd, who narrated it from Abu Imran from al-Mushaath ibn Tarif from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr."
"3966","3960 - Narrated Nasr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna - Nasr said: I was told, and Abu Musa said: Narrated Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what are the vessels of the Basin? He said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, its vessels are more numerous than the stars of the sky on a clear night; whoever drinks a drink from it shall never thirst; the last thing upon him, two spouts of Paradise pour into it; its width is like its length, between Amman and Ilah; its water is whiter than milk and sweeter than honey. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Abu Imran except Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad, and we know of no narration of it from Abu Dharr except through this channel."
"3967","3961 - Narrated Nasr ibn Ali, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you cook a pot, increase its broth and ladle out for your neighbors."
"3968","3962 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Uthman ibn Umar, from Abu Amir al-Khazzaz, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not belittle any good deed; if you find nothing, meet people with a cheerful face; and when you cook a pot, increase its water and ladle out for your neighbors. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith, Abu Amir added in it, from Abu Imran, over the rest of the companions of Abu Imran: Do not belittle any good deed, so it became as though a separate hadith."
"3969","3963 - Narrated al-Abbas ibn Yazid al-Bahrani, from Awbad ibn Abi Imran al-Jawni, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Visit at intervals and love will increase. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from Abu Dharr except through this channel, and we know of no one who narrated it from Abu Imran except his son Awbad; and Awbad was not strong, though the people of knowledge narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"3970","3964 - Narrated Abu Ubaydullah Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan, from Ishaq ibn Idris, from Awbad ibn Abi Imran al-Jawni, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which of the two terms did Musa fulfill? He said: The most complete and most dutiful of them. He said: And if you are asked which of the two women he married, say: The younger of them. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except with this chain."
"3971","Report of Qatadah from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3965 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr, from Muhammad ibn Bakkar ibn Bilal al-Dimashqi, from Said ibn Bashir, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sham is the land of gathering and resurrection. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except with this chain, except a man who narrated it but was not followed, narrating it from Muadh ibn Hisham, from his father, from Qatadah, from Said ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr."
"3972","Report of Budayl ibn Maysarah from Abdullah ibn al-Samit from Abu Dharr. 3966 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Harb al-Nashai, from Yahya ibn Abi Zakariyya al-Ghassani Abu Marwan, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Budayl ibn Maysarah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: My close friend (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me to join my kin even if they turn away, to speak the truth even if bitter, to let no blamer deter me concerning Allah, to look to those below me and not to those above me, to sit with the poor, and to say often: There is no power nor strength except through Allah. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration by Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Budayl ibn Maysarah except this hadith; and Budayl did not hear from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, though he was early."
"3973","Report of Abu Abdullah al-Anazi from Abdullah ibn al-Samit. 3967 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Ammar ibn Abd al-Jabbar, from Shubah, from Said al-Jurayri, from Abu Abdullah al-Anazi, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved speech to Allah is: Glory be to Allah and with His praise."
"3974","3968 - And narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdullah al-Sadusi, from Rawh, from Shubah, from al-Jurayri, from Abu Abdullah, from Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith with this wording from Abu Dharr except through this channel."
"3975","Report of Nuaym ibn Qanab from Abu Dharr. 3969 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Salim ibn Nuh, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala, from Nuaym ibn Qanab, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the wording mentioned in the next report."
"3976","3970 - And narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdullah al-Sadusi, from Rawh ibn Junadah, from Shubah, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala, from Nuaym ibn Qanab, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of a woman is like a rib; if you wish to straighten it you break it, and if you enjoy it, you enjoy it while it is crooked. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from Abu Dharr except through this channel; and we know of no one who narrated from Nuaym ibn Qanab except Abu al-Ala, and he is a man of Basra."
"3977","Report of Abdullah ibn Qudamah ibn Sakhr from Abu Dharr. 3971 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Muslim, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ali ibn Zayd, who said: Al-Hasan said to me: Ask Abdullah ibn Qudamah ibn Sakhr about this hadith, so I met him at the door of the governor's house and asked him, and he said: Abu Dharr claimed that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk, when they passed a valley, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: You are in a cursed valley, so hasten. So he rode his horse and urged it on, and the people urged on. Then he said: Whoever has kneaded his dough or cooked a pot, let him pour it out. Then we marched. Then he said: O people, there is today no living soul upon which a hundred years shall pass but Allah shall pay no regard to it. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except with this chain."
"3978","Report of Mihjan from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3972 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi, from Daylam ibn Ghazwan, from Wahb ibn Abi Dubayy, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Mihjan, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The evil eye indeed causes a man to climb a high place by Allah's permission, then to fall from it. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated these words from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Dharr, and we know of no path for it from Abu Dharr other than this path; and this Wahb ibn Abi Dubayy is a man of Basra from whom Daylam narrated hadiths."
"3979","Report of Amr ibn Bujdan from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3973 - Narrated Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan and Bishr ibn Muadh, from Yazid ibn Zuray, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Amr ibn Bujdan, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Pure earth is the ablution of the Muslim or the believer, even if he does not find water for ten years; when he finds water, let him fear Allah and let him touch his skin - or he said: his body - with water, for that is better."
"3980","3974 - And narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Qabisah ibn Uqbah, from Sufyan, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Amr ibn Mihjan or Mihjan, from Abu Dharr - Qabisah doubted - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from Abu Dharr except with this chain."
"3981","Report of Zayd ibn Wahb from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3975 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abu Muawiyah, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was walking with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the lava tract of Medina while we looked at Uhud, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: I would not love to have Uhud as gold with me while a third night passes and I have of it a dinar except a dinar I keep for a debt. Then he walked and said: O Abu Dharr. I said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The wealthy are the fewest on the Day of Resurrection except those who say with wealth thus and thus and thus. Then he walked and said: O Abu Dharr, stay as you are until I come to you. So he went and was hidden from me, and I heard clamor and a voice, and I said: Perhaps something has happened to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I was about to follow him, then I remembered his saying: Do not move until I come to you. So I waited for him until he came, and I mentioned to him what I had heard. He said: That was Jibril (peace be upon him) who came to me and said: Whoever of your nation dies not associating anything with Allah enters Paradise, even if he commits adultery and even if he steals."
"3982","3976 - And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"3983","3977 - And narrated to us Khallad ibn Aslam, from al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, from Shubah, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, al-Amash, Hammad and Abd al-Aziz, meaning Ibn Rufay, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to the hadith of Abu Muawiyah. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated by al-Amash, Habib ibn Abi Thabit, Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman and Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah narrated it from Zayd ibn Wahb from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3984","3978 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Ibn Abi Adi, from Shubah, from Habib, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril said to me: Whoever of your nation dies not associating anything with Allah enters Paradise or shall not enter the Fire. I said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals? He said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals."
"3985","3979 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abu Muawiyah, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr, raise your gaze and look at the highest man you see in the mosque? So I looked and saw a man wearing a fine garment, and I said: This one. He said: O Abu Dharr, look at the lowest man you see in the mosque? So I looked and saw a man wrapped in a garment, and I said: This one. He said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, this one is better with Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the earth full of the like of this one. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of the wording of this hadith from Abu Dharr except through this channel and another channel narrated by al-Amash from Ibrahim al-Taymi from his father from Abu Dharr; and the hadith of Zayd ibn Wahb is better known."
"3986","3980 - And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the shade of the Kabah saying: They are the losers, by the Lord of the Kabah. He said it twice. I said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, who are they? He said: The wealthiest, except those who say: thus and thus, and few are they. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, no one dies leaving camels, cattle or sheep without paying their due except that they shall come on the Day of Resurrection, the greatest and fattest they were, goring him with their horns and trampling him with their hooves; whenever the last of them passes, the first of them is returned upon him, until judgment is passed among the people. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith has been narrated from Abu Dharr through another channel."
"3987","3981 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I went out one night, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alone with no one with him. So I thought he disliked anyone being with him, and I walked in the shadow of the moon. He turned and saw me and said: Who is this? I said: Abu Dharr, may Allah make me ransom for you. He said: So I walked with him a while, and he said: The wealthy are the fewest except those who give, meaning, to their right and to their left and before them and behind them. So I walked with him a while, and he said: Sit here. And he stayed away from me long, and I heard him while he approached, repeating: even if he steals. So I said: May Allah make me ransom for you, with whom were you speaking at the side of the lava tract, for I heard no one return anything to you? He said: That was Jibril who met me at the side of the lava tract. He said: Give your nation glad tidings that whoever dies not associating anything with Allah enters Paradise. So I said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals? He said: Yes. I said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals? He said: Yes, even if spiteful. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated by al-Amash and others from Zayd ibn Wahb, and Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay added words in it, so we mentioned it because of his addition."
"3988","3982 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, who said: I heard Muhajir Abu al-Hasan reporting from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The intensity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell, so cool the prayer in intense heat, meaning the noon prayer. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except through this channel with this chain."
"3989","3983 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Jabbar, from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, from al-Harith ibn Hasīrah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Abu Dharr said: For me to swear repeatedly that Ibn Said is the Dajjal is dearer to me than to swear once that he is not. A child was born among the Jews, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to his mother asking her how long she carried him. So I asked her, and she said: Twelve months. So I came to him and told him. He said: Ask her about his cry when he fell to the ground. She said: A word that escaped me. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I have hidden for you something hidden, what is it? He said: Great is the affair of Afra and the smoke; when he wished to say: the smoke, he could not, so he said: the dukh. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be gone, you shall never precede the decree. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except with this chain."
"3990","3984 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, meaning Ibn Abd al-Hamid, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A man of the desert said: O Messenger of Allah, the hyena has consumed us. Then he returned and said: The hyena has consumed us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For other than the hyena I fear more for you: when the world is poured upon you in abundance, would that my nation not wear gold."
"3991","3985 - And narrated to us Ibn Mamar, from Abu Nuaym, from Sufyan, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"3992","3986 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from a man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to the hadith of Jarir. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words except from Abu Dharr, and we know of no path for it other than this path; and the hyena means the year of drought, so they complained to him of the severity of the years hardship."
"3993","Report of al-Marur ibn Suwayd from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3987 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib, from Waki, from al-Amash, from al-Marur, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know the last of the people of Paradise to enter and the last of the people of the Fire to come out of the Fire: a man shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection and it shall be said: Present to him his minor sins and conceal from him his major ones. So it shall be said to him: You did on such-and-such day such-and-such, and he admits without denying, fearful of the major ones. So it shall be said: Give him for every evil deed a good deed. Then he says: I have sins I do not see here. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laugh when he mentioned this hadith until his molars appeared. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith except from Abu Dharr with this wording, and we know of no other path for it from Abu Dharr."
"3994","3988 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib, from Waki, from al-Amash, from al-Marur, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, blessed and exalted, says: Whoever brings a good deed shall have ten like it or more, and whoever brings an evil deed shall have its like or I forgive; whoever draws near to Me a span, I draw near to him a cubit; whoever draws near to Me a cubit, I draw near to him a fathom; and whoever comes to Me with the earth full - I think he said - of sin after not associating anything with Me, I come to him with its full of forgiveness. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from al-Marur from Abu Dharr through more than one channel."
"3995","3989 - And narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Adami, from Muhammad ibn Muhabbab Abu Hammam al-Dallal, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Mansur, from Lahiq ibn Humayd, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"3996","3990 - Ibrahim said: And Mansur narrated to me, from Ribi, from al-Marur, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"3997","3991 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Kufi, from al-Khalil ibn Kurayz, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Abu Farwah, from Shimr ibn Atiyyah, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah, blessed and exalted, says: O My servants, whoever of you does a good deed, I reward it tenfold or more; whoever of you does an evil deed, I requite it with an evil deed or forgive; and whoever meets Me not associating anything with Me, I meet him with the earth full of forgiveness. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration by Shimr ibn Atiyyah from al-Marur other than this hadith; and this Muhammad ibn Jabir, his hadith has been borne."
"3998","3992 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, from Waki, from al-Amash, from al-Marur, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: There was speech between me and a man whose mother was non-Arab, so I taunted him through her. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr, you are a man in whom there is pre-Islamic ignorance. Do not taunt your brothers; Allah has placed them under your hands, so feed them from what you eat, clothe them from what you wear, and do not burden them - I think he said - with what they cannot bear; if you burden them, help them. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from al-Marur through other than this chain."
"3999","3993 - Narrated Ishaq, from Waki, from al-Amash, from al-Marur, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he sat in the shade of the Kabah. When he saw me he said: They are the losers, by the Lord of the Kabah. I said: Who are they? He said: The wealthiest, except those who say with wealth thus and thus and thus. And there is no owner of camels, cattle or sheep who does not pay their zakah except that they shall come on the Day of Resurrection, the greatest they were, goring him with their horns; whenever the last of them passes, the first of them returns upon him, until judgment is passed among the people."
"4000","3994 - And narrated to us Amr ibn Ali, from Umar ibn Ali, from Musa ibn al-Musayyab, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning as the previous divine hadith."
"4001","3995 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Muhammad ibn Sabiq, from al-Muharibi, from Musa ibn al-Musayyab, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah, blessed and exalted, says: O My servants, all of you are sinful except those I protect, so seek My forgiveness and I forgive you; all of you are astray except those I guide, so ask Me and I guide you; all of you are poor except those I enrich, so ask Me and I provide for you. And if the first of you and the last of you, your living and your dead, your moist and your dry, gathered upon the most pious heart of a servant of Mine, they would not increase My dominion by a gnats wing. And if the first of you and the last of you, your living and your dead, your moist and your dry, gathered and every person asked what his wish attains, I would give every asker what he asked, and it would not decrease except as when one of you passes by the shore of the sea and dips a needle in it then removes it. That is because I am Generous, Glorious, Bountiful; My giving is a word and My punishment is a word; when I intend a thing I only say to it: Be, and it is."
"4002","3996 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd, who said: I saw Abu Dharr wearing a fine garment with his slave wearing its like, so I asked him about that. He mentioned that he had reviled a man in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and taunted him through his mother. So the man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are a man in whom there is pre-Islamic ignorance. Your brothers and servants Allah has placed under your hands; whoever has his brother under his hand, let him feed him from what he eats and clothe him from what he wears, and do not burden them with what overcomes them; if you burden them, help them with it."
"4003","3997 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Wasil, from al-Marur, who said: I heard Abu Dharr narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Jibril came to me and gave me glad tidings that whoever of your nation dies not associating anything with Allah enters Paradise. I said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals? He said: Even if he commits adultery and even if he steals."
"4004","3998 - And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, from Ubaydullah ibn Musa, from Mahdi ibn Maymun, from Wasil, from al-Marur, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4005","3999 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Wasil, from al-Marur, who said: I heard Abu Dharr saying: Allah, blessed and exalted, said: If a servant filled the earth with sins then did not associate anything with Me, I would forgive him the earth full of sins or its fill. And if he intends a good deed but does not do it, a good deed is written for him; if he does it, ten good deeds are written for him. And if he intends an evil deed but does not do it, nothing is written; if he does it, one evil deed is written. And if he draws near to Me a span, I draw near to him a cubit; if he draws near to Me a cubit, I draw near to him a fathom; and if he comes to Me walking, I come to him running."
"4006","4000 - And narrated to us Khalid ibn Yusuf, from Abu Awanah, from Asim, from al-Marur ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4007","Report of Yazid ibn Sharik from Abu Dharr. 4001 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Salamah ibn al-Fadl, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from Yazid ibn Sharik, who said: We entered upon Abu Dharr and said: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform tamattu while you were with him? He said: What are you and that? That was something particular to us, meaning mutah."
"4008","4002 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Ubaydullah ibn Musa, from Israil, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father and al-Harith ibn Suwayd, who said: Abu Dharr said: Mutah was a concession which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted us, or which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was granted."
"4009","4003 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Ibn Abi Adi, from Shubah, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, who said: Mutah was a concession for us, meaning the mutah of hajj."
"4010","4004 - And I think Nasr ibn Ali narrated from Abu Ahmad, from Sufyan, from al-Amash and Ayyash al-Amiri, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, who said: The mutah of hajj was a concession for us."
"4011","4005 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Uthman ibn Hakim, from Abu Ghassan, from Qays, from Abu Husayn, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, who said: Mutah was a concession for us. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Abu Husayn except Qays, and Abu Dawud and Abu Ghassan narrated it from Qays."
"4012","4006 - Narrated Zayd ibn Akhzam al-Tai, from Said ibn Amir, from Shubah, from Abd al-Akram, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, who said: Mutah was particular to us. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Shubah from Abd al-Akram except Said ibn Amir."
"4013","4007 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, who said: I heard Abd al-Warith ibn Abi Hanifah, who said: I heard Ibrahim al-Taymi narrating from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said concerning the mutah of the pilgrim: It is not for you, nor are you part of it; it was only a concession for us, the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4014","4008 - Narrated Bishr ibn Khalid al-Askari, from Said ibn Maslamah, from Habib ibn Hassan, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Mutah was particular to us. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Habib ibn Hassan except Said ibn Maslamah."
"4015","4009 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Mukharrimi, from Yahya ibn Adam, from al-Mufaddal, meaning Ibn Muhalhil, from Bayan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Shatha, who said: I was with Ibrahim al-Nakhai and Ibrahim al-Taymi, and I said: I have intended to combine hajj and umrah this year. So Ibrahim al-Nakhai said: Had your father been alive he would not have intended that. And Ibrahim al-Taymi said, from his father, from Abu Dharr: Mutah was only a concession for us. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Bayan except al-Mufaddal ibn Muhalhil; and Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Shatha ascribes no hadith with a chain except this hadith."
"4016","4010 - Narrated Amr ibn Ali, from Yazid ibn Harun, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was upon a donkey, with the sun at its setting, and he said: O Abu Dharr, do you know where this sun sets? So I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: It sets in a muddy spring, then goes until it falls prostrating to its Lord beneath the Throne; when it is to emerge, Allah permits it; and when Allah intends to bring it forth from its setting place, He withholds it, and it says: O Lord, my journey is far. So He says: Rise from where you set. That is when no soul shall benefit from its faith that had not believed before or earned good in its faith. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah from Ibrahim from his father from Abu Dharr except Sufyan ibn Husayn; and Yunus ibn Ubayd, Sulayman al-Amash and Harun ibn Sad narrated it from Ibrahim al-Taymi."
"4017","4011 - As for the hadith of Yunus, narrated to us Muammal ibn Hisham, from Ismail ibn Ibrahim, from Yunus, meaning Ibn Ubayd, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the sun sets it comes beneath the Throne and prostrates, and it is said to it: Rise from where you used to rise. And when the night comes, it seeks permission, and it is said to it: Rise from where you set. Then he recited this verse: Do they await but that the angels come to them, or your Lord comes, or some of the signs of your Lord come; the day some of the signs of your Lord come, no soul shall benefit from its faith that had not believed before or earned good in its faith. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated by Ibn Ulayyah from Yunus from Ibrahim al-Taymi from his father from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4018","4012 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Rawh ibn Ubadah, from Hammad, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, who said: Ibrahim ibn Yazid al-Taymi narrated to me, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it; but he did not say: from Ibrahim al-Taymi from his father, rather he left it disconnected."
"4019","4013 - As for the hadith of Harun ibn Sad, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Muammal ibn al-Sabbah and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn bint Hajjaj al-Sawwaf, from al-Hakam ibn Marwan, from Abu Maryam, from Harun ibn Sad, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to the hadith of al-Hakam from Ibrahim al-Taymi from his father from Abu Dharr."
"4020","4014 - As for the hadith of al-Amash, narrated to us Amr ibn Ali, from Abu Muawiyah, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque when the sun had set, and he said: O Abu Dharr, do you know where the sun goes? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: It goes until it prostrates, then seeks permission of its Lord to return, and permission is granted to it; and it is as though it has been told: Return from where you came. So it returns to its setting place. That is His saying: And the sun runs to its resting place."
"4021","4015 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Ibn Abi Adi, from Shubah, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which mosque was placed first? He said: The Sacred Mosque, then the Furthest Mosque. I said: How long was between them? He said: Forty years. So wherever the prayer reaches you, pray, for that is a mosque. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrates these words from the Prophet (peace be upon him) except Abu Dharr, and we know of no path for it from Abu Dharr except through the path of al-Amash; and more than one narrated it from al-Amash."
"4022","4016 - Narrated Salm ibn Junadah ibn Salm, from Waki, in the house, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from his father, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning as the previous hadith."
"4023","4017 - And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, from Ahmad ibn Yunus, from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever builds for Allah a mosque, even the size of a sand-grouses nest, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated it from Sufyan raised except Salm ibn Junadah from Waki, and we know of no follow-up for Salm ibn Junadah upon this hadith. This hadith is only known raised from the hadith of Ahmad ibn Yunus from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash; and Yahya ibn Adam narrated it from Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz."
"4024","4018 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar, from Yunus ibn Bukayr, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Raise your gaze and look at the highest man you see in the mosque? So I looked and there was a man wearing a fine garment, and I said: This one. He said: Look at the lowest man you see in the mosque? So I looked and there was a man wrapped up, and I said: This one. He said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, this one is better with Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the earth full of the like of this one. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated by Abu Muawiyah from al-Amash from Zayd ibn Wahb from Abu Dharr; and we know of no follow-up for Yunus ibn Bukayr upon this narration of his from al-Amash from Ibrahim al-Taymi."
"4025","4019 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Jabbar, from Yunus ibn Bukayr, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Abu Dharr, with the same meaning as the previous hadith."
"4026","Report of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla from Abu Dharr. 4020 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Yahya ibn Hammad, from Abu Awanah, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Shall I guide you to a treasure of the treasures of Paradise? I said: Yes. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Abu Dharr through more than one channel; Yala ibn Ubayd narrated it from al-Amash from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm from Abu Dharr, so Abu Awanah and others differed in this narration."
"4027","4021 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Asim, from Sufyan, from Ibn Abi Layla, from his brother Isa, from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about wiping the pebbles - meaning in prayer. He said: A single wiping. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith from Abu Dharr except from the hadith of Ibn Abi Layla from him."
"4028","Report of Maymun ibn Abi Shabib from Abu Dharr. 4022 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Bashshar, from Abd al-Rahman, from Sufyan, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Fear Allah wherever you are, follow the evil deed with a good deed and it wipes it out, and treat people with good character."
"4029","Report of Kharashah ibn al-Hurr from Abu Dharr. 4023 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, from al-Amash, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah shall not speak to on the Day of Resurrection, nor look at them: an aged adulterer, a lying king, and a poor arrogant one."
"4030","4024 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Ali ibn Mudrik, from Abu Zurah, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three Allah shall not speak to on the Day of Resurrection, nor look at them, nor purify them, and theirs is a painful punishment. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it three times. Then Abu Dharr said: Who are they? They have failed and lost; they have failed and lost; they have failed and lost - three times. He said: The one who lets his garment trail, the one who reminds of favors, and the one who sells his goods with a false oath. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no ascribed hadith narrated by Kharashah from Abu Dharr except these two hadiths."
"4031","Report of Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir from Abu Dharr. 4025 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, from Abu Farwah al-Hamdani, from Abu Zurah ibn Amr ibn Jarir, from Abu Dharr and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to sit among his companions, and a stranger would come not knowing which of them he was. So we spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about making for him something by which a stranger would recognize him when he came. So we built for him a bench of clay, and he used to sit upon it - I think he said - and we sat around him. While we sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his gathering, a man approached, of the most handsome of people in face, the sweetest of people in scent, the cleanest of people in garment, as though his clothes had never been touched by dirt. He greeted from the edge of the mat and said: Peace be upon you, O Muhammad. He said: And upon you peace. He said: May I come near, O Muhammad? He said: Come near. He kept saying: May I come near, O Muhammad? And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying to him, until he placed his hand upon the knee of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said: O Muhammad, what is Islam? He said: To worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, to establish the prayer, to give the zakah, to perform pilgrimage to the House, and to fast Ramadan. He said: If I do this, have I submitted? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth. When we heard the man say to the Messenger of Allah: You have spoken the truth, we disapproved of it. Then he said: Tell me, what is faith? He said: Faith in Allah, the angels, the Book, the prophets, and belief in the decree, all of it. He said: If I do that, have I believed? He said: Yes. He said: You have spoken the truth. He said: O Muhammad, tell me, what is excellence? He said: To worship Allah as though you see Him, for if you do not see Him, He sees you. He said: You have spoken the truth. He said: Tell me, O Muhammad, when is the Hour? He did not answer him. Then he repeated, and he did not answer him again. Then he repeated, and he did not answer him. Then he raised his head and swore to him by Allah - or he said: By the One who sent Muhammad with guidance and the religion of truth, the one asked knows no more than the asker; but it has signs: when you see the herders of sheep competing in building, and see the barefoot naked as kings of the earth, and see a woman give birth to her mistress - among five none knows but Allah: Indeed Allah has knowledge of the Hour, sends down the rain, knows what is in the wombs; no soul knows what it shall earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it shall die; indeed Allah is Knowing, Aware. Then he rose to the sky. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One who sent Muhammad with guidance, I knew no more of him than any man among you; he was Jibril (peace be upon him), in the form of Dihyah al-Kalbi. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from Abu Dharr except through this channel with this chain, except a weak chain narrated by al-Sari ibn Ismail, in which he confused the chain. And this hadith, we know of no one who narrated it from Abu Farwah with this chain except Jarir."
"4032","4026 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, from al-Mughirah, from al-Harith, meaning al-Ukli, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Hurayrah; and Ibn Abi Shubrumah and Umarah, meaning Ibn al-Qaqa, from Abu Zurah, from Abu Dharr or Abu Hurayrah, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which people are most deserving of my good companionship? He said: Your mother, then your mother, then your mother. I said: Then whom? He said: Then your father. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Abu Hurayrah, and we know of no one who said: from Abu Dharr, other than those we mentioned; and the correct in my view is from Abu Hurayrah. And the hadith of al-Mughirah from al-Harith al-Ukli from Abu Zurah from Abu Hurayrah, we know of no one who narrated it except Jarir."
"4033","Report of Zayd ibn Zabyan from Abu Dharr. 4027 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Shubah, from Mansur, who said: I heard Ribi narrating from Zayd ibn Zabyan, raised to Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Three Allah loves, and three Allah hates. As for those Allah loves: a man who comes to people and asks them by Allah, not asking them by kinship between him and them, and they refuse him, then a man lags behind them and gives him secretly, none knowing of his gift except Allah and the one he gave; and people who travel their night until, when sleep is dearer to them than anything set beside it, they alight and lay their heads, then one stands cajoling Me and reciting My verses; and a man in a detachment who meets the enemy and they are routed, then he turns with his chest until he is killed or victory is granted him. And the three Allah hates: the aged adulterer, the poor conceited one, and the unjust rich one."
"4034","4028 - And narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, from Shayban - meaning Ibn Abd al-Rahman - from Mansur, from Ribi, from Zayd ibn Zabyan, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Three Allah loves, and three Allah hates. Allah loves a man among people to whom a beggar comes asking them by the face of Allah, not asking them by kinship between him and them, and they turn away from him, and he lags behind them where none sees him except Allah and the one he gave; and He loves a man in a squadron that is broken, who charges fighting until Allah grants him victory or he is killed; and He loves a man among people who travel by night, whose night journey grows long, then they alight at the end of the night, sleep being dearer to any of them than anything set beside it, so they sleep while he stands reciting My verses and cajoling Me. And He hates the aged adulterer, the miserly, the arrogant - I think he said - and the conceited."
"4035","4029 - And narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Arafah, from Abu Hafs al-Abbar, from Mansur, from Ribi, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he did not place Zayd ibn Zabyan between Ribi and Abu Dharr - and he said in his hadith: and the poor conceited one. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated by al-Amash from Mansur from Ribi from Abdullah, raised; Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated that."
"4036","Report of Habib ibn Jammaz from Abu Dharr from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4030 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Wahb ibn Jarir, from his father, who said: I heard al-Amash narrating from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Habib ibn Jammaz, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and alighted at Dhu al-Hulayfah; some men hastened to Medina, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spent the night and we spent it with him. In the morning he asked and said: They hastened to Medina and the women; yet they shall leave it at its best. And he spoke good of those who stayed with him. Then he said: Would that I knew when a fire shall emerge from Yemen, from Mount al-Warqan, by which the necks of camels at Busra shall be lit. Al-Bazzar said: We preserve these words only from Abu Dharr with this chain; we know of no other path for Abu Dharr, nor do we know that any narrated from Habib ibn Jammaz other than Abdullah ibn al-Harith, nor that he narrated any hadith other than this one."
"4037","Report of Talq ibn Habib from Abu Dharr. 4031 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Talq ibn Habib, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I guide you to a treasure of the treasures of Paradise? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was also narrated by Abu Bishr from Talq ibn Habib; and we do not know that Talq ibn Habib heard from Abu Dharr."
"4038","Report of al-Huzayl ibn Shurahbil from Abu Dharr. 4032 - Narrated Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan, from Ishaq ibn Idris, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Layth, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, and he is Abu Qays, from al-Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat while two sheep butted before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); one gored the other and caused it to miscarry. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed, and it was said: What makes you laugh? He said: Wonder at her; by the One in whose Hand is my soul, requital shall surely be taken through her on the Day of Resurrection."
"4039","4033 - And narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Suwayd, from Hammad, from Layth, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Tharwan, from al-Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith with this wording except from Abu Dharr, and we know of no one who ascribed it from Layth except Hammad ibn Salamah."
"4040","Report of Ubayd ibn al-Khashkhash from Abu Dharr. 4034 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Yala ibn Ubayd and Abu Dawud, from al-Masudi - Abu Dawud said: from Abu Umar, and Yala said: from Abu Amr - from Ubayd ibn al-Khashkhash, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sat with him in the mosque. He said: O Abu Dharr, seek refuge with Allah from the devils of men and jinn. I said: O Messenger of Allah, are there devils among men? He said: Yes. He said: O Abu Dharr, shall I guide you to a treasure of the treasures of Paradise? He said: I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: There is no power nor strength except through Allah, for it is of the treasures of Paradise. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is fasting? He said: An obligation that suffices. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is prayer? He said: A good thing laid down; whoever wishes, let him do little, and whoever wishes, let him do much. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is charity? He said: Multiplied manifold, and with Allah is increase. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of the two is better? He said: The effort of one with little, or a secret gift to a poor one. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of what was revealed to you is greatest? He said: Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, until the end of the verse. I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of the prophets was first? He said: Adam. I said: And was he a prophet, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, a prophet spoken to. I said: O Messenger of Allah, how many are the prophets? He said: Three hundred and fifteen, a great multitude. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words with this wording except from Abu Dharr; and we know of no narration by Ubayd ibn al-Khashkhash from Abu Dharr except this hadith."
"4041","Report of Nisah ibn Shaddad from Abu Dharr. 4035 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Salamah ibn al-Fadl, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah, from Abdullah ibn al-Miqdam, from Ibn Shaddad, from Abu Dharr, with the wording mentioned in the next report."
"4042","4036 - And narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Arafah, from Ismail ibn Ayyash, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah, from Abdullah ibn al-Miqdam, from Nisah ibn Shaddad, from Abu Dharr - the two being close in their hadiths - who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he rode, when a man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, the other has committed adultery. So he turned away from him. Then he came a second time and said: The other has committed adultery. So he turned away from him. Then he returned a third time and said: The other has committed adultery. So he turned away from him. Then he repeated a fourth time and said: The other has committed adultery. So he dismounted and ordered him stoned. Then he rode, then dismounted and said: O Abu Dharr, your companion has been forgiven and admitted to Paradise. And the wording is that of Salamah ibn al-Fadl. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrates these words with this wording except Abu Dharr; and Abd al-Malik ibn al-Mughirah is known, while Abdullah ibn al-Miqdam and Nisah ibn Shaddad, we know of them being mentioned in no ascribed hadith except this one."
"4043","Report of Abu Murawih al-Ghifari from Abu Dharr. 4037 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Hisham ibn Urwah. 4038 - And narrated to us Tamim ibn al-Muntasir, from Abdullah ibn Numayr, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abu Murawih, from Abu Dharr, informing him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: Faith in Allah and striving in His path. It was said: Which slaves are best? He said: The dearest in price and most precious to their owners. He said: If I cannot do that? He said: You help one in need or make for one lacking skill. I said: What if I am too weak for that? He said: You spare people from evil, for it is a charity you give upon yourself. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no ascribed hadith narrated by Abu Murawih from Abu Dharr except this hadith."
"4044","4039 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Ubaydah, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid, from Ibn Abi al-Zinad, from his father, from Urwah, from Abu al-Murawih, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4045","Report of Suwayd ibn Yazid from Abu Dharr. 4040 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib and Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Quraysh ibn Anas, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Suwayd ibn Yazid, who said: I saw Abu Dharr sitting alone in the mosque, so I seized the chance and sat with him, and I mentioned Uthman to him. He said: I shall never say of Uthman but good, because of something I saw with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I used to follow the private moments of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and learn from him. One day I went, and he had gone out, so I followed him, and he sat in a place, and I sat with him. He said: O Abu Dharr, what brought you? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger. Then Abu Bakr came, greeted and sat to the right of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said to him: What brought you, O Abu Bakr? He said: Allah and His Messenger. Then Umar came and sat to the right of Abu Bakr. He said: O Umar, what brought you? He said: Allah and His Messenger. Then Uthman came and sat to the right of Umar. He said: O Uthman, what brought you? He said: Allah and His Messenger. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took seven or nine pebbles, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he put them down and they fell silent. Then they were placed in the hand of Abu Bakr, and they glorified in his hand until he heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he put them down and they fell silent. Then he took them and they were placed in the hand of Umar, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then they were put down and fell silent. Then he took them and they were placed in the hand of Uthman, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then they were put down and fell silent. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith except from the hadith of Suwayd ibn Yazid from Abu Dharr; and Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated it with added words not in the hadith of Suwayd. And we know of no one who narrated it from Suwayd other than al-Zuhri, nor from al-Zuhri other than Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar; and Salih is lenient in hadith, though a group of the people of knowledge bore his hadith and narrated from him."
"4046","What Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrated from Abu Dharr. 4041 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Mihran ibn Abi Umar, from Sufyan, meaning al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Dharr, with the wording of the night prayer mentioned in the next report."
"4047","4042 - And narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Ubaydullah ibn Musa, from Sufyan, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We fasted with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the month of Ramadan, and he did not stand with us until seven nights remained. Then he stood with us until a third of the night or so had gone. Then he did not stand with us on the fourth night, and stood with us on the fifth night until about half the night had gone. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you prayed the rest of our night for us. He said: When a man is with the imam until he finishes, the rest of his night is reckoned for him. Then he did not stand with us on the sixth night, and stood with us on the seventh night, and sent to his family and women, and they gathered, and he stood with us until we feared missing success. I said: And what is success? He said: The pre-dawn meal. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith with this wording except from Abu Dharr, and we know of no path for it from Abu Dharr other than this path; and more than one narrated it from Dawud."
"4048","4043 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, from Abd al-Ala, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: We fasted with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and he mentioned the hadith at length."
"4049","4044 - Narrated Umar ibn al-Khattab, from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Himsi, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Abdullah ibn Salim, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I used to follow the private moments of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). One day I went, and he had gone out, so I followed him, and he sat in a place, and I sat with him. Then Abu Bakr came, greeted and sat to the right of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Umar came and sat to the right of Abu Bakr. Then Uthman came and sat to the right of Umar. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took pebbles, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he put them down and they fell silent. Then he put them in the hand of Abu Bakr, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he put them down and they fell silent. Then he took them and put them in the hand of Umar, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he put them down and they fell silent. Then he took them and put them in the hand of Uthman, and they glorified in his hand until I heard for them a humming like the humming of bees. Then he put them down and they fell silent."
"4050","Report of Abu Salim al-Jayshani from Abu Dharr. 4045 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Said ibn Abi Ayyub, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Jafar, from Salim ibn Abi Salim al-Jayshani, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Abu Dharr, I see you weak, and I love for you what I love for myself: do not command over two, nor take charge of an orphans wealth. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no one who narrated this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except Abu Dharr, and we know of no narration of it from Abu Dharr except through this channel."
"4051","Report of Abu Marwan from Abu Dharr. 4046 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Affan, from Wuhayb, from Musa ibn Uqbah, who said: Ata ibn Abi Marwan reported to me, from his father, that he reported to him that he entered upon Abu Dharr among men of Aslam, among them a man of Juhaynah. So Abu Dharr asked them: What brought you? They said: We came to greet you and hear from you. He said: Shall I not give you glad tidings? They said: Yes. He said: Whoever meets Allah not associating anything with Him shall be forgiven, even if he has the earth full of sins. Then the Juhani said: Did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So Abu Dharr glorified Allah, then said: Would it befit a Muslim man to say of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he did not say? Then he said: Peace be upon you, and rose."
"4052","4047 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Yaqub ibn Ishaq, from Wuhayb, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, who said: We entered upon Abu Dharr, among us a man of Aslam or a man of Juhaynah. He said: What brought you? We said: We came to greet. He said: Receive glad tidings; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say - then he mentioned similar to the hadith of Affan."
"4053","Report of Urwah ibn al-Zubayr from Abu Dharr. 4048 - Narrated Amr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Dawud, from Jafar ibn Abdullah ibn Uthman al-Qurashi, from Umar ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: I heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, how did you know that you are a prophet? He said: I did not know until I was informed of that. O Abu Dharr, two angels came to me while I was in a valley of Mecca. One of them said: Is it he? He said: Then weigh him against a man. So I was weighed against a man and outweighed him. He said: Then weigh him against ten. So he weighed me against ten and I outweighed them. Then he said: Weigh him against a hundred. So he weighed me against a hundred and I outweighed them. Then he said: Weigh him against a thousand. So he weighed me against a thousand and I outweighed them. Then one of them said to the other: Had you weighed him against his nation, he would have outweighed it. Then one of them said to the other: Split his belly. So he split my belly and removed from it the devils portion and the clinging blood, and cast it away. Then one of them said to the other: Wash his belly as a vessel is washed, and wash his heart as a cloth is washed. Then he called for tranquility as though white papyrus, and it was placed in my heart. Then one of them said to his companion: Stitch his belly. So he stitched my belly and placed the seal between my shoulders; and they had scarcely turned from me as though I witnessed the matter with sight. And Ibn Mamar added in his hadith: And they began scattering upon me from the scale pan. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words from Abu Dharr except through this channel; and we do not know that Urwah heard from Abu Dharr."
"4054","Report of Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm from Abu Dharr. 4049 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Yala ibn Ubayd, from al-Amash, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I guide you to a treasure of the treasures of Paradise: There is no power nor strength except through Allah. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith has been narrated from Abu Dharr through more than one channel."
"4055","4050 - And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever says after the dawn prayer, while folding his legs before speaking: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise; He gives life and death; in His hand is good, and He is over all things capable - ten times, for each of them shall be ten good deeds, ten evil deeds shall be effaced from him thereby, ten degrees shall be raised for him thereby, for each of them shall be the freeing of a slave, and that day he shall be protected from every evil of Satan, and no sin shall follow him to overtake him except association. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of this hadith with this wording except from Abu Dharr with this chain."
"4056","4051 - Narrated Yusuf ibn Musa, from Jarir, from Layth, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the same meaning as the next divine hadith."
"4057","4052 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Yala ibn Ubayd, from Musa ibn al-Musayyab, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the two being close in their hadiths, the wording being that of Layth - who said: Allah, blessed and exalted, says: O My servants, all of you are sinful except those I protect, so seek My forgiveness and I forgive you; all of you are astray except those I guide, so ask Me and I guide you; all of you are poor except those I enrich, so ask Me and I provide for you. Whoever of you knows that I have power over forgiveness, I forgive him by My power, and I do not care. Had the first of you and the last of you, your men and your jinn, your living and your dead, your moist and your dry, gathered upon the most pious heart of a servant of Mine, that would not increase My dominion by a gnats wing. And had the first of you and the last of you, your living and your dead, gathered upon the most wretched heart of a servant of Mine, that would not decrease My dominion by a gnats wing. And had they gathered and every asker asked his wish, I would give every asker what he asked Me, and that would not decrease except as when one of you passes by the sea and dips a needle in it then removes it. That is because I am Generous, Everlasting, Bountiful; I do what I will; My giving is a word and My punishment is a word; My command, when I intend a thing, is only to say to it: Be, and it is. Al-Bazzar said: This hadith was narrated from Shahr from Abd al-Rahman from Abu Dharr by more than one."
"4058","Report of Abu Idris al-Khawlani from Abu Dharr. 4053 - Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hani, from Abu Mushir Abd al-Ala ibn Mushir, who said: Said ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to me, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Allah, blessed and exalted, who said: O My servants, I have forbidden injustice upon Myself and made it forbidden among you, so do not wrong one another. O My servants, you err by night and day, and I forgive sins, so seek My forgiveness and I forgive you. O My servants, all of you are hungry except those I feed, so seek food from Me and I feed you. O My servants, all of you are naked except those I clothe, so seek clothing from Me and I clothe you. O My servants, had the first of you and the last of you, your men and your jinn, been upon the most pious heart of a man among you, that would not increase My dominion anything. O My servants, had the first of you and the last of you, your men and your jinn, gathered in one plain and every person among them asked Me what he asked, nothing of My dominion would decrease except as the sea decreases when a needle is dipped in it a single dip. O My servants, they are only your deeds which I preserve for you; whoever finds good, let him praise Allah, and whoever finds other than that, let him blame none but himself. Said said: When Abu Idris narrated this hadith, he knelt upon his knees. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Abu Dharr through more than one channel."
"4059","Report of Asim ibn Sufyan, Abu Bishr ibn Asim, from Abu Dharr. 4054 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Asim, from Umar ibn Said ibn Abi Husayn, who said: Bishr ibn Asim reported to me, that his father reported to him that he heard Abu al-Darda or Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: I sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to spend the night at his door to wake him for his need, and he permitted me. So I spent a night turning over something, or recalling something I wished to ask him of in the morning. Then he came out to me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I lay wakeful tonight wishing to ask you of something upon my mind: the wealthy have clearly outstripped us; they pray as we pray, fast as we fast, and do deeds, having wealth with which they give charity, while we have nothing with which to do that. He said: Shall I not tell you, O Abu Dharr, of a deed by which you catch those before you and outstrip those after you except those who do the like of your deed? You glorify after every prayer thirty-three times, magnify thirty-three times, and praise thirty-four times. Abu Asim said: He is Abu Dharr, but Umar ibn Said said: Bishr ibn Asim narrated to me that his father reported to him that he heard Abu al-Darda or Abu Dharr. Al-Bazzar said: These words have been narrated from Abu Dharr through more than one channel, and narrated from others as well."
"4060","Report of Usamah ibn Nuaym, also called Usamah ibn Salman, from Abu Dharr. 4055 - Narrated Abu Hurayrah Muhammad ibn Firas al-Basri and Muhammad ibn Mamar, from Abu Dawud, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban, from his father, from Makhul, from Ibn Nuaym - thus he said - that Abu Dharr narrated to them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, blessed and exalted, accepts, or forgives His servant - or he said: accepts the repentance of His servant - so long as the veil has not fallen. It was said: And what is the falling of the veil? He said: That the soul departs while associating partners. Al-Bazzar said: We know of no narration of these words except from Abu Dharr with this chain."
"4061","4056 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Ibn Thawban narrated to us, from his father, from Makhul, from Umar ibn Nuaym al-Ansi, from Usamah ibn Salman, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Indeed Allah forgives His servant so long as the veil has not occurred."" They said: What is the veil? He said: ""So long as the soul does not die while it is associating partners with Allah."""
"4062","Khalid ibn Uhban from Abu Dharr. 4057 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Jarir - meaning Ibn Abd al-Hamid - narrated to us, from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from Abu al-Jahm, from Khalid ibn Uhban, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What will you do, O Abu Dharr, with rulers after me who appropriate this fay (war booty/revenue) for themselves?"" He said: I said: Then, by the One Who sent you with the truth, I will lay down my sword until I meet you. He said: ""Shall I not guide you to what is better for you?"" He said: I said: Yes. He said: ""Be patient until you meet me."""
"4063","4058 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu al-Jahm, from Khalid ibn Uhban, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever separates from the community by a handspan has cast off the yoke of Islam from his neck."" We do not know Khalid ibn Uhban to have narrated any supported hadith from Abu Dharr except these two hadiths, and we do not know anyone to have narrated from Khalid ibn Uhban except Abu al-Jahm."
"4064","Ghudayf ibn al-Harith from Abu Dharr. 4059 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Makhul, from Ghudayf ibn al-Harith, he said: Abu Dharr said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Indeed Allah, blessed and exalted, has placed the truth upon the tongue of Umar; he speaks by it."" We do not know this wording to be narrated from Abu Dharr except with this chain."
"4065","Umm Dharr from Abu Dharr. 4060 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Mujahid, from Ibrahim ibn al-Ashtar, from his father, from Umm Dharr, she said: When the pain of Abu Dharr grew severe - or she said: he was attended (by death) - I said: You will die in a deserted land and I have nothing with which to shroud you. He said to me: Watch the road. I kept going out and looking, then returning to him. While I was thus, I saw men approaching as if they were vultures. I waved my garment to them and they hastened until they came toward me. I said to them: Will you attend a man from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Who is he? I said: Abu Dharr. So they ransomed him with their fathers and mothers, then entered upon him. He said to them: Rejoice, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to a group of whom I was one: ""A man among you will surely die in a deserted land, attended by a party of Muslims."" None of that group died except in a town and community except me. And I heard him say: ""If three of a man's children die, he will not enter the Fire - or the Fire will not touch him."" So when I die, shroud me; I adjure by Allah any man who shrouds me if he was a chief, or a courier, or a captain. He said: None of that group but had engaged in something of that except a youth among them who said: I will shroud you in two garments in my bag, woven by my mother. He said: You shall shroud me. He said: So he passed away, and they washed him, shrouded him, prayed over him, and left. The whole party were Yemenis."
"4066","Jasrah bint Dajajah from Abu Dharr. 4061 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl informed us, he said: Qudamah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Jasrah bint Dajajah. 4062 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Qudamah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Jasrah bint Dajajah al-Amiriyyah, and the wording is that of Ibn Mamar. She said: I performed Umrah in Rajab and reached al-Rabadhah at Asr prayer; they called the adhan and iqamah, then said: O Abu Dharr, come forward and lead the people. He refused, so Abu Dharr called a man and he refused, then a second and a third and they refused, until the sun yellowed or nearly yellowed. Then a man came forward and prayed with them. When he finished, Abu Dharr turned to them and told them about some of their prayer. He said: The Messenger of Allah led us in the last Isha prayer; when he finished he stood to pray after the nightfall, and people stood behind him. When he saw them praying behind him he returned to his saddlebag; when he saw they had left the station he returned to them and stood praying. I came until I stood behind him, and he gestured me to his right. Then Ibn Masud came and stood behind me and behind him, so he gestured to him to his left, and he stood. Meanwhile each person recited and prayed individually, while the Prophet recited a single verse: {If You punish them, they are Your servants} to the end of the verse, until he prayed Fajr with it - bowing with it, prostrating with it, standing with it, supplicating with it, and sitting with it. Abu Dharr gestured to Abdullah ibn Masud to ask him about what he did last night, reciting a single verse though Allah had taught him the whole Quran - had another done it we would have blamed him. He said: I am not one to ask him about anything until he begins with me. So Abu Dharr asked him, and he said: I supplicated for my ummah. He said: What were you answered, and what was returned to you? He said: ""What if they were to glimpse it, many of them would abandon prayer."" He said: Shall I not go and give the people glad tidings of that? So he went off swiftly like the throw of a stone. Umar said: O Messenger of Allah, if you send it to the people they will rely and abandon worship. He said: So he sent me back and I said nothing. We do not know anyone to have narrated this account from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu Dharr, nor do we know for it any route other than this route. Qudamah ibn Abdullah was narrated from by Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, Muhammad ibn Ubayd, Muhammad ibn Fudayl and others. As for this Jasrah bint Dajajah, we do not know anyone to have narrated from her except Qudamah."
"4067","Abd al-Rahman ibn Mikhraq from Abu Dharr. 4063 - Ahmad ibn Aban al-Qurashi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yazid ibn Jadhubah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mikhraq, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Indeed Allah, blessed and exalted, created a wind and settled it in a house, and closed a door upon it. If the door were opened it would sweep away what is between heaven and earth. What reaches you comes only through the gaps of that door. You call it al-Janub (the south wind), and with Allah it is al-Azyab."" This hadith we do not know to be narrated except from Abu Dharr, nor do we know for it a route from Abu Dharr except this route."
"4068","Musa ibn Talhah from Abu Dharr. 4064 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sam, from Musa ibn Talhah, he said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to fast three days of each month, the white days: the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth."" This hadith has been narrated from Abu Dharr by more than one route, and others narrated it from Yahya ibn Sam, among them al-Amash and Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad and others."
"4069","Ibn Hujayrah from Abu Dharr. 4065 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Abdullah al-Yahsibi narrated to us, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from Ibn Hujayrah, from Abu Dharr, in marfu form, he said: ""Indeed the treasure which Allah mentioned in His Book is a solid tablet of gold. I marvel at one who is certain of the Decree, why does he toil? I marvel at one who remembers the Fire, why does he laugh? I marvel at one who remembers death, why is he heedless? There is no god but Allah; Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu Dharr except from this angle with this chain."
"4070","Muawiyah ibn Thalabah from Abu Dharr. 4066 - Ali ibn al-Mundhir and Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Amir ibn al-Sibt, from Abu al-Jahhaf Dawud ibn Abi Awf, from Muawiyah ibn Thalabah, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali: ""O Ali, whoever separates from me, Allah separates from him; and whoever separates from you, O Ali, has separated from me."" We do not know this wording to be narrated except from Abu Dharr with this chain."
"4071","4067 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, he said: Marthad - or Abu Marthad - narrated to me, from his father, he said: I met Abu Dharr at the middle Jamrah and asked him about Laylat al-Qadr. He said: No one asked about it more than I did. I said: O Messenger of Allah, was it sent down upon the prophets, revealed to them therein, then lifted? He said: ""Rather it remains until the Day of Resurrection."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of them is it? He said: ""If I were permitted I would inform you of it, but seek it in the nineties or the seventies, and do not ask me again."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and began to speak, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, in which of the seventies is it? He became angry with me with an anger like which he had never been angry with me before nor after, then said: ""Did I not forbid you about it? If I were permitted I would inform you - or inform you all - of it, but..."" and he mentioned a phrase, that it be in the last seven."
"4072","4068 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar and Zurayq ibn al-Sakht narrated to us, they said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, from Abu Zumayl, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about Laylat al-Qadr: was it sent down upon the prophets, revealed to them therein, then lifted? He said: ""Rather it remains until the Day of Resurrection."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me which night it is? He said: ""If I were permitted I would inform you - or inform you all - of it, but it is in the nineties or the seventies, and do not ask me again."" So I said: I adjure you, O Messenger of Allah, you must inform me in which of the nineties it is. He became angry with me with an anger like which he had never been angry with me before nor after, then said: Did I not forbid you to ask me about this - or about it? I said: I adjure you, O Messenger of Allah, you must inform me. He said: Did I not forbid you to ask me? It is in the last seven. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu Dharr except from this angle with this chain."
"4073","4069 - al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad al-Jurashi narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Abu Zumayl, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I was weighed against a thousand of my ummah and I outweighed them, and they began to scatter away from me from the scale pan."""
"4074","4070 - al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad al-Jurashi narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, from Abu Zumayl, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Your smiling in the face of your brother is charity; your pouring from your bucket into your brother's bucket is charity; your enjoining good and forbidding evil is recorded for you as charity; your removing a thorn and stone from the road is charity; and your guiding one astray from the road is charity."""
"4075","4071 - al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to us, from Abu Zumayl, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The majority (in wealth) are the minority (in reward), except one who says with wealth: thus and thus."""
"4076","4072 - al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to us, from Abu Zumayl, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Neither the dusty earth has carried nor the green sky has shaded one with a tongue more truthful than Abu Dharr, resembling Isa ibn Maryam."" Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do you affirm that for him? He said: ""Yes, so affirm it for him."""
"4077","4073 - al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah narrated to us, from Abu Zumayl, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Abu Dharr, I saw as if I was weighed against forty, you among them, and I outweighed them."" These hadiths which al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated from Ikrimah, we do not know anyone to have shared with him in them from Ikrimah."
"4078","A cousin of Abu Dharr from Abu Dharr. 4074 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim al-Balkhi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from a cousin of Abu Dharr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever drinks wine, his prayer is not accepted for forty nights. If he repents, Allah turns to him. If he returns, it is the same. If he returns, it is the same."" He said: I do not know whether in the third or the fourth, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If he returns, it is Allah's due to give him to drink from the clay of al-Khabal."" It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the clay of al-Khabal? He said: ""The exudate of the people of the Fire."" This hadith was narrated by others besides Ubaydullah from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from a man, from Abu Dharr; and Ubaydullah named the man."
"4079","Abu Nasr from Abu Dharr. 4075 - Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Muhadir - meaning Ibn Muwarri - narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Nasr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The thickness of the earth is a journey of five hundred years; between the upper earth and the lowest heaven is five hundred years, its thickness five hundred years; the thickness of the second the same; between each two earths the same; between the upper earth and the heaven five hundred years; the thickness of the heaven five hundred years; between the lowest heaven and the second a journey of five hundred years and the thickness of the heaven five hundred years; then each heaven likewise until he reached the seventh; then between the seventh and the Throne a journey of all that combined."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu Dharr except with this chain. This Abu Nasr I think is Humayd ibn Hilal, and he did not hear from Abu Dharr."
"4080","Mujahid from Abu Dharr. 4076 - Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The best deed is love for Allah and hate for Allah."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu Dharr with this chain, nor do we know Mujahid to have heard from Abu Dharr."
"4081","4077 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Wasil - meaning al-Ahdab - from Mujahid, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I was given five not given to anyone before me: the earth was made for me a purifier and a mosque; spoils were made lawful for me though not lawful for any prophet before me; I was aided by dread over my enemy a month's journey; I was sent to every red and black; and I was given intercession, which will reach of my ummah whoever dies not associating anything with Allah."" This hadith was narrated by Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad from Mujahid from Ibn Abbas, and Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated it from Mujahid from Ibn Umar, and al-Amash narrated it from Ubayd ibn Umayr from Abu Dharr."
"4082","al-Hasan from Abu Dharr. 4078 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Juwayriyah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about prayer? He said: ""The completion of deed."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, I ask you about charity. He said: ""Charity is a wondrous thing."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, I have left the best deed in my view - or its best: fasting. He said: ""Good, but not there."" I said: O Messenger of Allah, which charity is best? - and he mentioned a phrase - I said: If I do not do or cannot? He said: ""From your surplus food."" I said: If I do not? He said: ""With half a date."" I said: If I do not? He said: ""With a good word."" I said: If I do not? He said: ""Spare people from evil, for it is a charity you give on yourself."" I said: If I do not? He said: ""Remove harm."" I said: If I do not? He said: ""You wish to leave no good in you at all."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated with this wording except from Abu Dharr with this chain. End of the thirty-third; praise be to Allah abundantly as He deserves."
"4083","Musnad of Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him). Hadith of Abu al-Darda from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - what Fadal ibn Ubayd narrated from Abu al-Darda. 4079 - Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ayyub ibn Habib al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Abd al-Khaliq narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Ziyadah ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Indeed Allah, blessed and exalted, descends in the three hours remaining of the night: in the first hour He opens the Remembrance which none but He has seen - Allah effaces what He wills and confirms what He wills. Then He descends in the second hour to the Garden of Eden, which none but He has seen and which has not occurred to a human heart; none of the sons of Adam shall dwell therein with Him except three: the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs. Then He says: Blessed is whoever enters you. Then He descends in the third hour to the lowest heaven and says: Is there no seeker of forgiveness, that I may forgive him? Is there no asker asking Me, that I may give him? Is there no supplicant calling Me, that I may answer him? Until the Fajr prayer."" Likewise Allah says: {And the recitation of dawn; indeed the recitation of dawn is witnessed}. He said: The angels of night and day witness it. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle. Ziyadah ibn Muhammad we do not know anyone to have narrated from except al-Layth, and we do not know Fadalah ibn Ubayd to have supported from Abu al-Darda other than this hadith, then the one after it."
"4084","4080 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Ziyadah ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd, that two men came seeking healing for themselves from urine; they were taken to Abu al-Darda, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Our Lord Who is in heaven, hallowed be Your name; Your command is in heaven and earth; as Your mercy is in heaven, place Your mercy in earth; forgive us our sins and faults, You are the Lord of the good; send down a mercy from Your mercy upon this pain."" So they said it and were healed. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle. Ziyadah ibn Muhammad - we have already mentioned him; Fadalah ibn Ubayd narrated from Abu al-Darda only these two hadiths, and we mentioned them with their defect, for we did not preserve them from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle."
"4085","4081 - Muhammad ibn Isa al-Tamimi narrated to us, he said: al-Abbas ibn Najih al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Abd al-Aziz, nephew of Ismail ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi al-Muhajir, narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Abi Karimah, from Hayyan the freedman of Abu al-Darda, he said: I heard Umm al-Darda - or Umm al-Darda narrated to me - from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found a group of Arabs boasting among themselves. I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he said: ""What is this, O Abu al-Darda, that I hear?"" I said: O Messenger of Allah, these Arabs are boasting among themselves. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O Abu al-Darda, when you boast, boast of Quraysh; when you vie in numbers, vie with Tamim; when you fight, fight with Qays. Know that its face is Kinanah, its tongue Asad, and its horsemen Qays. O Abu al-Darda, indeed Allah has horsemen in His heaven with whom He fights His enemies, and they are the angels; and He has horsemen in His earth with whom He fights His enemies, and they are Qays. O Abu al-Darda, indeed the last to fight for Islam, when nothing remains but its mention and of the Quran but its script, will be a man of Qays."" He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which Qays? He said: ""From Sulaym."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle. Al-Abbas ibn Najih is unobjectionable; this Bakr ibn Abd al-Aziz is not known for transmission though known by lineage; likewise Sulayman ibn Abi Karimah. But since we did not preserve this wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle, we had to produce it and clarify its defect."
"4086","4082 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Yusuf al-Sanani, from Yazid ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Makhul, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding Allah's saying {and beneath it was a treasure belonging to them}: It was ""gold and silver."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle; its chain is good. Yazid ibn Yusuf is unobjectionable, and those before and after him are trustworthy. We do not know for the hadith from Abu al-Darda a connected route other than this route."
"4087","4083 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Israil narrated to us, from Layth, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me moving my lips and said: ""O Abu al-Darda, what are you saying?"" I said: I am remembering Allah. He said: Shall I teach you something better than remembering Allah by night with day and by day with night? I said: Yes. He said: ""Say: Glory be to Allah to the number of what He created, and glory be to Allah to the fullness of what He created, and glory be to Allah to the number of all things, and glory be to Allah to the fullness of all things, and glory be to Allah to the number of what His Book has enumerated, and glory be to Allah to the fullness of what His Book has enumerated; and praise be to Allah to the number of what He created, and praise be to Allah to the fullness of what He created, and praise be to Allah to the fullness of all things, and praise be to Allah to the number of what His Book has enumerated, and praise be to Allah to the fullness of what His Book has enumerated."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this chain; its chain is good except Abu Israil alone, for the scholars spoke about him and weakened him. Al-Thawri and those below him narrated from him, and people bore his hadith despite its weakness. We wrote it only because we do not preserve this hadith from another, and we do not know Yazid ibn al-Asamm to have narrated from Abu al-Darda other than this hadith."
"4088","4084 - al-Hasan ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Jarawi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Rabah narrated to us, he said: I heard Nimran ibn Utbah al-Dhimari mention from Umm al-Darda, she said: I heard Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When a servant curses something, the curse ascends to heaven and its doors are closed before it; then it descends to earth and its doors are closed before it; then it goes right and left. If it finds no passage, it returns to the one who was cursed: if he deserves it, otherwise it returns to its utterer."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way with this wording except with this chain from Abu al-Darda from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Walid ibn Rabah we do not know anyone to have narrated from except Yahya ibn Hassan, and Yahya is trustworthy, a hadith scholar. Nimran ibn Utbah we do not know anyone to have narrated from except al-Walid, and he is his nephew - he is al-Walid ibn Utbah. We mentioned this hadith despite its weakness only because we did not preserve it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle."
"4089","4085 - al-Hasan ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: al-Walid al-Dhimari narrated to us, he said: Nimran ibn Utbah narrated to me, he said: We entered upon Umm al-Darda while we were small orphans; she wiped our heads and said: Rejoice, my sons, for I hope you will be in your father's intercession - may Allah have mercy on him. I heard Abu al-Darda say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The martyr will intercede for seventy of his household - or: the martyr will surely intercede for seventy of his household."""
"4090","4086 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Thawban ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Mubashshir ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Tammam ibn Najih, from Kab ibn Dhuhl, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat, we sat around him; when he intended to leave, he left his sandal or part of what was on him. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this chain. Tammam ibn Najih is a Syrian; Mubashshir, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid, and Kab ibn Dhuhl narrated from him. This hadith is not preserved from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle, so we wrote it; for Tammam and Kab are not strong in hadith."
"4091","4087 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywah, from his father, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""What Allah has made lawful in His Book is lawful, what He has forbidden is forbidden, and what He has been silent about is pardon. So accept Allah's pardon, for Allah would not forget anything."" Then he recited this verse: {And your Lord was never forgetful}. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywah was narrated from by a group; Abu Raja narrated from Abu al-Darda more than one hadith. Its chain is sound, for Ismail ibn Ayyash was narrated from by people who bore his hadith."
"4092","4088 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, relative of Ahmad ibn Mani, narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Ibn Halbas Yunus ibn Maysarah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Indeed Allah says to Isa ibn Maryam: I shall send after you a nation: if what they love befalls them they praise and thank; if what they hate befalls them they count it (for reward) and are patient - without forbearance or knowledge."" He said: O Lord, how is this, without forbearance or knowledge? He said: ""I shall give them of My forbearance and My knowledge."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from the narration of Abu al-Darda with this chain from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Muawiyah ibn Salih is trustworthy; Yunus ibn Maysarah ibn Halbas is trustworthy, among the devout Syrians; his hadith is collected. Its chain is good."
"4093","4089 - Abu Kurayb and Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sad al-Ansari narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Rabiah ibn Yazid al-Dimashqi, from Aidhullah Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Dawud used to say: O Allah, I ask You for Your love, the love of whoever loves You, and the deed that conveys to me Your love. O Allah, make Your love dearer to me than my family and cold water."" And when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Dawud (peace be upon him), he said: ""He was the most worshipful of mankind."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Muhammad ibn Sad was narrated from by Muhammad ibn Fudayl with hadiths in which none shared with him, except that we did not preserve his hadiths from another, so we mentioned them and clarified their defect."
"4094","4090 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Musa ibn al-Saib, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The servants have not asked for anything better than that He forgive them and grant them well-being."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle. Similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than this wording, so we mentioned each hadith with its wording in its place. Salim ibn Abi al-Jad did not hear anything from Abu al-Darda as far as we know."
"4095","4091 - Abu Kurayb or another narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Ayyub al-Ifriqi narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every beast of prey with fangs, the trussed-up (target), plunder - and I think he said: the domestic donkey. This hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording by various routes. As for Abu al-Darda, he is among the highest to narrate that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Darda for his eminence. We did not list all that was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from this angle with this wording unless it changes a wording or adds something. Its chain is good. We do not know Said ibn al-Musayyib to have narrated from Abu al-Darda other than this hadith, nor anyone to have narrated this hadith from Safwan ibn Sulaym except Abu Ayyub; and from this Abu Ayyub narrated Abd al-Rahim and Ibn Abi Zaidah."
"4096","4092 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Habibah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""I am your share among the prophets, and you are my share among the nations."" We do not know anyone to have narrated this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except Abu al-Darda, nor from Abu al-Darda except Abu Habibah, nor from Abu Habibah except Abu Ishaq, nor from Abu Ishaq except al-Thawri, nor from al-Thawri except Zayd, nor from Zayd except Abu Kurayb; and we do not know anyone to have corroborated him in this hadith."
"4097","4093 - Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Idris al-Awdi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Habibah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The one who gives charity at his death is like one who gives a gift after he is full."" This hadith was narrated by al-Thawri from Abu Ishaq from Abu Habibah from Abu al-Darda from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); others besides al-Thawri also narrated it. We do not know for it a route from Abu al-Darda except Abu Habibah al-Tai; and Abu Habibah we do not know anyone to have narrated from except Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani alone. We do not know this wording to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle alone. We mentioned the hadith of Idris from Abu Ishaq to the exclusion of others only because we do not know anyone to have narrated this hadith from Idris except Ismail ibn Ibrahim."
"4098","4094 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid Abu Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shimr ibn Atiyyah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, he said: I was with Abu al-Darda when a man of Medina entered and asked him. He said: Where did you leave Abu Dharr? Abu al-Darda said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. Had Abu Dharr cut off a limb of mine, I would not desert him, for what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say about him. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu al-Darda from a better angle than this angle, nor do we know for it a rarer route."
"4099","4095 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif narrated to us, from Ata, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda."
"4100","4096 - And Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, from Ata al-Kaykharanı, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4101","4097 - And Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif narrated to us - meaning - from Ata al-Kaykharani, from Ata ibn Nafi, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4102","4098 - And Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Yala ibn Mamlak, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Nothing heavier than good character is placed in the Scale on the Day of Resurrection."" And Ibn Uyaynah added in his hadith: ""Good character indeed brings its bearer to the rank of fasting and prayer."" This hadith we do not know to be narrated from Abu al-Darda except from these angles we mentioned. As for Mutarrif from Ata, and al-Qasim from Ata, he is Ata al-Kaykharani. As for the hadith of Amr ibn Dinar from Ibn Abi Mulaykah from Yala ibn Mamlak, we do not know anyone to have narrated it but Ibn Uyaynah alone. Yala was narrated from by Ibn Abi Mulaykah with another hadith. The hadith is of good chain. Ata ibn Nafi is Ata al-Kaykharani, a Meccan."
"4103","4099 - Ibrahim ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Provision seeks the servant as his term seeks him."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Abu al-Darda, nor do we know for it a route other than this route. We do not know anyone to have narrated it from al-Walid except Hisham ibn Khalid; he was unobjectionable except that he was not corroborated in this hadith. The scholars bore it from him and mentioned it from him. Its chain is sound except for what they mentioned of Hisham ibn Khalid's being alone with it; we know no defect in it."
"4104","4100 - One of our companions narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Sulayman, from Muawiyah ibn Yahya, from Yunus ibn Maysarah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard him swear by Allah - and I never heard him swear at anything before it - he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has chosen for you the best speech, four phrases: Glory be to Allah, Praise be to Allah, There is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest."
"4105","4100 (m) - And from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked about Allah's saying: {Every day He is in a matter}. He said: Among His matters is to forgive a sin, relieve a distress, raise a people and lower others. This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - so we mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Darda when it came to us. We did not know at this time a better chain for this wording, so we mentioned it unless we find a better chain; for Muawiyah ibn Yahya is weak in hadith, while Yunus ibn Maysarah and those after him and before Muawiyah are trustworthy. So we mentioned this hadith though we did not preserve it from another."
"4106","4101 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Atiyyah ibn Qays, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Report and you will be lessened (in reward)."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. It was narrated from Abu al-Darda from another angle as mawquf; but Suwayd supported it from Baqiyyah. I fear Baqiyyah did not hear it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam from Atiyyah ibn Qays; for Abu Bakr is trustworthy, Atiyyah is unobjectionable, while the hadith as marfu is denounced."
"4107","4102 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Rashid ibn Dawud al-Sanani narrated to me, from Abu Uthman al-Sanani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Rain was withheld in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we asked Allah's Prophet to pray for rain for us. He prayed for rain. Allah's Prophet went out in the morning, and there were people talking. They said: We were given rain last night by such-and-such a star. Allah's Prophet said: ""Allah does not bestow a blessing upon a people but they wake up disbelieving in it."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated by various routes from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than this wording, but like it in meaning. So we mentioned this hadith for its changed wording and for Abu al-Darda's added speech in it over the rest of the hadiths, which are of better chain than it."
"4108","4103 - One of our companions narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, he said: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Wadin ibn Ata, from Nasr ibn Alqamah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: ""Allah indeed took Dawud from among his companions, yet they were neither tempted nor turned away; and the companions of the Messiah remained upon his guidance and his sunnah for two hundred years."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a connected route except from Abu al-Darda with this chain. Its chain is good; all in it are known for transmission, well-known."
"4109","4104 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Damrah ibn Habib, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Cut down your food (into small loaves); it will be blessed for you therein."" Ibrahim ibn Abdullah said: I heard one of the scholars explain it, he said: It is making the loaves small. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this chain from Abu al-Darda connected. Its chain is good, among the chains of the Syrians."
"4110","4105 - Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Khushani narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Busr ibn Ubaydullah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with his head dripping; he prayed with us in a single garment wrapped around him, its ends crossed. When he finished, Umar ibn al-Khattab said to him: You pray in a single garment, and in it? He said: ""Yes, I pray in it, and in it"" - meaning ritual impurity (janabah). We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle, except the first part about praying in a single garment. As for the addition of his praying in a garment with janabah, we do not know anyone to have added it with a sound chain. Al-Hasan ibn Yahya is unobjectionable; Zayd ibn Waqid is unobjectionable in hadith - his hadith is collected; the rest of the chain are trustworthy well-known."
"4111","4106 - Umar narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Utbah narrated to us, he said: I heard Yunus ibn Maysarah ibn Halbas narrate from Abu Idris, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""No disobedient to parents shall enter Paradise, nor an addict of wine, nor a denier of the Decree."""
"4112","4107 - And with his chain, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""A servant does not reach the reality of faith until he knows that what befell him would never have missed him, and what missed him would never have befallen him."""
"4113","4108 - And with his chain, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, tell us: what we do - is it something already concluded, or something we begin anew? He said: ""Each person is facilitated for what he was created for."" These three hadiths with one chain have good chains. They are narrated from others besides Abu al-Darda, so we sufficed with the narration of Abu al-Darda therein from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), unless another besides Abu al-Darda adds something in a wording of them, in which case the hadith is written for the addition he added; otherwise that suffices."
"4114","4109 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Shall I not guide you to better than much prayer and charity?"" He said: Yes. He said: ""Mending relations."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a connected chain better than this chain for this wording. Its chain is sound, though its wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is strange."
"4115","4110 - Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn Nujayh narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar Hafs narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Numayri, from Anas ibn Malik, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Whoever fulfills a desire of his brother will be forgiven."" We do not know the wording of this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this chain. Nasr ibn Nujayh is Basran; Hafs ibn Umar is Basran, not strong in hadith. We wrote this hadith despite its defect only because it is not preserved from the Messenger of Allah; so we wrote it."
"4116","4111 - Muhammad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Busr ibn Ubaydullah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""While I was asleep I saw the pillar of the Book carried away from beneath my head; I thought it was taken, so I followed it with my sight, and it was set down in al-Sham. Know that faith, when tribulations occur, is in al-Sham."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except among the hadiths of the Syrians. Abdullah ibn Busr, Abu al-Darda, and Wahshi ibn Harb narrated it. We do not know for it a better chain from Abu al-Darda than this chain. It was also narrated from Abu al-Darda from another angle, and this is the best chain narrated also from Abu al-Darda."
"4117","4112 - Muhammad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Malik, from Abu Ubaydullah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I shall surely find myself disputing with one of you at the Hawd (Pool); it will be said: You do not know what he innovated after you."" Abu al-Darda said: Supplicate Allah not to make me of them. He said: ""You are not of them"" - and this is not in anyone's hadith. This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by many routes. Abu al-Darda added in his hadith: ""Supplicate Allah not to make me of them,"" and this is not in the hadith of anyone we know to have narrated similar wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we wrote it. Muhammad ibn Muhajir is trustworthy; Yazid ibn Abi Malik is trustworthy; Abu Ubaydullah is well-known among the Syrians."
"4118","4113 - Muhammad ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated to me, from Yunus ibn Halbas, from Abu Idris Aidhullah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the dunghill of a people in which was a dead kid. He said: ""Its owners have no need of it."" They said: O Prophet of Allah, had its owners any need of it they would not have discarded it. He said: ""By Allah, the world is more insignificant to Allah than this kid is to its owners. Let me not find it has destroyed one of you."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes. The highest to narrate it from him is Abu al-Darda with this chain. Its chain is sound, of Syrian hadith. In Abu al-Darda's hadith is an addition over the rest of the hadiths: ""Let me not find it has destroyed one of you."""
"4119","4114 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Sad, from Uthman ibn Hayyan, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey in intense heat, and none among the people fasted except the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abdullah ibn Rawahah. We do not know anyone to have narrated this hadith with this wording except Abu al-Darda alone. Its chain is good. Hisham ibn Sad is trustworthy; Uthman ibn Hayyan is trustworthy; those after them are trustworthy."
"4120","4115 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and one of us would place his hand on his head from the severity of heat; none of us fasted except the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abdullah ibn Rawahah. This hadith has been narrated from Abu al-Darda from another angle. Ismail ibn Ubaydullah added a letter therein, which we mentioned for the addition and the goodness of its chain."
"4121","4116 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Usamah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Sinan Abu Farwah, he said: Abu Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: I heard an elder in the Sacred Mosque say: Abu al-Darda said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Everything has a prime part, and the prime part of prayer is the first takbirah; so guard it."" Abu Ubaydullah said: I narrated it to Raja ibn Haywah, and he narrated to me, he said: Umm al-Darda narrated to me, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). We do not know this hadith to be narrated with this wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with this chain from Abu al-Darda from him. Similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than its wording, which we shall mention - Allah willing - in its place. Yazid ibn Sinan al-Ruhawi was narrated from by people; Abu Ubaydullah is not known; what follows in the chain is sound."
"4122","4117 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Bishr narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umarah narrated to us, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abu al-Darda and Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with them), they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The slaughter of the fetus is the slaughter of its mother."" This hadith has come from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes. Abu Said al-Khudri and Abu Ayyub narrated it; the highest to narrate it is Abu al-Darda. So we mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Darda and Abu Umamah, since it was joined to Abu al-Darda, and we shall not repeat it from others unless there is an addition."
"4123","4118 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Musa al-Saghir, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Before you is a steep pass in which none is saved except every unencumbered one."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way except from this angle. We do not know anyone to have narrated it but Abu Muawiyah from Musa al-Saghir. Musa is a trustworthy Kufan narrated from by people; Hilal ibn Yasaf is well-known; the rest of the chain is sound."
"4124","4119 - Muhammad ibn Miskin and al-Hasan ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Jarawi narrated to us, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Musa al-Saghir, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""{Say: He is Allah, One} equals a third of the Quran."" This hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes with this wording. We do not know it to be narrated from Abu al-Darda with a connected chain better than this; its chain is sound. We do not know Hilal ibn Yasaf to have narrated from Umm al-Darda except these two hadiths, nor anyone to have narrated this hadith from Abu Muawiyah except Asad ibn Musa, and he is trustworthy of Egypt, called Asad al-Sunnah."
"4125","4120 - Abdah ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Is there recitation in every prayer? He said: ""Yes."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a chain with this wording except from Abu al-Darda. Its chain is good: Muawiyah ibn Salih is trustworthy; Abu al-Zahiriyyah is well-known, narrated from by people; Kathir ibn Murrah is also well-known, narrated from by people."
"4126","4121 - Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Said al-Barrad, from Uthman ibn Hayyan, he said: I was with Umm al-Darda when I took a flea and cast it into fire. She said: I heard Abu al-Darda say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""None punishes with fire except the Lord of Fire."" He said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Whoever conveys to a ruler the need of one unable to convey it, Allah will make his feet firm upon the Sirat on the day feet slip."" This hadith - part of its wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than this wording, namely: ""None punishes with fire except the Lord of Fire."" Similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes. Abu al-Darda added: ""Whoever conveys to a ruler..."" This latter from Abu al-Darda we do not preserve from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a connected route except from this angle, so we wrote it. Said al-Barrad was narrated from by Hammad ibn Zayd; Said ibn Zayd is his brother, Basran."
"4127","4122 - al-Hasan ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us; and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abu Hisham al-Mughirah ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever dies not associating anything with Allah will enter Paradise."" I said: Even if he fornicates and steals? He said: ""Even if he fornicates and steals, despite Abu al-Darda's nose."" This hadith has been narrated from Abu Dharr and Abu al-Darda by various routes. The best chain narrated in that from Abu al-Darda is this chain, for al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah is well-known of Kufa; Zayd ibn Wahb is well-known, trustworthy. So we mentioned this chain for its fame. We shall not repeat it from Abu al-Darda nor Abu Dharr unless they add therein, by other than this chain, speech that must be written for its addition; otherwise we sufficed with this hadith with this wording to the exclusion of others."
"4128","4123 - al-Hasan ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr - meaning al-Awzai - narrated to us that Walid ibn Hisham narrated to him that his father narrated to him, he said: Madan ibn Abi Talhah narrated to me that Abu al-Darda informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke fast. He said: I met Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said to him: Abu al-Darda narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke fast. He said: He spoke true, and I poured his ablution water for him. This hadith with this wording has been narrated by various routes from Abu al-Darda, but we sufficed with this chain to the exclusion of others, for in this chain Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated it from al-Awzai, so we wrote it for that and did not list it from others."
"4129","4124 - al-Hasan ibn Yahya and Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad al-Raqqashi narrated to us, they said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Khumayr narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Marthad, from the daughter of Abu al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""If you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much, and you would go out to the open grounds seeking to be saved, yet not be saved."" One of them said: ""And I do not know whether you will be saved or not."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu al-Darda except from this angle. It has been narrated from others besides Abu al-Darda from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by sounder routes than this. We wrote this hadith from Abu al-Darda though another is sounder in chain than it, for in it is an addition in wording, namely ""seeking to be saved,"" so we wrote it. We do not know anyone to have supported this hadith from Shubah except Muslim; a group besides Muslim narrated it from Shubah and stopped it at Abu al-Darda."
"4130","4125 - Bishr ibn Khalid and Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Musab narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Muhammad, from Bilal ibn Abi al-Darda, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Your love of a thing blinds and deafens."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from Abu al-Darda nor from another besides Abu al-Darda except from this angle. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam is trustworthy; Khalid ibn Muhammad is not known; Bilal ibn Abi al-Darda is well-known in lineage and narration - more than one person narrated from him. Had we not preserved the hadith from anyone from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with this chain, we would not have written it, given Khalid ibn Muhammad."
"4131","4126 - Ibrahim ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Abbas narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Satan has despaired of being worshipped in the Arabian Peninsula, but he is content with you in belittled sins."" This hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes."
"4132","4127 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, he said: Zayd ibn Artah narrated to me, he said: I heard Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrate from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The camp of the Muslims on the day of the great battle is in al-Ghutah, beside a city called Damascus."" Similar wording of this hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from another angle, so we mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Darda for its good chain and did not list it from others unless it adds speech, in which case it is written for the addition."
"4133","4128 - Khalid ibn Muhammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Bilal ibn Abi al-Darda, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Neither the green sky has shaded nor the dusty earth carried one with a tongue more truthful than Abu Dharr."" This hadith has been narrated from Abu al-Darda from another angle, so we mentioned this hadith for its rare chain; for we did not preserve from Ali ibn Zayd from Bilal ibn Abi al-Darda other than this hadith, nor do we know anyone to have narrated this hadith from Ali ibn Zayd except Hammad ibn Salamah."
"4134","4129 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak al-Suri narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to me, from Busr ibn Ubaydullah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) came, holding the edge of his garment, his knee exposed. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him, he said: ""As for your companion - I think he said - he has quarreled."" He came until he greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, there was something between me and Umar, and I hastened against him; then I regretted what happened, so I asked him to forgive me and he refused. I followed him to al-Baqi until he sheltered in his house from me, so I came to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""May Allah forgive you, Abu Bakr,"" three times. Then Umar regretted when Abu Bakr asked him to forgive him and he had refused. He left his house until he came to Abu Bakr's house and asked: Is Abu Bakr here? They said: No; we know he went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he greeted him. The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began to flush, until Abu Bakr feared something disliked might come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) toward Umar. When Abu Bakr saw that, he knelt on his knees and said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I was the more unjust. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""O people, Allah sent me to you and you said: You lie; while Abu Bakr said: You speak truth. He consoled me with himself and his wealth. Will you then leave my companion to me?"" three times. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a connected route from him except from this angle from Abu al-Darda alone. Sadaqah is sound in hadith, of Damascus - he is Sadaqah ibn Khalid; Zayd ibn Waqid and Busr ibn Ubaydullah - we have already mentioned them."
"4135","4130 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak al-Suri narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Waqid narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, and Yunus from Abu Idris from Muadh, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""The first thing my Lord forbade me after worshipping idols is drinking wine and quarreling with men."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a connected chain except with this chain. Amr ibn Waqid is not strong; people bore his hadith and narrated from him; those before and after him are trustworthy."
"4136","4131 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Abi al-Shatha, he said: Shayban ibn Qays narrated to me, he said: Shabib ibn Nuaym al-Kalai narrated to me, from Yazid ibn al-Nimran, that Abu al-Darda narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever takes land of the jizyah land and places it upon his neck has turned his back on Islam."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Umarah ibn Abi al-Shatha, Shayban ibn Qays, Shabib ibn Nuaym al-Kalai, and Yazid ibn Nimran are not known for transmission. We wrote this hadith despite its defect only because we did not preserve it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle, so we wrote it and clarified its defect."
"4137","4132 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad al-Nadr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Sad ibn Masud al-Tujibi, he informed him that he heard Abdullah ibn Jubayr inform that he heard Abu al-Darda inform that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I am the first for whom permission is given on the Day of Resurrection to raise his head. I shall raise my head and recognize my ummah on my right and my left."" It was said: How will you recognize them, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Radiant, marked (in light) from ablution, and their offspring a light before them."" We do not know a hadith to be narrated with its wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we wrote it. This Sad ibn Masud is not well-known; Abdullah ibn Jubayr we do not know for transmission. But since this hadith mentions an added wording not in another's hadith, we wrote it for that and clarified its defect."
"4138","4133 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Damrah ibn Habib, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Indeed Allah has given you in charity a third of your wealth at your death."" This hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes. The highest to narrate that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is Abu al-Darda, and we know no route for it from Abu al-Darda other than this route. Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam and Damrah are known for transmitting knowledge; hadith was borne from them."
"4139","4134 - Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qatii narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is not fitting for a habitual curser to be eminent with Allah, mighty and exalted."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle. Its chain is good. We do not know Zayd ibn Aslam to have narrated from Umm al-Darda other than this hadith."
"4140","4135 - Ibrahim ibn Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood praying, and we heard him say: ""I seek refuge in Allah from you."" Then he said: ""I curse you with Allah's curse,"" three times. Then he reached with his hand as if taking something. When he finished prayer they said: O Messenger of Allah, we heard you say in prayer something we never heard you say before, and saw you extend your hand. He said: ""Allah's enemy Iblis came with a brand of fire to place it in my face. I said: I seek refuge in Allah from you, and he did not retreat. Then I said to him and he did not retreat. Then I said to him: May Allah curse you with the perfect curse of Allah, and he did not retreat. Then I said it and he did not retreat. Then I said it and he did not retreat. Then I intended to seize him; had it not been for the supplication of our brother Sulayman, he would have become tied up, the boys of Medina playing with him."" This hadith is of good chain. We do not know a hadith like its wording to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Similar meaning and part of its wording has been narrated from more than one Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). In this is added speech not in the rest of the hadiths, so we wrote it for the addition therein and the goodness of its chain."
"4141","4136 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Shabbawayh narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Idris al-Sakuni, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: My close friend enjoined upon me three: fasting three days of each month, not to sleep except upon witr, and the forenoon prayer in travel and residence. This hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes, so we mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Darda for his eminence and the goodness of its chain - unless another adds speech, in which case it is written for the addition."
"4142","4137 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam ibn Subayh narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding {Every day He is in a matter}: Among His matters is to forgive a sin, relieve a distress, answer a caller, raise a people and lower others. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah has concluded for every servant his term, his provision, his resting place, and his trace."" This hadith has been narrated from Abu al-Darda by various routes, and this is among the best chains narrated from him. We mentioned elsewhere from Abu al-Darda {Every day He is in a matter}; in this is an addition in explanation, and in it the addition: Allah has concluded for every servant from five - and this is not in the hadith we wrote."
"4143","4138 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr narrate from his father, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he passed by a woman near term upon a tent and said: ""Perhaps he intends to approach her."" They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""I had intended to curse him with a curse that would enter the grave with him: how does he bequeath to him when it is not lawful for him? How does he use him as a servant when it is not lawful for him?"""
"4144","4139 - Ibrahim ibn Abdullah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir informed us, he said: Zayd ibn Artah narrated to me, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""You are provided and aided only by your weak ones; seek out your weak for me."" This hadith's chain is good. We do not preserve with its wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording but other than this wording, so we mentioned each hadith with its wording in its place."
"4145","4140 - Ubaydullah ibn Yaish narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Maysarah al-Bakri narrated to us, from Anas, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had no flour sifted for him, and he had but one shirt. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from any Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Abu al-Darda with this chain. Said ibn Maysarah was narrated from by Yunus with hadiths not corroborated; the scholars bore them despite their weakness."
"4146","4141 - Hisham narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Sumay narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Aftas narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we mentioned poverty and feared it. He said: ""Is it poverty you fear?! By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the world will surely be poured upon you until it cannot be lifted. By Allah, I shall surely leave you upon the like of the white plain, whose night and day are alike."" Abu al-Darda said: Allah and His Messenger spoke true; he left us, by Allah, upon the like of the white plain, whose night and day are alike. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Its chain is good."
"4147","4142 - Ibrahim ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Shaddad narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Salim al-Qaddah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ubaydullah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The merit of prayer in the Sacred Mosque over others is a hundred thousand prayers; in my mosque a thousand prayers; and in the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis five hundred prayers."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Its chain is good."
"4148","4143 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Utbah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Maysarah, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Allah, blessed and exalted, created Adam when He created him; He struck his right shoulder and brought forth a white progeny as if they were motes, and struck his left shoulder and brought forth a black progeny as if they were char. He said to those in his right: To Paradise, and I do not care; and said to those in his left: To the Fire, and I do not care."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Its chain is good."
"4149","4144 - Umar ibn al-Khattab al-Sijistani narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Utbah narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Maysarah narrated to us, from Abu Idris, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""You will surely raise armies: an army in al-Sham, Egypt, Iraq, and Yemen."" They said: Choose for us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""Upon you is al-Sham."" They said: We are herdsmen and cannot bear al-Sham. He said: ""Whoever cannot bear al-Sham, let him join his Yemen, for Allah has guaranteed me al-Sham."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better than this hadith of Abu al-Darda. Similar wording has been narrated from others besides Abu al-Darda, and we mentioned the hadith of Abu al-Darda for his eminence and the goodness of its chain."
"4150","4145 - Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us. 4145 (m) - And Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated it to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Raja ibn Haywah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Jamil, from Kathir ibn Qays, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The scholars are the successors of the prophets. The prophets did not bequeath dinar or dirham; they bequeathed knowledge."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle. Its chain is fair. Dawud ibn Jamil and Kathir ibn Qays we do not know to be known in other than this hadith."
"4151","4146 - Abdullah ibn Said al-Kindi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Maysarah, from Abu Idris, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Purifications are four: trimming the mustache, shaving the pubic hair, clipping the nails, and the siwak."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle. Muawiyah ibn Yahya - we have already mentioned him, for he is not strong; the scholars narrated from him and bore his hadith."
"4152","4147 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, he said: Rashid ibn Muhammad al-Himmani narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4153","4148 - And Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Mulhami narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Rashid al-Himmani narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me: ""Not to associate anything with Allah even if burned; not to abandon an obligatory prayer deliberately - whoever abandons it deliberately has disbelieved; and not to drink wine, for it is the key to all evil."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Rashid Abu Muhammad is Basran, unobjectionable; more than one narrated from him. Shahr ibn Hawshab was narrated from by people; they spoke about him and bore his hadith."
"4154","4149 - Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Ziyad al-Musaghghar, from al-Hasan, from al-Miqdam, he said: Ubadah, Abu al-Darda, and al-Harith ibn Muawiyah sat together. Abu al-Darda said: Which of you remembers when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us toward a camel of the spoils? When he finished, he took a tuft from the camel and said: ""Nothing is lawful for me of what Allah has bestowed upon you, not even the like of this, except the fifth - and the fifth is returned among you."" This hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording by various routes with other than this wording but like it in meaning. We mention each hadith with its wording in its place, Allah willing. Al-Miqdam al-Ruhawi we do not know anyone to have narrated from except al-Hasan with this hadith; Ziyad al-Musaghghar we do not know anyone to have narrated from except Israil."
"4155","4150 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Damrah ibn Habib, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Indeed Allah loves every sorrowful heart."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this chain."
"4156","4151 - Salamah ibn Shabib and al-Fadl ibn Abi Talib narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Abu Umar al-Sini, from Umm al-Darda, she said: A guest stayed with Abu al-Darda. He said: Are you staying, that we release (animals to graze), or departing, that we fodder? Then Abu al-Darda said: Some poor people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, the wealthy have taken the world and the Hereafter: they pray as we pray, fast as we fast, strive and give charity while we give none. He said: ""O Abu al-Darda, shall I not guide you to a matter: when you do it you catch those before you and none after you catches you except one who says the like of your saying? Glorify Allah in each prayer thirty-three times, praise thirty-three times, and magnify thirty-four times."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes. We do not know anyone to have perfected and connected it except Yazid ibn Harun from Sharik."
"4157","4152 - Nasr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Mukhtar, from Muhammad ibn Wasi, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Let mosques be your seat, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Indeed Allah has guaranteed for whoever's house is the mosques: safety and passage over the Sirat on the Day of Resurrection."" This hadith - similar wording was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than its wording. We do not know this wording to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except with this chain from this angle. It is of good chain."
"4158","4153 - Humayd ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Whoever comes to his bed intending to rise and pray at night, then his eyes overcome him until morning, what he intended is written for him, and his sleep is charity."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way except from this angle with this chain. It is of good chain, from the strange hadith of al-Amash, connected in chain."
"4159","Musnad of Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him). Hadith of Thawban. 4154 - Bishr ibn Muadh Abu Sahl al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that Allah's Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever leaves a hoard after him, it will be represented on the Day of Resurrection as a bald serpent with two spots, following him, saying: Woe to you, what are you? It will say: I am your hoard which you hoarded. He said: It will keep on until it seizes his hand, then follows the rest of his body - or: in the rest of his body."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with other than this wording. We know no route for Thawban other than this route; its chain is good."
"4160","4155 - Bishr ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever asks for charity while rich in it, it will be a blemish on his face on the Day of Resurrection."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording by an established route from him other than this angle, nor do we know for it a route from Thawban other than this route."
"4161","4156 - And Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Bukayr ibn Abi al-Samit narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast."""
"4162","4157 - And al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, he said: Zufar ibn Hubayrah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban narrated to us, from his father, from Makhul, from Abu Asma, from Thawban, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated this hadith from Abu Qilabah from Abu Asma from Thawban - so said Yahya ibn Abi Kathir; the memorizers narrated it from Yahya. Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated it from Abu Qilabah from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani from Shaddad ibn Aws; Khalid is a memorizer. Ayyub narrated it from Abu Qilabah from one who narrated to him from Shaddad. Asim al-Ahwal and Abu Ghifar narrated it from Abu Qilabah from Abu al-Ashath from Abu Asma from Shaddad. The hadith of Khalid is in my view the best of them. We do not know anyone to have narrated the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit from his father from Makhul from Abu Asma but Zufar ibn Hubayrah."
"4163","4158 - And Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated it to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Thawban, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its like. These chains from Thawban regarding ""The cupper and the cupped have broken fast"" are good chains. As for Qatadah from Shahr, we do not know anyone to have narrated it from Qatadah but Said ibn Abi Arubah. As for Qatadah from Salim, we do not know anyone to have narrated it from Qatadah but Bukayr ibn Abi al-Samit, a Basran elder, unobjectionable - except that this hadith is not preserved from Qatadah from Salim. I think Bukayr erred therein when he said: from Salim. It was narrated from al-Layth ibn Sad from Qatadah from al-Hasan from Thawban, and he erred therein; for the trustworthy narrate it from al-Hasan from Abu Hurayrah, and al-Layth erred therein. Ayyub ibn Abi Sukayn al-Wasiti narrated it from Qatadah from Shahr ibn Hawshab from Bilal, and erred therein - also called Ibn Miskin; Ayyub's kunyah is Abu al-Ala. The hadith is in my view most like the hadith of Ibn Abi Arubah, for he is more retentive than others."
"4164","4159 - Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim, from Madan, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever's soul leaves his body while he is free of three: arrogance, debt, and misappropriation - Paradise is due for him; or he said: for him is Paradise."
"4165","4159 (m) - He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever prays over a funeral has a qirat; whoever waits for it until it is buried has two qirats, the smaller of them like Uhud."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Its chain is good, from Thawban."
"4166","4160 - Abu Kamil narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Layth, from Abu Zuraah, from Abu Idris, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the briber, the bribed, and the intermediary. This hadith - his saying ""and the intermediary"" - we do not know to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any way except from this angle, so we wrote it. We clarified that this hadith is from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym from Abu Zuraah from Abu Idris. Dhawwad ibn Ulbah inserted between Abu Zuraah and him a man from Layth, mentioning him from Abu al-Khattab. Abu al-Khattab is not well-known, though Layth narrated from him other hadiths; his hadith is written only when what is narrated is not preserved except from him."
"4167","4161 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Muzahim ibn Dhawwad ibn Ulbah narrated to us, from his father, from Layth, from a companion of his called Abu al-Khattab, from Abu Zuraah, from Abu Idris, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Women who seek divorce (without cause) are the hypocrites."" This hadith has been narrated from Abu Hurayrah - al-Hasan narrated it from him, though al-Hasan did not hear from Abu Hurayrah. Thawban narrated it by this route. We clarified the defect of Layth and Abu al-Khattab, and sufficed with the hadith of Thawban herein to the exclusion of others."
"4168","4162 - Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Rushdayn ibn Sad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum, from Hubayrah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Asma, from Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - I think he raised it - he said: ""Lying is recorded, except what benefits a Muslim thereby or harm is averted from him thereby."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Rushdayn ibn Sad was not retentive; Ibn al-Mubarak and those below him narrated from him and bore his hadith. Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad likewise - his hadith does not indicate he was retentive, for in his hadith are denounced reports; he was among the wise, and people narrated from him. Neither Rushdayn nor Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad can be a proof in a hadith when alone with it, nor either of them when alone with a hadith."
"4169","4163 - Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri informed us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Three will fight at this treasure of yours, each of them the son of a caliph; then it will reach none of them. Then black banners will come from the east and kill you with a killing no people were killed with."" Then he mentioned something: ""When you see him, pledge to him even crawling on snow, for he is Allah's caliph, al-Mahdi."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from another angle with this wording; and this wording we know only in this hadith. Though most of this hadith's meaning has been narrated, we chose this hadith for its soundness and Thawban's eminence. Its chain is sound."
"4170","4164 - And Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Rabiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Abu Uthman, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Emigration will not cease so long as the disbelievers are fought."" This hadith has been narrated from more than one with similar wording. We mentioned the hadith of Thawban to the exclusion of others, unless one adds in its meaning speech, in which case it is written for the addition therein."
"4171","4165 - And Ibrahim narrated to us with his chain from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Whoever innovates an innovation, shelters an innovator, claims (lineage) to other than his father, or allies with other than his masters - upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; neither exchange nor compensation is accepted from him."" And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""How will you be among people whose covenants and trusts are corrupted and who become dregs?"" And he interlaced his fingers. They said: What shall we do, O Messenger of Allah? He said: ""Be patient; mix with people in character but oppose them in deeds."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes. Part of its wording we do not preserve from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this chain, so we wrote the hadith in full for its addition. What we found of the hadith with one more trustworthy than the narrator of this hadith, we mentioned from the more trustworthy, and affirmed what we preserved only with this chain in its place."
"4172","4166 - al-Qasim ibn Hashim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn al-Sakan al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Jubayr informed me, he said: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to me, he said: Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked to pray after midday until the sun rose high. Aishah said: O Messenger of Allah, I see you like prayer at this hour. He said: ""In it the doors of heaven are opened, and Allah, blessed and exalted, looks with mercy upon His creation. It is a prayer Adam, Nuh, Ibrahim, Musa, and Isa used to observe."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from Thawban with this chain. Utbah ibn al-Sakan narrated from al-Awzai hadiths not corroborated; Salih ibn Jubayr we do not know anyone to have narrated from but al-Awzai."
"4173","4167 - Ibrahim ibn Said and Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated to us, from al-Abbas ibn Salim, from Abu Sallam, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""My Hawd (Pool) is from Aden to Amman of al-Balqa. Its water is sweeter than honey, more fragrant than musk, whiter than milk. Its vessels are more numerous than the stars of heaven. Whoever drinks a draught from it will never thirst thereafter."" It was said: O Messenger of Allah, who are the first people to come to you - or to it? He said: ""The emigrants, disheveled in head, soiled in clothes, who do not marry the pampered nor are doors opened for them."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in any connected way with this wording except from this angle from Thawban. Its chain is good: Muhammad ibn Muhajir is trustworthy; al-Abbas ibn Salim is unobjectionable; Abu Sallam is well-known."
"4174","4168 - And Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Sallam, that he heard Abu Sallam, he said: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to me that Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him, he said: I was standing with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a rabbi of the Jews came and said: Peace be upon you, O Muhammad. I pushed him a push that nearly felled him. He said: Why did you push me? I said: Why do you not say: O Messenger of Allah? The Jew said: We call him by the name his family named him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""My name is Muhammad, with which my family named me."" The Jew said: I came to ask you about something. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: ""Will it benefit you if I tell you?"" He said: I shall hear with my ears. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) tapped with a stick he had and said: ""Ask."" The Jew said: Where will people be when the earth is changed to other than earth, and the heavens? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""They are in darkness below the Bridge."" He said: Who are the first people to cross? He said: ""The poor emigrants."" The Jew said: What is their welcoming gift? He said: ""Slaughtered for them is the whale of Paradise, which used to eat from its edges."" He said: What is their drink upon it? He said: ""From a spring therein called Salsabil."" He said: You spoke true. This hadith - similar wording has been narrated; but with these phrases and this length, we do not know anyone to have narrated it but Thawban, nor do we know for it a route from Thawban except this route. Its route is good, for Muawiyah ibn Salih was narrated from by scholars; likewise Zayd ibn Sallam and Abu Sallam; Abu Asma is a well-known man narrated from by people."
"4175","4169 - Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated to us, he said: Rayhan ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that Allah's Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) magnified the matter of begging. He said: When the Day of Resurrection comes, the people of Jahiliyyah will come carrying their idols on their backs. Their Lord, blessed and exalted, will ask them, and they will say: Our Lord, You sent no messenger to us, nor did any command of Yours reach us; had You sent a messenger to us we would have been Your most obedient servants. Their Lord will say to them: If I command you with a command, will you obey Me? They will say: Yes. So He will command them to head for Hell and enter it. They will set off until, when they near it, they find for it rage and roaring, and return to their Lord saying: Our Lord, take us out of it - or: protect us from it. He will say to them: Did you not claim that if I commanded you with a command you would obey Me? He will take their covenants upon that and say: Head for it and enter it. They will set off until, when they see it, they fear and return, saying: Our Lord, we feared it and cannot enter it. He will say: Enter it abased. Allah's Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Had they entered it the first time, it would have been cool and safe upon them."""
"4176","4170 - Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban with its like. This hadith from Thawban we preserve only from this route we mentioned. We do not know anyone to have narrated it from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from Abu Asma from Thawban but Abbad ibn Mansur, nor from Abbad but Rayhan ibn Said. We do not know anyone to have narrated the hadith of Aban but Ishaq ibn Idris. It is strange from Ayyub and from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir. This hadith's text from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not known except from this angle."
"4177","4171 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Rabiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Ashath, from Abu Uthman, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is a duty upon every Muslim: the siwak, bathing on Friday, and touching of his family's perfume if he has any."""
"4178","4172 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, relative of Ahmad ibn Mani, narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Abu Yahya, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us after Fajr prayer and said: My Lord came to me tonight in the best form and said: O Muhammad, do you know in what the Highest Assembly disputes? He said: I said: No. He said: Then he mentioned something. He said: There appeared to me what is between heaven and earth. He said: I said: Yes, my Lord - they dispute over expiations and ranks. As for ranks: feeding food, spreading peace, and standing at night while people sleep. As for expiations: walking on feet to congregations, performing ablution fully in disliked (cold) times, and sitting in mosques after prayers. Then He said: O Muhammad, say and you shall be heard; ask and you shall be given. He said: I said: Teach me. He said: Say: O Allah, I ask You for good deeds, abandoning evils, love of the poor; to forgive me and have mercy on me; and if You intend trial for a people, take me to You unforsook. O Allah, I ask You for Your love, the love of whoever loves You, and a love that conveys to me Your love. This hadith has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with similar wording by various routes, so we mentioned the hadith of Thawban to the exclusion of others, for in the other hadiths there is confusion. We sufficed with this hadith, and in it is also an addition not in the hadith of Muadh ibn Jabal, nor in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, nor in the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Aish."
"4179","4173 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Salih, from Utbah Abu Umayyah al-Dimashqi, from Abu Sallam, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution and wipe over the khuffs and the head-cover."
"4180","4174 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Dawud, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while on a journey of his: ""We shall travel by night tonight, Allah willing, so let no weak-riding one come out with us."" A man came on a small she-camel of his and was thrown; his thigh was crushed and he died. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered prayer over him and announced: ""Paradise is not lawful for a disobedient one."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from this angle with this wording. Similar wording has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes, but the wording we preserve only from Thawban by this route. Rashid ibn Dawud is unobjectionable, so his hadith was borne; al-Haytham ibn Humayd is well-known, unobjectionable."
"4181","4175 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Rayhan narrated to us, from Abbad, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""And all that they are promised is in a hundred years."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated with this wording except from Thawban alone. More than one narrated it from Abu Qilabah, among them Qatadah and Ayyub - except Mamar, who erred therein, saying: from Abu Qilabah from Abu Asma from Shaddad ibn Aws. The correct is: from Thawban; the error is from Mamar."
"4182","4176 - And Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Sallam, that he heard Abu Sallam, he said: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to me that Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him, he said: I was standing with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a rabbi of the Jews came and said: I ask you about something which none on earth knows but a prophet. He said: ""Will it benefit you if I tell you?"" He said: I shall hear with my ears. He said: I came to ask you about the child. He said: ""As for the fluid of the man and the fluid of the woman: when they join, if the man's fluid overcomes the woman's fluid, they produce a male by Allah's permission; if the woman's fluid overcomes the man's fluid, they produce a female by Allah's permission."" The Jew said: You spoke true. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""This one asked me about it, and I had no knowledge of any of it until Allah informed me of it."" This hadith with this wording we preserve only from Thawban with this chain. Similar wording has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes, but the wording Thawban narrated, none of the hadith scholars who reached us corroborated him in it."
"4183","4177 - Muhammad ibn Zanjawayh narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Shaddad Abu Ammar narrated to me, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he finished his prayer, sought Allah's forgiveness three times, then said: ""O Allah, You are Peace, and from You is peace; blessed are You, Possessor of majesty and honor."" We do not know anyone to have said therein ""he sought Allah's forgiveness three times"" before saying ""O Allah, You are Peace"" except in this hadith from Thawban. Its chain is good: Shaddad Abu Ammar is well-known; the rest of the chain are known, needing no commendation."
"4184","4178 - Muhammad ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan and Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari, from Abu Asma al-Rahabi, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever fasts Ramadan and follows it with six of Shawwal is as one who fasted the year."""
"4185","4179 - Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari narrated to us, from Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani, from Abu Asma al-Rahabi, from Thawban, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its like. This hadith has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes - Abu Hurayrah, Jabir, Abu Ayyub and others narrated it - so we mentioned the hadith of Thawban in this place, unless another adds speech, in which case it is written for the addition."
"4186","4180 - Ahmad ibn Amr ibn Hanan narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Musa, from Yazid ibn Shurayh, from Abu Yahya the muezzin, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is not lawful for a Muslim to look into a house until he seeks permission; if he does, he has entered. He shall not lead a people and single himself out with a supplication to their exclusion; if he does, he has betrayed them. He shall not pray while withholding (urine/stool) until he relieves himself."" This hadith - similar wording was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by various routes with other than this wording, and in it is an addition. We do not know it to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except from Thawban with this chain. Its chain is good. Baqiyyah said: Shubah used to ask me for this hadith, so I narrated it to him, and he said: You have satisfied me, O Abu Muhammad - how did Habib ibn Salih narrate to you from Yazid ibn Shurayh? As if Shubah deemed this hadith good and had Baqiyyah repeat it."
"4187","4181 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Nafi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Rabiah, from Abu al-Ashath, from Abu Uthman, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Three are attached to the Throne: kinship says: O Allah, I am by You, so let me not be severed; trust says: O Allah, I am by You, so let me not be betrayed; blessing says: O Allah, I am by You, so let me not be denied."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated with this wording except from Thawban with this chain. Part of its wording has been narrated with other than its wording by various routes, so we mentioned this hadith of Thawban because it combined two aspects: differing wording and an addition in hadith. It is not preserved except from this angle. We have already mentioned Yazid ibn Rabiah and this Abu Uthman, so we sufficed without repeating them."
"4188","4182 - al-Qasim ibn Hashim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Utbah ibn al-Sakan al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: Ubadah ibn Nusayy and Hubayrah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me; they heard Abu Asma say: Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was fasting outside Ramadan when vomiting - I think - befell him; he performed ablution then broke fast. I said: O Messenger of Allah, were you not fasting? He said: ""Yes, but I vomited and broke fast."" Next day I heard him say: ""This day is in place of my breaking fast yesterday."" This hadith has been narrated from Thawban and others besides Thawban with other than this wording. In this hadith is an addition not in the hadith of any who narrated similar wording, namely ""This day is in place of my breaking fast yesterday."" This is preserved only from this angle. We do not know anyone to have narrated it from al-Awzai with this wording but Utbah ibn al-Sakan. We do not know Ubadah ibn Nusayy to have narrated from Abu Asma other than this hadith. We have already mentioned Utbah in other than this hadith."
"4189","4183 - Ibrahim ibn Mustamirr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: ""O Allah, I ask You for good things, abandoning evils, love of the poor; to turn to me in forgiveness; and if You intend trial for Your servants, to take me to You unforsook."" This hadith has been narrated from Thawban by another route. We do not know anyone to have narrated this hadith - Qatadah from Abu Qilabah from Abu Asma from Thawban - but Said ibn Bashir. Hisham, author of al-Dastuwai, said: from Qatadah from Abu Qilabah from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Lajlaj from Ibn Abbas. We mentioned the hadith of Qatadah only to show its variance from Qatadah; otherwise we had mentioned similar wording from Thawban."
"4190","4184 - Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""One who visits the sick is in the orchard of Paradise until he returns."""
"4191","4185 - Ahmad ibn Malik narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its like. This hadith from Thawban we do not know to be narrated from another besides Thawban with this wording from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Its chain is good. We do not know anyone to have narrated the hadith of Qatadah but Said ibn Bashir. The scholars narrated from Said ibn Bashir and bore his hadith, though in his hadiths are reports none but he corroborated."
"4192","4186 - al-Abbas ibn Abdullah al-Baksa'i narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Ubayd al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: (Abdullah ibn al-Ala from al-Ala ibn Zabr) narrated to us, from Abu Sallam, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Bakh, bakh, for five - how heavy they are in the Scale: There is no god but Allah; Glory be to Allah; Praise be to Allah; Allah is Greatest; and a righteous child who dies to a Muslim man and he counts him (for reward)."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle from Thawban. Its chain is good: Zayd ibn Yahya is known, unobjectionable; Abdullah ibn al-Ala ibn Zabr and his father are well-known; Abu Sallam is well-known, whom we mentioned."
"4193","4187 - Muhammad ibn Hassan al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Rayhan ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abbad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""No man of Paradise plucks its fruit but its like is restored in its place."""
"4194","4188 - Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Aban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its like. This hadith from Thawban we do not know to be narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by a connected route with better than this chain. We do not know anyone to have narrated the hadith of Ayyub but Abbad ibn Mansur, nor from Abbad but Rayhan. We do not know anyone to have narrated the hadith of Yahya ibn Abi Kathir but Ishaq ibn Idris from Aban."
"4195","4189 - Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Rayhan narrated to us, from Abbad, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked - he said: The disbeliever's molar is like Uhud, and the thickness of his skin forty cubits by the Mighty One's cubit. We do not know anyone to have narrated it from Ayyub but Abbad ibn Mansur, nor from Abbad but Rayhan ibn Said. The scholars narrated from Rayhan, such as Ali ibn al-Madini, Ibn Ararah, Ibrahim ibn Said and others."
"4196","4190 - al-Abbas ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us; and Ahmad ibn Malik al-Qushayri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Habib narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Madan, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""My Hawd (Pool) - I drive people from it for my household. I shall surely strike them with this staff of mine until it overflows."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked and said: ""Its width from my station to Amman; whiter than milk, sweeter than honey. Two spouts pour into it, extending to it from Paradise: one of silver and the other of gold."" This hadith - similar wording has been narrated with other than its wording in the story of the Hawd from Thawban, so we mentioned this hadith for its addition and the goodness of its chain. We mentioned Thawban's other hadith because in it is a wording not in this; we mentioned each separately, disliking to mention the addition alone lest one without knowledge deny it."
"4197","4191 - al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Mamar narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) escorted a funeral, a man brought him a mount to ride, and he refused. When the bier was lowered, another man brought him a mount and he rode it. The first met him and said: O Messenger of Allah, I offered you my mount to ride and you refused, while so-and-so offered you his mount and you rode it. He said: ""You offered me your mount while the angels escorted the funeral, and I would not ride while the angels walked. Had you offered it after it was buried, I would have ridden it."" We do not know this hadith to be narrated with this wording except from Thawban with this chain. It is of good chain. We do not know its wording to have come from anyone but him with a connected chain. Amir ibn Yasaf narrated it from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir mursal, not saying from Abu Salamah nor Thawban; Mamar is firmer than Amir ibn Yasaf."
"4198","4192 - Bishr ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Amir ibn Yasaf narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its like. He did not support it."
"4199","4193 - Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr, from his father, from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he said: This journey is exertion and burden; when one of you prays witr, let him bow two rakahs - if he wakes, well, otherwise they are his."
"4200","Musnad of al-Irbad ibn Sariyah (may Allah be pleased with him). al-Irbad ibn Sariyah. 4194 - al-Hasan ibn Arafah narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Buhayr ibn Sad narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Madan, from Abu Bilal, from al-Irbad ibn Sariyah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The martyrs and those who die on their beds will dispute before our Lord over those who die in plague. The martyrs say: Our brothers! The bedridden deceased say: Our brothers! They died on their beds as we died. Allah, blessed and exalted, will say: Look at their wounds: if their wounds resemble the wounds of the slain, they are with them and of them."" And their wounds resembled their wounds. We do not know this hadith to be narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this wording except from this angle with this chain. Its chain is good."
